{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","hash":"712f72a16231920543ab960d964d7c203f40b5016cd1940a753972f24bc47c47","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01873Ng8eQXjGjRxPXuLVJPU","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Plan updated: step 4 of 9 is in progress."}]},{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EbpBy13tiLEMtfQRmNKgma","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t=== 8_1883-04-21_p1_sn86076999_00415627385_1883042101_0867.json\n2\tMorning appeal. [volume] (Carson City, Nev.) 1877-1906 · 1883-04-21\n3\tcrops: ['/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/data/crops/9b46a1e5e158fc5c90fb2382.jpg', '/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/data/crops/6edd0fb35f212cf0fde8edd4.jpg', '/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/data/crops/983f1f80b5ddc8a749484139.jpg']\n4\theadline: A GHOST SAVES HIS CHAR.\n5\t\n6\t\n7\tACTER\n8\t\n9\tA spiritual Conn unicatlon That\n10\t\n11\t\n12\tDid some Good.\n13\t---\n14\tDid Some Good.\n15\t\n16\t\n17\tA gentleman employed at ;\n18\t\n19\t\n20\tGovernment institution in thi.\n21\t\n22\t\n23\tcity, and a man who has nc\n24\t\n25\t\n26\tleaning whatever toward spirit\n27\t\n28\t\n29\tualism, relates the following in\n30\t\n31\t\n32\tcident\n33\t\n34\t\n35\tIn 1853 his father, while\n36\t\n37\t\n38\ttreasurer of a local railroad in\n39\t\n40\t\n41\tMassachusetts,, died. After his\n42\t\n43\t\n44\tdemise the directors of the COM\n45\t\n46\t\n47\tpany found a deficiency in the\n48\t\n49\t\n50\taccounts Of the deceased\n51\t\n52\t\n53\tamounting to eighteen hundred\n54\t\n55\t\n56\tand fifty odd dollars. The fact\n57\t\n58\t\n59\twas communicated to the fam\n60\tlily of the late treasurer and the\n61\tapparent detalcation caused\n62\t\n63\t\n64\tthem much grief and shame.\n65\tIn 1861 a noted Boston medium\n66\t\n67\t\n68\tnamed Mansfield, visited San\n69\tFrancisco, creating great ex-\n70\tcitement by reason of his Won\n71\tderful performances and he was\n72\tcalled on by some Of the best\n73\tpeople of that city. One day\n74\tthe gentleman above referred to,\n75\taccompanied by the late Rev.\n76\tThomas Starr King, visited\n77\tMansfield purely out of curiosity.\n78\tThe former was requested by\n79\tthe medium to write upon a\n80\tslip of paper the name of any\n81\tone in the spirit world with\n82\twhom he desired to communi-\n83\tcate. He wrote the name of his\n84\tfather, and without exhibiting\n85\til to Mansfield placed it into a\n86\tsmall tin tube which the latter\n87\thanded him and sealed it in\n88\tsuch a manner with a private\n89\tseal that any tampering could\n90\tbe easily detected. Mansfield\n91\trequested that the tube and the\n92\tcontents be left in his possession\n93\tfor twenty-four hours, after\n94\twhich the gentlemen might call\n95\tfor a letter from the spirit world.\n96\tOn the following day the gen\n97\ttleman in question again called\n98\ton the medium and substan-\n99\titaly received this communi.\n100\tcation from his deceased father\n101\t\n102\t\n103\tHe informed his son that he\n104\twas cognizant of the grief which\n105\tlis apparent defalcation had |\n106\tcaused the family, but that he |\n107\twas entirely innocent of any !\n108\tfurther stating that /\n109\tf a certain book would be ex- |\n110\t,mined, which could be found |\n111\tn a stated spot, and opened at /\n112\t/ given page, it would be found |\n113\that the date of the year had\n114\tenormously been placed in the\n115\tlollar column and added up as\n116\tash, which would account for\n117\the apparent deficiency in his\n118\tAlthough reposing\n119\ttut little faith in the matter,\n120\the son wrote East about it, and !\n121\twhen he received a reply it was\n122\t@ the purport that the book\n123\tmentioned was in the indicated\n124\tlace, and on the stated page\n125\tt was found thai 1858, the\n126\tof the supposed defal-\n127\talton, had been added to the\n128\tash account by mistake.\n129\t\n130\t=== 1_1893-06-24_pna_sn94052989_00175040250_1893062401_0653.json\n131\tThe morning call. [volume] (San Francisco [Calif.]) 1878-1895 · 1893-06-24\n132\tcrops: ['/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/data/crops/7011f58d3ce855c0c49db39f.jpg', '/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/data/crops/236d11faf74ed962becc363e.jpg', '/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/data/crops/fd74bee3529815e438295e8b.jpg', '/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/data/crops/f0abbc8e2cad9ca6cc338043.jpg', '/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/data/crops/87ace9ab09c4c5fd48c4f4ae.jpg']\n133\theadline: WITHOUT ANY EXCUSE\n134\t\n135\tForging Documents to. Inculpate\n136\t\n137\t\n138\tthe Statesmen of France.\n139\t\n140\tThe Ghost of Panama Will Not Down,\n141\tThough 1t Should Be at\n142\t\n143\t\n144\tRest.\n145\t\n146\tSpecial to THE MORNING CALL\n147\t---\n148\tLONDON, June 23.-The matter of the\n149\tdocuments that the Paris Boulangist organ.\n150\tCocarde. alleged have been stolen from\n151\tthe British Embassy in Paris, came up in\n152\tthe Commons to-day. Sir Edward Grey,\n153\tParliamentary Secretary Of the Foreign\n154\tOffice. replied that no documents had been\n155\tstolen from the British embassy. He\n156\tadded that the documents NJ. Millevoye\n157\tread were gross torgeries, and be did not\n158\tknow from where or by whom the for\n159\tgeries had been supplied.\n160\t\n161\t\n162\tPARIS, June ON. Ducret, editor of\n163\tCocarde, which published the story con-\n164\tcorning the documents stolen from the\n165\tBritish embassy, and NJ. Norton. sup\n166\tposed to be the forger of the same d0cu-\n167\tments, were arrested to-day.\n168\t\n169\t\n170\tErnest Vale presented in the Deputies\n171\tto-day the report of the commission of\n172\tinquiry into the Panama scandals. The\n173\treport exculpates from all blame M. de\n174\tFreycinei. ex-Mlnister of War, and M.\n175\tFloquet, ex-Presldent of the Chamber of\n176\tDeputies. 1t also pronouuces false in\n177\tevery particular the story that 104 Depu-\n178\tties were bought with Panama money.\n179\t\n180\t=== 5_1908-10-24_p2_sn90050307_00414212888_1908102401_0383.json\n181\tThe Prescott daily news. · 1908-10-24\n182\tcrops: None\n183\theadline: BOHEMIAN FOR NOVEMBER.\n184\t---\n185\tAn unusual number of short stories are printed in The Bo- hemian Magazine for November. They are all good, but conspic- uous among them in gripping in terest and dramatic force is one entitled \"An Anarchist o the Big Timber\" by Henry Oyen-a story that relates the lone fight of backwoodsman, who believed he was being outraged in his rights, against a powerful lum- Der corporation in Chicago An other story with decidedly humorous vein is that by Pro- ter Emerson ( Browne, entitled, From Them That Have Vent\" this tells how ''Corrigan\" put his faith in mediums and of what befell. Other articles, are ''Twen- ty Thousand Miles with Bem- hardt.\" \"The Ghost of Science Latest methods of Craft\" etc.\n186\t\n187\t The portfolio of actresses of the present theatrical season which is printed in color in The Bohemian this month is especial ly attractive. The humorous de- partment as usual is full of new and witty things and is the best department of it's kind we have yet seen\n188\t\n189\t=== 44_1868-10-26_pna_sn84024738_00271741959_1868102601_0410.json\n190\tThe daily dispatch. [volume] (Richmond [Va.]) 1850-1884 · 1868-10-26\n191\tcrops: None\n192\theadline: LOCAL MATTERS.\n193\t---\n194\t\n195\t\n196\t\n197\tINTERNAL REVENUE TAx.-The follow.\n198\ting will show the amount of taxes assessed\n199\ton tobacco which has been shipped, in\n200\tbond, from Richmond for each month since\n201\tthe lst Of January, 1863, January, $751;\n202\tO17.23; February, s67,062.90;= March\n203\ts91, ,3S65O; April. $l78,5S7.95;, May.\n204\ts349 ,741. go; June, s3s2,5O1; July, 1884,\n205\t1888; August, 1921 ,O96.36 September,\n206\tFsl59, ,9s6.72-making a total Of $685.\n207\t5l2.79. .... Il. - s.1-55, est ~\"\n208\tDuring the month of September there\n209\twas collected at the Office of the internal\n210\trevenue in this city 73,9l3.7S. OF this\n211\tamount there was collected on distilled\n212\tspirits, at fifty cents per gallon, $40, ,49O.66\n213\ton manufactured tobacco, at thirty-twc\n214\tcents tax, $S,925.46; and on smoking to-\n215\tbacco, at sixteen cents tax, 81, ,l60.58. ana\n216\t\n217\t=== 3_1905-06-09_p1_sn86063592_00271764935_1905060901_0561.json\n218\tLas Vegas daily optic. [volume] (Las Vegas, N.M.) 1880-1908 · 1905-06-09\n219\tcrops: None\n220\theadline: The Whole Trail at Portland For $500\n221\t---\n222\tStrangely CARRIED people are seen on\n223\tall sides. now a diminutive donkey\n224\tbelabored by a boy in flowing robe\n225\tdashes past, or a monstrous camel\n226\tloaded with human freight lmnbers\n227\tthrough the street. Ferocious looking\n228\t| warriors dance about in mock combat.\n229\tThe shrieking strains Of curious Oi-\n230\tentail instruments are waited from the\n231\ttheatre on the soft Oregon ozone.\n232\tFor twenty-five cents the visitor gains\n233\tentrance to this foreign show house\n234\tand spends a pleasant half hour view\n235\ting the antics of a strange people.\n236\t\n237\t\n238\tA wonderful show is located almost\n239\tOpposite the streets of Cairo, the in\n240\tliant i%ncubators. There is no gaiety\n241\tabout this concession. 1t represents\n242\tthe most astonishing achievement of\n243\tmodern science, the n%urturing OF the\n244\thuman being by artificial means. In\n245\tthe pleasing little building housing\n246\tthis attraction are several ingeniously\n247\tCONSTRUCTED i%ncubators containing real\n248\tlive infants. Visitors are allowed to\n249\tgaze at them through plate glass, and\n250\tapparently the tots are content to re-\n251\t\n252\t\n253\tmain in their snug homes. The ad-\n254\t\n255\t\n256\tmission to the infant %incubators is\n257\t\n258\t\n259\t25 cents.\n260\t\n261\t\n262\tAnd SO on down the Trail the visitor\n263\t\n264\t\n265\tmay meander. A great many of the\n266\t\n267\t\n268\tshows offer admissions as low as III\n269\t\n270\t\n271\tcents. The visitor finds foolish shows\n272\t\n273\t\n274\tand serious shows, or he may devote\n275\t\n276\t\n277\this time to instructive shows. No\n278\tmatter how hard he hits the Trail\n279\t\n280\t\n281\the cannot spend more than seven dol\n282\t\n283\t\n284\tlars, which will admit him to thirty\n285\t\n286\t\n287\tfive attractions, unless he chooses tc\n288\t\n289\t\n290\ttake in a show more than once. The\n291\t|entire list OF concessions, with theil\n292\t\n293\t\n294\tprice of admission, follows\n295\t\n296\t\n297\tGay Paree, Is cents; Concert hall\n298\t\n299\t\n300\t25c; Infant in%cubators, 25c; Cascade\n301\t\n302\t\n303\tgardens. csc; White Swan, 10c; Jap\n304\t\n305\t\n306\tanese village and theatre, Csc: Div\n307\tling Elks, Csc; New York ANNA\n308\tshow. csc; Haunted swing, 25c\n309\tStreets of Cairo, 15c; Theatre. 25c\n310\tDonkey ride 25c; Camel ride soc; car\n311\tnival OF Venice, soc; Land of Mid\n312\t: might Sun, CSC; Tellevue, csc: Daven\n313\tport farm, csc; Sistine Madonna, 10c\n314\t} Trip to Niagara Falls, CSC: Galveston\n315\t, Flood. csc; California Radium Exh'\n316\t\n317\t\n318\tbit. 10c: Burns Cottage. 10c; Shootin\n319\t: Gallery. 10c; Mirror Maze. 10c; Whit\n320\t, Slave or Glimpse of the Harem, 10c\n321\t/ Grand Siberian Railway, csc; an\n322\tRlne G0tto. 25c.\n323\t\n324\t=== 14_1907-10-12_p1_sn93061711_00280773712_1907101201_0295.json\n325\tThe Tucumcari news and Tucumcari times. [volume] (Tucumcari, N.M.) 1907-1921 · 1907-10-12\n326\tcrops: None\n327\theadline: Ghost Sale and Fish Pond\n328\t---\n329\tEverybody is invited to attend\n330\tthe box supper at Revuelno Sta.\n331\tton next Wednesday night, Oct\n332\tr. All ladies' bring supper for\n333\t,vo, The funds collected will be\n334\tpsed in the completion Of uhe\n335\tschool building,\n336\t\n337\t=== 6_1901-10-18_p6_sn83045462_00280655430_1901101801_0356.json\n338\tEvening star. · 1901-10-18\n339\tcrops: None\n340\theadline: \n341\t---\n342\tsociations were rejected, and the conven-\n343\ttion voted to admit hereafter a delegate\n344\tfor each fifty members of the subordinate\n345\t\n346\t\n347\tUnions\n348\t\n349\t\n350\tWith the consideration of several amend\n351\tments to the bylaws the morning session\n352\tclosed. The delegates did not visit the\n353\t\n354\t\n355\tWhite House but will instead send com\n356\t\n357\t\n358\tmittee of two members to confer with the\n359\t\n360\t\n361\tPresident tomorrow at noon.\n362\t\n363\t\n364\tThe Closing Sessions.\n365\t\n366\t\n367\tThe discussions during the morning ses-\n368\tsions were keen and earnest. The after\n369\t\n370\t\n371\tneon is to include the election of officers.\n372\t\n373\t\n374\tIn the evening at the closing meeting the\n375\t\n376\t\n377\tdelegates will be given test exhibitions.\n378\t\n379\t\n380\tThe mediums chosen are Mrs. May S. Pep-\n381\t\n382\t\n383\tper, whose letter tests of Wednesday ex\n384\tcited the wonder of the non-spiritualists\n385\t\n386\t\n387\tpresent. and Miss Margaret Gauie whose\n388\t\n389\t\n390\ttests tpnight promise to be entertaining\n391\tas those of Mrs. Pepper Only one address\n392\t\n393\t\n394\tis to be delivered.\n395\t\n396\t\n397\tOi Conferences Held.\n398\tA conference on the local societies occu-\n399\tpied the delegates the greater part of yes-\n400\tterday afternoon. Chairman Gates of\n401\tspecial committee on reports from the 'slo-\n402\tcalls made statement to the convention\n403\t| in which he deplored forcibly the neglect\n404\t| OF many delegates to provide the national\n405\tassociation with statements of their assets\n406\tand membership. In reply considerable\n407\t| group of the de1inquents made their way to\n408\tre-\n409\t| quired blanks Mr. Schrm made an an\n410\t| nouncement which met with the most en-\n411\tthuSiaStic favor, to the effect that a leg\n412\t| acy to the Baltimore Union which had ,een\n413\tcontested in the courts had been so ad-\n414\tjusted by compromise as to give the cause\n415\t| OF spiritualism the sum of over $25 900, and\n416\twith that sum the congregation intended\n417\t| to erect a modest hall and create an IN\n418\t| dowment fund.\n419\t\n420\t\n421\t! An address by Mr.. J. b. Towns md\n422\t| former law partner to the late Senator C.\n423\t8. Brice of Ohio, was on the program for\n424\t| the close of the afternoon meeting. but\n425\t| consumed nearly all of the session,\n426\t! The chief features of the evening session\n427\twere the spirit readings of Mrs Zaii.l\n428\t| Brown kates and Miss Margaret Ga,,Ie\n429\t| Both mediums have methods similar 10\n430\tthose used by most exponents of the spir-\n431\t| itualistic faith. There were addresses Of\n432\tthirty minutes each during the evening by\n433\tMr. Thomas Grimshaw, an English stud.n,\n434\tof psychic phenomena, and Mrs Helen p\n435\tRussegue, one of the most argumen.atira\n436\t| of the delegates on the rolls of the present\n437\t| convention.\n438\t\n439\t\n440\t| In response to the request of Mrs Sarah\n441\tE. Musser that a tree in front of 2Oa9 T.\n442\t| street northwest be removed the District\n443\tCommissioners have replied that 1t stands\n444\ton private property. and therefore they are\n445\tunable to undertake its. removal\n446\t\n447\t=== 33_1888-04-05_p1_sn92051501_00271762665_1888040501_0409.json\n448\tThe Press. · 1888-04-05\n449\tcrops: None\n450\theadline: \n451\t---\n452\tThe Conn. Spiritualists' Association at its annual meeting in Hartford last week elected Mrs. M. A. Dwight of Stafford one of it's vice presidents The president is Geo. w. Burnham, and the secretary and treasurer Mrs. J. c. Robinson, both of Willimantic.\n453\t\n454\t=== 3_1875-10-25_p1_sn85033673_0028077943A_1875102501_1005.json\n455\tPublic ledger. [volume] (Memphis, Tenn.) 1865-1893 · 1875-10-25\n456\tcrops: None\n457\theadline: OBITUARY.\n458\t---\n459\tDied, on the morning Of the 23d inst,\n460\tGILMER. son OF George Il, and Louisa Dabney,\n461\tat their residence in DeSoto county, Miss.\n462\taged twelve years.\n463\t\n464\t\n465\tThe dread pcourge Of the country, swamp\n466\tfever, seized upon this interesting little boy\n467\tand soon hurried his spirit into the unknown\n468\tworld. Not only was he the sweet treasure Of\n469\this own family, but beloved by all who knew\n470\thim. Sweet tempered, amiable and sprlghtly.\n471\the made himself interesting to strangers as\n472\twe'll as friends. When the shadows of death\n473\tencompassed him, inspired by the affectionate\n474\timpulses of his heart, he called together his\n475\tfather, his mother and brothers, and asked\n476\teach one to kiss him before his going into the\n477\tspirit land. One brother. when kissing him.\n478\ttold him he would soon get we'll and they\n479\twould take the hunt together they had talked\n480\tabout. mo, Ned\" he replied. II believe\n481\tl would rather 90 to neavenl\" AnS snre\n482\tenough, a few hours afterward, Inc gallant\n483\tsoul Of tae little Christian soldier did start on\n484\tits journey to Heaven, Comfort to the bro\n485\tken-hearted mother She need not be told to\n486\tremember little GILMER. His image and tho\n487\tmemories Of his sayings and doings viii be\n488\tenshrined in her heart of hearts, and will\n489\tcast a sad shadow over all her future life.\n490\tsee in thy gentle eyes a tear;\n491\t\n492\t\n493\tlney turn TO me. il soFrowful thought;\n494\tInou thinkest OF friends. the good and dear\n495\t/, Who were for a time. aud now are not.\n496\tLike these fair children Of cloud and past\n497\t\n498\t\n499\tmat blisten 2 moment and then Are lost.\n500\t\n501\t\n502\tFlake after fake\n503\t\n504\t\n505\tAll lost In the dark and silent lake\" O.\n506\t\n507\t=== 10_1912-10-25_pna_sn93055779_0029587269A_191210250