ttle lips too trem-\n671\tuIous for words. lie held her hand\n672\tand mighty struggle went on within\n673\thim. lie knew what the tears meant\n674\tand lc longcd to take her in his arms;\n675\tbut pride and caution whispered:\n676\t\n677\t\n678\t\"Wait! You lave the world before\n679\tyou\n680\t\n681\t\n682\tAud SO he spoke no word, but left\n683\ther.\n684\t\n685\t\n686\tlie had never really meant to give\n687\ther up. He iutcHdcd in the near\n688\tfuture to 90 back for her; but, first,\n689\the was too poor to take the time, and\n690\taficrward the business world claimed\n691\thim as its slavc-its slave when most\n692\the felt himsclfit lis master.\n693\t\n694\t\n695\tSO the days passed by and lie never\n696\twent back.\n697\t\n698\t\n699\tThen he heard that another had\n700\twon her, and for moment j. Wee,\n701\tdon bradshaw admitted to himself\n702\tthat he hal possibly made mistake\n703\tIN life after all.\n704\t\n705\t\n706\tIn course of time hc married his\n707\tpartners daughter. lie paid her bills,\n708\ttreated her with, respectful considcra-\n709\tton, and when sic died regretted the\n710\tunfortunate circumstance. But her\n711\tface had no place on the window. The\n712\tyouth of the past was crying to the\n713\tman of to-day for rcparation and\n714\twould not be silcnced.\n715\t\n716\t\n717\t''Why did you treat ms SOP he\n718\tcried. \"Why did you stno my love,\n719\tteaching me this terrible absolute ill.,\n720\tdifference TO everything good or bad?\n721\tWhy did you train me to think that\n722\tmoney and what money could buy\n723\twas the best in life and nothing else\n724\ti1attcrcd? What have you given me in\n725\tfcturn for youth, love, aud libcrty?\"\n726\t\n727\t\n728\tAnd Weldon Bradshaw bowed his\n729\thead in silence. Dead Sea fruit.\n730\t\n731\t\n732\tClearly he saw now, as in the light\n733\tof noonuay, the life ho had missed.\n734\tThe life of love and higher aspiration,\n735\tthe abncgation of self that leads 10\n736\tthe \"largcr heart, the kindlicr hand\"\n737\t\n738\t\n739\tAll this he saw, and groancd in\n740\tspirit.\n741\t\n742\t\n743\tOn through the night rushed the\n744\texpress; but side by side with it kept\n745\tthe phantom train filed with the\n746\tghosts of Weldon Bradshaw's past.\n747\t\n748\t\n749\tThe Young bride had gone TO sleep\n750\twith her head on her husbands shoul-\n751\tder and the other passengers were in\n752\tvarious stages of unconsc:ousucss.\n753\t\n754\t\n755\tSuddenly violent, shuddering jerk\n756\tthroughout the train-a mighty crash\n757\tand hcaving, and then silence more\n758\tterrible. ;, My.r ~....-.--..-.~-~-...>\n759\t\n760\t\n761\tSilence f5r moments space only,\n762\thowever; thon cries, questions, excla-\n763\tnations wild confusion of tongues.\n764\t\n765\t\n766\tThe engine had jumped the track on\n767\ta down grade and half dragged the\n768\tbaggage car with it; but the passen-\n769\tger coaches were only badly shaken.\n770\t1t would have been terrible disaster\n771\tbut for the qu!ckncss a\"d nerve of the\n772\tenginc%--hc stopped the train in its\n773\town length, but bis life was the price.\n774\t\n775\t\n776\tThe crowd grew silent as they stood\n777\tabout the wreck and that motionless\n778\tobject now stretched upon the road\n779\tside.\n780\t\n781\t\n782\tThey pecrcd into each others pale\n783\tfaces, scarcely visible by the flickering\n784\tlight of a few lanterns carried in\n785\tnervous hands.\n786\t\n787\t\n788\tThank God! they were all safe\n789\tbut one man had given ur his life for\n790\tthem.\n791\t\n792\t\n793\t''lIe did his duty nobly,\" they said,\n794\tand then began TO think how they\n795\tcould continue their journey.\n796\t\n797\t\n798\t1t was no; that they were unfeelng\n799\tonly \"practicap; there was moth\n800\ting to be done and they were in a\n801\thurry.\n802\t\n803\t\n804\tOnly Bradshaw remained standing\n805\tby the body-he felt shaken, un-\n806\tncrvcd, strangely Old. Those silent\n807\tlips seemed bidding him stay. A\n808\tv6feo was speaking 10 him ihro0gls\n809\tthem UNDECIDED by other cars Even\n810\tas am wilt thou be. ~ Frank Les\n811\tlies Illustrated.\n812\t\n813\t\n814\tThe Human Figure.\n815\t\n816\t\n817\tThe proportions of the human Go-\n818\t\n819\t=== 11_1891-06-28_p16_sn90059522_00175033579_1891062801_0574.json\n820\tSt. Paul daily globe. · 1891-06-28\n821\tcrops: None\n822\theadline: PERSONALS.\n823\t\n824\tATTENTION. ATTENTION.\n825\tKNOWLEDGE IS POWER.\n826\tFIRST TIME IN ST. PAUL\n827\t\n828\t\n829\tOF MRS. DIR. DALE,\n830\t\n831\t\n832\tTRANCE CLAIRVOYANT\n833\t\n834\tAND SPIRIT MEDIUM.\n835\t---\n836\t\n837\t\n838\t\n839\tBorn with a double veil and wonderful\n840\tprophetic gift of second sight; has the power\n841\tof any two mediums you ever met; this she\n842\tis prepared to prove. Tells your entire life,\n843\tpast, present and future, in dead trance;\n844\tevery hidden mystery revealed. Tells the\n845\tfull name of future husband or wife, with\n846\tage and date of marriage, and tells whether\n847\tthe one you love is true or false tells all\n848\tyour business affairs with utmost truth, gives\n849\tadvice on divorce. contested wills specula-\n850\ttons, etc., tells your life from the cradle to\n851\tthe grave; positively no imposition; reveals\n852\teverything; understands the science of the\n853\tPersian and Hindoo magic\" or ancient\n854\tcharming, andprepares Egyptian talismans,\n855\twhich will overcome your enemies, remove\n856\tfamily troubles, restore lost affections, make\n857\tmarriage with the one you love, no failure,\n858\tremove evil influences. bad habits. Cures\n859\twitchery, rheumatism. all female complaints,\n860\tdrunkenness. opium and morphine habits,\n861\tfits and all loDg-standing and mysterious\n862\tdiseases.\n863\t\n864\t\n865\tBusiness strictly private and confidential.\n866\t\n867\t\n868\tCONSULTATION FREEt\n869\t\n870\t\n871\tSend stamp for circular.\n872\t\n873\t\n874\tCut this out and call early, for this adver-\n875\ttisment will not appear every day.\n876\t\n877\t\n878\tOffice hours, 9 a. m. to p. m. daily. Ad-\n879\tdress,\n880\t\n881\t=== 3_1925-09-10_pna_sn85040310_0051415885A_1925091001_0171.json\n882\tThe Telegraph-courier. [volume] (Kenosha, Wis.) 1888-1946 · 1925-09-10\n883\tcrops: None\n884\theadline: Wins SANDER Case\n885\t\n886\t\n887\tBy Proving Illusion\n888\t---\n889\tBerlin. - Professor Albert\n890\tMoll, one of 'Berlin's foremost psyche\n891\tphysicists, was victorious recently in\n892\tSANDER suit brought against him\n893\tby Frau Rudolf, spiritualistic medi-\n894\tUNI, for criticizing one of her per-\n895\tformances. He had said that one could\n896\tspeak of a spiritualistic phenomenon\n897\tonly then, if every step of the\n898\t\"seance\" could be carefully watched\n899\tand controlled by some licensed state\n900\tinstitution, such as the Techno-Physi-\n901\tcal institute.\n902\t\n903\t\n904\tAS the trial progressed Professor\n905\tMoll proposed that a seance be held\n906\tthen and there in the courtroom, and\n907\tthat Frau Rudolf be requested to\n908\tshow her tricks\" She strongly OBJ\n909\tjected, however stating that the at\n910\tmosphere of a courtroom was not con-\n911\tducive to summoning spiritual phe-\n912\tnomena. Professor Moll then played\n913\tthe part of the spiritualist without\n914\tdarkening the courtroom and request\n915\ted the judge to be the medium\" The\n916\tlatter's eyes were covered and the\n917\tprofessor placed both points of a\n918\tdrawing compass upon his hand, then\n919\tremoved one point. The judge failed\n920\tto notice this, and maintained both\n921\tpoints were still resting on his hand.\n922\tSeveral other experiments of a SINH\n923\tlar nature were made by DR.. Moll to\n924\tprove that many things that to the\n925\toccultist may seem like something\n926\tsupernatural have quite rational ex-\n927\tPLANTATION\n928\t\n929\t\n930\tThe court found DR.. Moll not guilty.\n931\t\n932\t=== 47_1880-05-08_pna_sn86088181_0020029693A_1880050801_0