1622\tdouble Wares at second. Here are the\n1623\tfigures.\n1624\t\n1625\t=== 10_1850-05-22_p1_sn84024718_00414183736_1850052201_0083.json\n1626\tStaunton spectator. [volume] (Staunton, Va.) 1849-1896 · 1850-05-22\n1627\tcrops: None\n1628\theadline: THE COCK LAIN GHOST.\n1629\t---\n1630\tThe recent 9Rochester knockings\" have recalled\n1631\t\"reminiscences Of similar impostures in the history OF\n1632\tthe past. Among the most celebrated is the story\n1633\tof the \"Cock lane Ghost\" which for time startled\n1634\tall London with its antics, and found believers a.\n1635\tmong the most learned as we'll as the most ignorant\n1636\tof the great metropolis.\n1637\t\n1638\t\n1639\tIn 1852, the Cock-lane ghost made its appearance\n1640\tin London. At the house of MIR. Parsons, the pous\n1641\tparish clerk of ST. Sepulchers, alarming knocklngs\n1642\tand soratchings heard BY hio daughtcI, a\" or'\n1643\tless child, only twelve years ofage. On being close\n1644\tiy questioned, she admitted that she had not only\n1645\theard these noises, but had seen fe apparilion OF\n1646\tMrs. Kent, formerly lodger in the house, reported\n1647\tto have died OF the small pox after she left H. but be-\n1648\tleved to have been murdered. The rumor spread\n1649\tlike wiId-fre and as it is always the creduous who\n1650\tfirst assembled to witness the supernatural, crowds\n1651\tof believers gathered at the Parish Clerks! The\n1652\tkuockings aud scratchings became more violent, as\n1653\tthe number Of believers increased. clergyman was\n1654\tselected among others to sit at the bedside OF Miss\n1655\tParsons 10 converse with, or exercise the ghos, as\n1656\tthe case might be. He informed the ghost In a loud\n1657\tvoice. that its answers, if in the affirmative, should\n1658\the indicated by one knock and If in the negative, by\n1659\ttwo knocks. The arrangement being concluded, Inc\n1660\tghost was asked if it the spirit of the murdered\n1661\tMary Kent, and it answered with one knock. Ihis\n1662\theightened the public anxiety, and directly she was\n1663\tremoved from her home, to several houses IN sue\n1664\tcession, and attended night after night by watchers\n1665\tand committees embracing all the learned professions.\n1666\tand number Of ladies. The knocklngs continued\n1667\tno one could account for them, and the faithful ASK\n1668\ted\"Iflhere is an mposture, explain how these KNOCK\n1669\tings are made?' Even DIR. johnson became cham\n1670\tpion of the ghost The sequel OF the affair is mus\n1671\tdescribed by the historian:\n1672\t\n1673\t\n1674\t\"At length on being pressed TO give some PROOF o.\n1675\tn\n1676\tits yeracity, the ghost consented 10 attend one o.\n1677\tthe gentlemen into the vault where The body was\n1678\tburied, and manifest lis presence by knock upon\n1679\tthe coffin.\n1680\t\n1681\t\n1682\t\"When the appointed hour arrived, the spirit was\n1683\tvery seriously advertised, shat the person to whom\n1684\tthe promise was made of striking the coffin was 'ner\n1685\tabout to vsitand that the performance OF The promise\n1686\t| into the church, and the gentleman to whom thf\n1687\tpromise was made, went with one more, into the\n1688\tvault The spirit was solemniy required to perform\n1689\tits promise, but nothing more than silence ensueo\n1690\tThe person supposed TO be accused by the ghos'\n1691\tthen went down. with several olher but no effect wa\n1692\tperceived. Upon their return, they examined tht\n1693\tGIRL hnt conld dra confession fIUI her. Detweei\n1694\tTwo and three, she desred and was permitted, to g\n1695\thome with her father.\n1696\t\n1697\t\n1698\t@This want of punctually In the ghost gave ia\n1699\ttai blow 10 its reputation. Even the most besotlet\n1700\tof the believers were staggered by it flimsy at\n1701\ttempt was therefore made 10 restore the ghosts credl\n1702\tby asserting that The coffin and the corpse had bee\n1703\tremoved, which, of course, had prevented the sPlrl\n1704\tfrom giving the signal butt on examination, the!\n1705\twere found to be safe in the vault otricter pre-\n1706\tcautions were now taken to guard against deception\n1707\tbeing practced by the girl her bed was slung Tx\n1708\thammock. in the middle of the room, and she wa\n1709\tclosely watched. Driven to her last Shifts she con\n1710\ttriyed TO secrete-but not unseen-a bit of board\n1711\tpreviously 10 her being put to bed and having,\n1712\tshe though secured the necessary materials Tor car\n1713\trying on the trick, she ventured to declare that sh\n1714\twould bring the ghost at six in the morning, she &C\n1715\tcordngly began 10 make the accustomed SOUNDS\n1716\tand, on being asked if she had in the bed any woo\n1717\tto strike upon, she positively denied The fact In\n1718\tbed clothes were opened, the board was i9une\n1719\tand this simple process annihilated the Cock-han\n1720\tGhost\"\n1721\t"}]},"uuid":"fb94b469-a671-497f-acef-e5fba77e4711","timestamp":"2026-10-31T07:39:57.423Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/c1/g03.txt","content":"=== 40_1879-01-04_p1_sn85042459_00280769009_1879010401_0005.json\nDaily Los Angeles herald. · 1879-01-04\ncrops: None\nheadline: Joo Jefferson.\n---\nOne wonders that the man is not\nentirely subservient 20 the actor,\nwrites a correspondent about Jeffer-\nson. Indeed, in private life, the\nsame tones, and even at times the\nbroken accent, are noticeable. That\nhe has good reason to be attached\nto his part, even \"a man of success\"\nwould admit, for it has brought\nhim, so I am told by an intimate\nfriend, over $1,nOO,0O0.\n\n\nIn private lifo he is very jolly,\nalways in good spirits, and in man\nners, while inclining to the brusque,\nthose of a gentleman. / had never\nseen him off the stage until last\nweek, and it must be confessed that\nno woman was ever so completely\nmetamorphosed by \"make up\" as\nour venerable jefferson. A little\nover the medium height, with q\nwell-knit frame and tendency to\nstoutness, regular and rather small\nfeatures, smooth face, and, in spite\nof his fifty years, thick, dark hair\nwithout a thread of silver. In\ndress. very plain, and wearing no\nJCW anu 40 L lClll al htt pclOIt,\nin voice and accent \"Rir,\" and,\nthat indeed, was the only recogniz.\nable point.\n\n\nWhile In Chicago he makes his\nhome at his fatber-in-law's, who\nresides In comfortable style on\nMichigan Avenue. 1t will be re-\nmembered that eleven years ego\nthe great actor married Miss\nWarren, of Buffalo, a lady who,\nthough not a member of the pro-\nfession, comes from an old and\nhighly respectable theatrical fami.\nly, and is u niece of ex-Mayor frice\nof this city. A blonde, aud very\n| graceful, she ia intellectual and\naccomplished, and has won many\nfriends through her charming\nmanners and generous heart.\n\n\nMr.. and Mrs. Jefferson have two\nbright little children, q son and\n\n=== 12_1841-08-03_p1_sn82015015_00415661265_1841080301_0822.json\nThe Madisonian. [volume] (Washington City [i.e. Washington, D.C.]) 1837-1845 · 1841-08-03\ncrops: None\nheadline: Nem Fork Correspondence.\n---\nNEW DIRK, August i 1911.\nThe news from Washington continues TO\ngladden. the hearts Of all Wings in this region,\nand those who unwaveringly resisted the over\nwhelming power Of misrule through the long\nnight of darkness, are more and more rejolced\nthat they have lived 10 see this day. The con\nfidently anticipated passage Of the BANK Bill\nand the REVENUE Bill, in addition TO the excel\nlent measures Of National defence and financial\nintegrity already enacted, have dissipated all\ngloom in this quarter. Every one feels that the\ncountry has now the very Congress that she has\nlong needed, and that all necessary and proper\nmeasures of relief TO the People will now be\nperfected But fearful will be the reyulsion,\nbitter the disappointment, if the BANKRUPT Bill\ndoes not become law before the adjournment.\nFriends at Washington would you but realize\nhow many crushed, despairing, helpless fellow\ncreatures are pining in hopeless and useless\nwretchedness for the passage of this act, you\ncould not, you DARE not go home without passing\nil! The spectres of the ruined and heart-broken\nof beggared families and blasted hopes-woud\nglare upon you from every glade and thicket by\nthe way-side. Be entreated, friends in Con-\ngress, if your understandings be not blinded TO\npolicy and your hearts dead to humanity, to\nsignalize this glorious extra session by letting\nthe hopelessly, uselessly oppressed GO FREE =\nDo SO, and millions will rise up To call you\nblessed, while you will have contributed i'm\nmensely to restore prosperity and business ac\nuvily to our depressed country.\n\n\nThe Loco-foco gathering in the Park on\nThursday evening was quite large one.\nthink from four to six thousand persons present.\nYou will have seen that the resolutions de-\nnounced every prominent measure of the session\nearept the Bankrupt Law, which was left en-\ntirely untouched. 1t was first intended to de-\nnounce the passage of any Bankrupt Bill with\nout including corporations but some of the best\nworking politicians of the party went to the\nleaders and told them that they would not stand\nt-that any measure calculated to impede the\npassage Of the Bankrupt bill would create an\nexplosion. SO it was given up. The direct as\nsertion that the Bank Charter shall be repealed\nat the first opportunity threat made under the\nsanction of the best names in the party here\nwill show the country what it has to expect from\nthe return of Loco -focoism to power.\n\n\nOur Markets wear healthy appearance\nBest Flour (Genesee) is quick at IS 81 1-2,\nand the small stock on hand is held at So\nGeorgetown IS rs; Ohio S. rs 81 l-2; Mi\nchigan Sc 62 S. rs. North Carolina Wheat,\nfirst quality, 81 23 a IS : inferior do. 81 a.\nRye 81 2 a 63c; Corn roc Oats 43c Ashes\nSIS so. Cotton, no change. N. c. Turpentine\n81 25; Tar s. 81. Coffee has advanced la.\nTea is also looking up.\n\n\nStocks droop U. S. Bank is down TO l6 Sd.\nIndiana Bonds SO ga So. New York Five\nand Halls, 90 l-2; City Elves SO.\n\n\nSterling bills 8 a 8 \"a; on Paris 5.25 $50.\n\n\n\"A terrible murder has been committed at Ho-\nboken. On Sunday morning last, Mary Cece\nliza Rogers, young woman who formerly\ntended Anderson's tobacco store, and was thence\nstyled the beautiful Cigar Girl left her mo-\ntheirs house, 1876 Nassau street, and was joined\nat the corner of Theatre Alley by young man.\nwith whom she proceeded to Hoboken. This\nwas the last that her friends have seen of her in\nlife. She did not return at night; her anxious\nrelatives advertised for her on Tuesday, and on\nWednesday her body was found in the Hudson,\noff Hoboken, bearing marks of horrible outrage\nand violent death cannot learn that any trace\nof her murderers has yet been discovered. |\nam assured that the girls character was ire\nproachablc\n\n\nWe had cold storm from Friday evening 9\nO'Clock till this morning The weather is still\ncool.\n\n\nYours, HAROLD\n\n=== 6_1920-04-02_p1_sn84024808_00212477497_1920040201_0122.json\nGreat Falls daily tribune. · 1920-04-02\ncrops: None\nheadline: Ouija Board for Him if\n\n\nHoover and Barnes Ever\n\n\nAgain Boss Grain Prices\n---\n\"But you did rely on Hoover's and\nBarnes statements?\" asked Represen-\ntative Mclaughlin. Republican, Nebraska.\n\n\nI've took them to be men CT their\nword\" replied Clements.\n\n\nLee G. Metcaff. representing the Hi\nnois Grain Dealers association. said if\nthere was ever another gain corporation\nwhen he was buying grain he would get\nan ouija board\"\n\n\nr. T. Smiley, representing Kansas and\nNebraska dealers, declared Mr.. Hoover's\nannouncement in August, 1917, of ne\ncessity to reduce the price of bread\ngrains was Infamous\n\n=== 18_1939-07-11_pna_sn88085187_00200291633_1939071101_0136.json\nThe Tacoma times. [volume] (Tacoma, Wash.) 1903-1949 · 1939-07-11\ncrops: None\nheadline: HELM HAUNTS\nTHE SENATE\n---\nClad in a startling froek coat\nof cream-colored cloth, ex-senator\nTom Hefiin haunts the senate cloak\nrooms these days like substantial,\nbut somewhat wistful. ghost. He\nis here for a job. and Goals of fire\nare being heaped on his head.\n\n\nHe once was famous as the most\nrahid snti-Cstholic in public life.\nNow he is about to be attached\nTO the government rayroll-snd the\ntwo men who are going to do the\ntrick are those very devout catho-\nlies. Jim Farley and Frank Muir\nply.\n\n=== 28_1909-11-12_p2_sn85038292_00415624098_1909111201_0356.json\nThe Democratic advocate. · 1909-11-12\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nOut through the DOrrlnBLOnS Japa-\nnese garden fed the Little White\nLady. There were lotus lilies on the\npond, and two great gray lanterned\nposts, green lighted. guarded the gates.\nThen came the white driveway and the\nstretch Of dark pines, and in the pines\nthe Little White Lady paused.\n\n\nThere\" she whispered and laughed\ndeiiciously. He'll never find me-\"\n\n\nBut he did find her, looming like\ngreat ghost In his white linen suit.\n\n\nyou can't get away from me-\" he\ncilanted.\n\n\nThe Little White Lady sat down on\na log. 'Tf you follow young women\ninto n dark stretch Of woods you\nshould bring chaperon with you,\"\nShe reproved him.\n\n\nMrs. Dorringtoa h asleep\"\n\n\nAsleep Jacqudine said. \"Why, it's\nonly O'clock\"\n\n\nHe laughed. Don't let us quarrel\nany more, Jackie\"\n\n\nat isn't whs QaarrcI,\" she said in\ndignantly.\n\n\n\"Then let us say 1t is l.\" he muir\nINJURED, \"and. having made of myself\nthe culprit, we win return to the origi-\nnal subject\"\n\n\n\"No You win ask me TO marry you\nagain, and won't\"\n\n\n\"And yet you wii give me no rea-\nson.\" he said gravely. coming close to\nher 'If you Will say once with your\neyes meeting mine that you do not love\nme ! viii promise not to ask you again\nfor fortnight\"\n\n\nHer eyes fell. II want to be free\"\n\n\nHe drew a long breath. \"Oh. Little\nWhite Lady\" he said, would you feel\nGagged to come to me? / have loved\nyou so long, and you are only nine\nteen now. | was such big boy when\nyou used to fiutter through the garden\nnext door and peep through the hedge\nand beg for our roses\"\n\n\n\"And even then you called me the\nLittle White Lady\" she reminded him.\n\n\n\"Yes. and when ] went away two\nyears ago ! thought you would give\nme your heart. Little White Lady.\nBut ever since my return you have\nevaded every question\"\n\n\nIdlueI ueeuS me.\n\n\nThat night Philip walford had\nlong talk with his host. \"Is the father\nstill daft on plant parasltesT' he asked.\n\n\n\"Yes, and Jacqueline reads to him\nevery night because his eyes are bad\"\n\n\nIT'S beastly shame\" Philip flung\nout\n\n\nWhen Jackie went home she refused\nto talk to Philip,\n\n\n\"Of course | saw him.\" she told her\nsisters wearily the first morning at\nbreakfast, \"and he's Just the same old\nPhil\"\n\n\ndid he make love TO your Mlnette\ndemanded.\n\n\n81 should hope not II was their\nfather who spoke. You know Jacque-\nline is already promised\"\n\n\n\"Oh. that\" laughed Minette. \"of\ncourse! But that wouldn't keep her\nfrom flirting with Philip\"\n\n\nJacqueline's face famed. 1T would\"\nshe declared. 81 wouldn't fiirt with\nPhilip for anything IN the whole wide\nworld. He is too good. too true. 10\nfiirt with\"\n\n\nMiutllt lOohtu NJ uFE fuIiblhiNs\nafter that defense\" she said. \"the\nbeast had better watch out for ri.\nval\"\n\n\nThe goggles were turned toward her.\n\"Minette,\" her father asked in a heavy\nvoice, 'whom do you call the beastr\"\n\n\n'Von Puttkamer.\" said Mlnette\nsaucily.\n\n\nAnd now the goggles were turned on\nJacqueline. my dear. have given\nmy word TO my friend\"\n\n\nJackie stood up and faced him. But\nIL haven't said that would father\"\nshe said.\n\n\nAnd with that she fed from the\nroom and TO the foot OF the garden,\nwhere there was an old sundial that\nmarked the dividing line between the\ntwo places Beyond was the walford\nrose garden. and on a stone bench\noverrun with Ivy sat Philip sketching.\n\n\n\"Ah. my Little White Ladyr' he said\nand made room for her, and then, as\nhe saw her face. What troubles you.\nDEAR\"\n\n\n\"Ts anybody happy. Philip she\nhDhCNs anu Sie WIS very Wultc.\n\n\nHe shook his head. \"Who knowsT'\nbe murmured But all the rest Of the\nworld might weep n only | could make\nyou smile\"\n\n\nLET me sit by you and watch YOU\"\nshe said. \"and don't ask Inc any ques.\ntions.\"\n\n\nWith his charcoal he sketched the\nbench on which she sat. and. smiling\nwhimsically, he drew her figure. chang\ning her modern gown Of dimity to one\nOF flowing classic lines. and on her\nhead he put wreath OF poppies. Then\nhe drew himself at her feet clad I'm\nleopard skin and laurel crowned. and\non their faces was the radiance of love\nand Of youth. And underneath the pic\nture he wrote. \"The Gods of 10y\"\n\n\nThere\" he said as he showed It to\nher. \"I have changed the faces so that\nno one will know. But you must hang\nII In your room, and when you think\nthat the world is dark look at II and\nremember that the day you tell me you\n]ove me that day win you and be\ngods Of joy. Jackie\"\n\n\nAfter that he saw little of her. anc\nhe busted. himself planning the great\nyearly festival that the Wafords al.\nways gave to their neighbors. This\nyear 1t was to be a costume ball. held\non the lawn.\n\n\nIl shall go as Spring.\" Minette\ntold Jacqueline, 'and Lucia wants to\nbe 'Shepherdess,' and you might be\nBeauty and have Won Puttkamer as\n\n=== 11_1897-01-16_p1_sn88064492_00295873814_1897011602_0032.json\nThe weekly Thibodaux sentinel and journal of the 8th Senatorial District. [volume] (Thibodaux, Lna. [i.e. La.]) 1875-1898 · 1897-01-16\ncrops: None\nheadline: \"A FEUILLETON\nLES ADVENTURES\n\n\n-DE-\n7. de\n\nPAR CHAR LIE DE s LIS.\n\nLIZA COUR EFT LA WILLIE.\n---\nL'opinlon publique est pour nous\ndit Elzevier, mails je doute du tribu-\nnal, il lui faudrait mieux que DIES\ntemoignages, il lui faudralt des\npreuves ecrites -. Oh les trouyer,\nil ny en- q pas.\n\n\nIl y en- q, maugrebleu !! s'ecria\nsur lie senil la voix du capitaine LIE\nSauyetat. J'arrive expires de Rotter-\ndam pour vous l'apprendre -. e\n| 81 vous me secondez, nous les auron\nDIES demain.\n\n\nA-oU PRIoLo A PEU D'AGREMENr.\n\n\nLIE capitaine La Sauvetat, vive-\nment interroge par Francois ct Daniel,\ns'expllqua ainsl\n\n\n-J'arrive tardlvement, parce que\n\n\n'attendais certaine cassette contenant\nles papiers lie mon frere. Ellie s'etait\negaree en- route. Entin ellie NEST\nparvenue.\n\n\nEth bien ?\n\n\nEllie etait vide. Les assassins,\nprobablement, avalent tout detruit\n\n\n-. mails ans plevoir une confess\nsion complete dlctee par lie capitaine\nBarriere d I'm moline theatin, qui me\nIa remise il = q cinq jours. Vous\nvoyez que je mai pas perdu de\ntends. Lisez.\n\n\nTandis que Daniel approchait la\nlampe, FRANCOIS s'etait empare de\nIecnt. .Tous deux lis lie lurent dun\nregard avide.\n\n\nNon settlement Barriere y racon-\nTAFT dams Gus ses details Reinforcement\nde Trancrede. mails encore il y\nfaisait Ia revelation s~uiyante :\n\n\nsee fut par Priolo, & taire\nintime du due Henri de Rohan, que\nIa duchesse Marguerite apprit lexis\ntence de son frere. Aussitot apres\nla mort du due, ellie ecrivit a Priolo,\nson espion, son complete, de brdler\nimmediatement les papiers du defunt.\n\n\nLItalien feignit d'obeir, mais garda\nlie testament par lequel Henri de\n\n\nRohan reconnaissait Tancrede pour\nson fills ; de plus, Ia. lettre de la\nduchesse Marguerite, un ayeu slgne\nd'elle-meme. Ces deux preuves\nauthentiques, irrefragables, Priolo\n\n\ncomptait les vendre au comte de\nChabot ; mails depuis dix ans, retenu\npar Ia lachete, il n'osa pas. ii\nm'avalt propose l'affalre, mais s'etalt\nrayise lie lendemain. Cette confidence\nme coute la vie. Je ne te demande\npas dc me venger, frere 80. LIE\n! theatin lie defend. II esige meme,\navant de s'engager a te faire tenlr Get\n\n\necrit, que je t'enloyne formel.ement\n\n\nd'spargner Priolo. Epargne.le done\n$50, mais pour reparer mon crime\nsache que les preuyes en- question ne\nquittent jamais rItalien. Files sont\nconsues dams un sachet quill porte au\n: cou. J'ai tout dit. Pardonne-moi.\"\n} Apres cette lecture\nQue faut-1l faire !? demanda\nFrancois.\n\n\nEth ! maugrebleu !! repondit le\ncapltalne, nous emparer du serpent,\n: ct, sous ga peau, saisir la preuve.\nGu lie trouver :? Comment\n\n\nFAVOR 4 notre merci +?\n&c soir meme, dit Daniel, gu\nPalais, il y q seance de mutt, ei\npreclsement pour entendre la deposi\nton de Priolo. Quelque adresse\nquelque subtitle quill y mette, sor\nrole 4 Leyde ne saurait lui faire\nhonneur, ct personne ne Sc souciers\nde paraitre de ses amis. II ser\nreviendra done seul $500.\n-Compris ! fit lie capitaine. Prof,\ntons de roccasion 80. La mutt es1\nnoire -. La mutt est noire -.\nAlerte -. ct que Dieu protege Il\n\n=== 31_1908-04-03_pna_sn83016689_00279551448_1908040301_0701.json\nThe Detroit times. [volume] (Detroit, Mich.) 1903-1920 · 1908-04-03\ncrops: None\nheadline: MARRIAGE A CASTLE\n\n\nHAUNTED BY SPOOKS'\n---\nLONDON, April 3.-AAutre temps.\nautres moeurs. At che time II was\nthe habit OF actresseJ 10 while away\nthe waits\" and intervals with needle\nwork and crochet work, but now q\nmore Intellectual occupation Is resort\nco TO, AT the Aldycn theater. for IN\nstance, the woman section OF the\nGay Gordons\" company pass away\ntheir time In putting down their views\nOF theatrical life In the form OF ep.-\ngrams\n\n\nIn q recent competition OF this ma\nture. the following ep!grams by Miss\nEllaline Terriss, were regarded as the\npest that we're submitted:\n\n\nART--An understudy for the wealth\ner In conversation.\n\n\nWORK--A disease Which Is neither\ninfectious nor contagious.\n\n\nTHE STAGE--A place OF illusions\nland o cure for them\n\n\nMARRIAGE dilapidate castle\nhaunted by two spooks--divorce ana\nalimony.\n\n\nSuCCESS--A mystery 20 your ell\nemies and disappointment to your\nfriends\n\n\nNEUTRALITY-SUSPICIOUS. OF human\nbeings with habit OF expecting their\nmoneys worth.\n\n\nSince Miss Terrisg is the wife OF\nthat we'll known actor manager, Mr..\nSeymour Hicks. her definition OF\nManager\" would be rather interest\ning\n\n\nIt's easy to induce some people TO\n\n=== 2_1829-09-03_pna_sn84020071_00414182938_1829090301_0126.json\nLitchfield enquirer. [volume] (Litchfield, Conn.) 1829-current · 1829-09-03\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\n-\ndeeds entitle them to any name which 10 most\nsurrounded by associations OF desperate VIII\nlainy. The public are little aware how COM\nplete are their arrangements for the commits.\nsion of crime, and for eluding the pursuit OF\nJustice. Their social haunts are to be found\nIn various and distant parts Of the city, and\nare provided with numerous avenues through\nwhich, at any signal Of danger, they can dis\nappear and escape detection. Sutton was\none of the gang, and the manner Of his cap\nture was as follows:\n\n\nThe Police having failed in arresting him\nas soon as was expected, the gentleman\nwhose store he had broken open, determined,\nIf it was possible thing, to have the villain\nsecured. He therefore repaired to the Five\nPoints, and after some inquiries, found a\nwatchman who had occupied that post for\nseveral years, and who knew many Of the\nFortress\" With him he entered into an ar\nrangement to secure Sutton and the watch\nman in his turn, entered into an arrangement\nwith one Of Sutton's associates, to point out\nthe place where he could be found. Having\nobtained the requisite Information, the watch\nman, accompanied by Mr.. s. Of the Sixth\nWard, proceeded to house in Laurens\nstreet, the flrst and second stories Of which\nwere occupied by the pretended wife and sis\nter of the criminal. IN the ceiling over the\nsecond story they found trap door, through\nwhich they drew themselves up into garnet\ncontaining several sleeping rooms. Their\nnext movement was through the scuttle OF\nthe roof-then upon the roof OF an adjoining\nhouse-and down through the scNttle into\nthe garrett. Here they found another trap\ndoor, through which they descended into the\nsecond story. They were now hard by the\nden in which Sutton was secreted and\nsounding the \"Sessame\" Of the \"Forties,\"\na door was Immediately thrown open by the\nobject OF their search, who, instead OF meet\nIng with some Of his associates as he expect\ned, fell suddenly into the hands OF the Oficer'\nand by him was taken to the Police Once\nwhere he met with a very cordial reception\nOn Monday morning he was escorted by the\nDeputy Sheriff, along with fifteen other prls-\noners, on board the steamboat Gen. Jackson\nand ere this has entered upon the duties aIlot-\ntod him by the Court, IN the spacious mansion\nut Sing sing.-Jour. Of Com.\n\n\nWe are called upon to notice another 10:\nstance Of self murder. A person named\nAmos Harrison, who had resided In this place\nfor some time past, last week swallowed such\nquantity Of laudanum AZ to occasion his\ndeath. He was about 90 years old had beer\n\n\nmarried but few months; and It 10 said the\n\n\nrash act was committed In consequence OF q\n\n\ndisagreement with his wife. Elizabeth. Jour\n\n\nThe Richmond Enquirer 10 unfortunate ir\n\n\nits correspondents. During the canvass FOL\n\n\nthe Presidency, It published the famous Iette'\n\n\nfrom Washington about the east room' fur\n\n\nnished, as the writer said, and he spoke only\n\n\nOf what no saw, with more than regal mng'\n\n\nniAcence. The whole was fiction; the\n\n\nwalls Of the east room were bare-tho hooI\n\n\nuncovered-and Of furniture thero wus none\n\n\nsava a. ,ye hai;asA snrna old chairs. Yet was\n\n\nThe Richmond Enquirer silent as TO the author\nOf so gross fabrication. Within few days,\n1t published, with all becoming adulation, nm\nalleged letter Of the Inst Sgnsr-Chas. Car\nroll Of Carrolton-speaking in tho courtly\nstyle Of the day, Of Gen. Jackson. LaWdari\na Iaudoto. was the caption Of the paragraph,\nand benedctions, warm and pure, were invo-\nked on the honored and venerable head Of the\nsupposed writer Of the letter, which It usher.\ned forth-when 10 it appears that again the\nEnquirer has been imposed upon, and that\nChos' Carroll OF Carrolton, wrote no such\nletter US is published in his name.\n\n\nN. y. American.\n\n\nTHE Turkish Retorm.-Thc reform lately\nmade by tho Sultan in the manners and Gus.\ntoms Of tho Turkish ladies' has not received\nthe attention which Il merits. It will produce\nbody knows that hitherto the wives and\ndaug htcrs ofthe Ottomans have led seclu-\nded life, jealousy guarded from general inter-\nted to appear unveiled In public, to make and\nwa Vtoifa kN In As,la\n\n\nin short, they now possess al the freedom of\naction and expression which belong to women\nIN the most civilized nations. What a change\nWillie wrought in the national character. The\nTurk will throw OF his solitary habits, and his\nhahitual Indifference to externals. lilo will be\nanimated by those little agreeable tribes which\nform half the charm Of life. Instead of smO-\nking his pipe from hour to hour, and waiting\nfor war.trump or an earthquake to rouse\nhim, he will become man Of the world, and\na lively member Of society. We do not des-\npair OF sceing him heading the dance or enact\ning pGs-seul in few years. He will lose\nthe stronger traits Of his character, but the\nloss Ofstrength will be paid for by the acqui-\nsition Of the graceful and the useful. His ter\nrife energy when excited. arises from the\nuF.freqUencyOf excitement. Habitualy calm and\nindifferent to ordinary things, he is a lion\nwhen reused. Hereafter portion of his en-\nergies will be wasted on the ordinary affairs of\nlife. In short, he will be more useful but\nless interesting understanding Courier.\n\n\nThe scenery along the valley Of the Con-\nnecticut is far more beautiful than that of the\nHudson. We took view from the top Of\nMount Holyoke last Saturday morning. The\nsun does not shine upon lovelier prospect.\n\n=== 20_1920-07-16_pna_sn86086499_00414214435_1920071601_0227.json\nWauwatosa news. [volume] (Wauwatosa, Wis.) 1900-1948 · 1920-07-16\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\n] N4O al uieill Ut. DuflflI- VINO, HSteSS\nne chose, need never keep office hours\nagain-he could devote himself Ia the\nresearch he loved; Cullen, whose. blknd,\nbulldog faith had made him forever\n/ independent, even Rufus Waring.\nwhose modest contribution, accepted\nout of spleenless colnmiseration, had\nsWelled to the dignity of four figures.\nand given him the means 10 show he\nworld TO Angela. And billiard himself\nhad made far more than all the other\nventurers conhined--i OF in money,\nperhaps, but in dividends payable in.\nthe medium of his self-respect.\n\n\nAnd YET. as the realities stood, now.\nne was sensitive TO the nothingness OF\nhis triumph, until such time as he had\nsome one to divide It with him. For\nthere is little pleasure in monopoly\nof happiness; not even Joke is fully\nestablished until some one appears to\n| share it; a secret is delectabe only\nwhen it's repeated. conquest is\n! empty without the popular acclaim. or\nthe arrival of the historian. He felt\nthis keenly; he reflected that Of all the\n| syndicate, he alone was without a\nbeneficiary. And today, when he had\nsteeled himself to speak to Carol :.\nLike countless generations of men\nbefore him. he began vaguely to\nwonder what he should do if she re-\nfused him.\n\n\nWhat would be left? Only the shell\nof achievement Would he go back to\nFrance? or would he remain in Amer\nlea. and struggle for success by en-\ndowning war charities out of his glori-\nGus income to-be? Also and\nthis was enervating what\nshould he say to her? 1t Is given to\nfew men to propose twice. in different\ncharacters, to the same girl.\n\n\nThe train plowed and painted through\n! the thickening drifts; billiards watch\nwas coming out of his pocket at five\nminute intervals; here was Rochester\nat last three hours late !.\nand there, shining dimly through\nbanked clouds, was the sun The\ntrain seemed warmed to greater effort\nby its mere appearance; billiard, who\nhad measured time hy weeks, then by\ndays, and more recently by reluctant\nhours. began to mark the minutes from\nhis mental calendar.\n\n\nAnd then. after an interminable\ncentury of impotence, the outlying viii\nlages. gray and smoky; the fiat wastes\nof Solvay; the road slowly becoming\nstreets; the buildings adding height\nSyracuse!\n\n\nHis feet were on the platform he\nwas hurrying forward. Ahead of him\nand in his excitement he STUN\nbled heavily there, coming\ntoward him Carol and the\nDoctor, befurred and rosy no\nquestion of the welcome they were\nbringing him\n\n\nHis own initial remarks were grossly\nincoherent. There were no words TO\nfit the situation perhaps he did it\ngreater justice by the disconnected\n/ sounds he made And then he was\nentering the Doctors closed car; they\nwere bouncing over the cobbes Of the\nlower city; they were attacking the\ngrade of James street, and he was\npeering out in an ecstasy Of memory\nat the houses where he had played In\nboyhood\n\n\nTwo o'clock on time for\ndinner to the seconds A house hang\ning with evergreen; a Christmas\nspirit permeating every nook aud\nGranny: Christmas odors-not all of\neyergreen-drifte( in tantalizing whiffs\n/ to meet him.\n\n\nA joyous interlude; a gay process\nsion; hush; gravely spoken bless\ning-Oh. that Christmasl\n\n\n\n\n\nThere came a time early In the eve\nming when billiard found himself\nalone with Carol. He had vague\nrecollection that they had been sent\n| TO look for something a corn\n| pepper, or some other equally futile\narticle and for an instant\nhe marveled at their expecting to find\n/ # in The sun-parlor. where they had\nwandered. But the sun-parlor was hap\nPLY unoccupied; and there were COM\n/ Portable chairs I'm it :. and something\nvery green and red and seasonable in\nall the windows; so that they both de-\nlayed prodigiously, and exchanged a\nnumber of highly inconsequential re-\nmarks about the decorations. Pres\nentity, without so much as a transient\nthought for the corn-pOpper, they sat\ndown with one accord. From dis\nlance the murmur of cheerful voices\nIn the living room was an adequate\naccompaniment 10 their thoughts.\n\n\nbilliards head was dropped low; his\nreyerie was so profound that not even\nCarols voice could rouse him-not I'M\ntil she spoke a second time.\n\n\nIT saba penny for Them\" she re-\npeated, amused.\n\n\n\"Ohp billiards. awakening was ex-\npIosive. \"Why, that's queer Z.\n/ was Just thinking about that MY\nself mean the first Sunday ever\n! came mn here 70 dinner. You said\n\n=== 9_1913-02-27_p5_sn85038485_00340583656_1913022701_1004.json\nThe Birmingham age-herald. · 1913-02-27\ncrops: None\nheadline: EXUM FINANCIAL DINNER\n\n\nAT COUNTRY CLUB TONIGHT\n\nGUESTS WILL OFFER SUCCESS\nTIONS AS TO BEST METHOD or\nCURING CITY'S FINANCIAL\n'TLLNESS\"\n---\nAt the Country club tonight Culpepper\nExum, president of the city commission.\nviii have as his guests so men of Bir-\nmingham. typifying the thought and Wis\ntive work. The dinner is the first of\nit's kind, as never before in the history\nof Birmingham has city official re-\nquested advice of the people in such a\nfashion The problem for those present\nto solve or attempt to solve is the sick\nness of Birmingham financially. The\nguests list, including some of the leaders\neral days ago. Financial data was dis\npatched to them for study. They are\nsupposed to be conversant with tire in\nside condition of the city's financial trou-\nBile and are expected, therefore. to offer\nsuggestions as to the best method of\ncuring that condition. Each guest will\nbe expected to outline what in his opinion\nis the best method to inaugurate. Presi-\ndent Exum is then expected to start the\nplan in operation.\n\n\nThe city's financial condition is not quite\nas bad as has been reported, it is said.\nAlthough it is true deficit exists, that\nis explained by the commission when\nthey point out that the deficit is the\nghost inherited by the commissioners\nfrom the aldermanic regime. Mr.. Exum\nthinks Birmingham is getting only\nsmall per cent of efficiency and is anx-\nious to get something for the city in the\nline of service worth while. The sug-\ngestions made tonight will be used to\ndraft bill for presentation at the extra\n\n=== 2_1890-06-21_p3_sn84021912_00211104302_1890062101_0346.json\nThe Arizona sentinel. · 1890-06-21\ncrops: None\nheadline: A Haunted House.\n---\nThis body of ours has been likened to tenement It often has haunted apartnent--the stomach Scared by the edrich sprite, dyspep ia, digestion flies and refuses to return. What can break the spell what raise the ban laid upon the unhappy organs? We answer unhesitatingly, Hostetters Stomach B.tters, and warranted in the re- sponse blythe recorded testimony of myriads ing period of over third of a century. A course of the Btters begun in any stage of the aflic5ion, and persistently followed, will terminate in cure positive, not partial The Btters restores tone to the epigastrie nerve, and puriies the juices exudng from the cellular tissue that act upon the food dgestvely, expels bile from the stomach and the blood, and promotes regular habit of body. Malaria kidney complaint, nervousness, rheumatism and neuralgia give way to this medicine\n\n=== 12_1921-09-22_p5_sn87062237_00383342818_1921092201_0090.json\nEvery evening, Wilmington daily commercial. · 1921-09-22\ncrops: None\nheadline: WHY?\nCopywright 1920 by\nWheeler Syndicate. Inc\n\nWhy Does a Ouija Board Move.\n---\nThe strange and apparently unac-\ncountable movements of the Ouija\nboard or \"planchette,\" when one or\ntwo persons rest their hands upon it, is\nascribed by many to supernatural in\nfluences. which is the reason that these\nboards are used in order TO secure the\nanswers to questions dealing with the\nfuture. But, unfortunately for this\ntheory, there is simple and entirely\nphysical explanation for the apparent\nphenomenon.\n\n\nThis explanation can be summed up\nin the three words \"rehex muscular\naction, which means nothing more than\nthe automatic and involuntary re-\nSpouse OF our muscles to impulses origi-\nmating in the brain and of which we may\n\n=== 18_1916-10-28_pna_sn90060976_00383347610_1916102801_0350.json\nThe Catholic bulletin. [volume] (St. Paul, Minn.) 1911-1995 · 1916-10-28\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nThe National Spiritualist Asso-\ncitation went OH record recently i'll\nfavor of cremation. The Catholic\nChurch has always forbidden the\npractice of cremation as being an\ninsult to the body which has been\nsanctified by the reception of the\nSacramento.\n\n=== 5_1915-03-03_pna_sn92053934_00202193845_1915030301_0037.json\nRock Island Argus. (Rock Island, Ill.) 1893-1920 · 1915-03-03\ncrops: None\nheadline: GIBBONS MASTER\n\n\nIN HUDSON FIGHT\n\nST. PAUL PHANTOM PROVES 4\nBETTER BOXER THAN\n\n\nEDDIE MeGooRTY.\n\nLatter Walks Into a Right Cross in\nTenth Which Spoiled Chance\n\n\nfor Draw\n---\nHudson WIS. March a-BattliuE\nFOR so minutes like O couple OF wild\n\n\ncats. Mike Gibbons and Eddie Nc\nGoorty gave each other poWmeliug\nhere last night. but Gibbons. the mas\nter OF the teu-round game. won the\npopular decision because he was the\nbest boxer and landed oftener than\nhts Badger foe. 10 was \"a rip-roarinE\nbattle. the tide swinging 20 and fro.\nfirst one and then the other leading.\nbut the phantom. always the cool. col\nlected boxer that he IS. piled up\nenough points 20 win.\n\n\nNear the finish there was little 10\nchoose between the two men. for\nEddie seemed 20 gain strength as the\nbattle progressed. landing clean and\nhard punches He then spoiled a rally\nwhich might have won him a draw in\nthe tenth round by walking into a\nterrific right Cross. II there was any\ndoubt as 20 the result up until this\ntime. this one wallop settled things.\nmade some 5000 bugs happy and was\na ~ ending for one OF the classiest\nbattles staged in the state.\n\n\nGetting away 70 a flying start and\ntaking down the verdict in the first\nround. Eddie lost ground in the second\nbecause Gibbons was warming TO his\nwork. kept q strict watch on the Osh-\nkOsh boys famous left. dancing in\nand out OF danger. He found that\nstraight left 10 the face would reach\nits mark almost without fail and at\nthe same time take the play away from\nEddie's blows. This was all the lee\nway he needed. for he followed this\nprogram until Eddie found q way TO\ndefend himself against the blow. and\nthen Gibbons. the master. found a new\nway 20 beat down his man.\n\n\nAs this was the biggest pugilistic af-\nfair this town has had since Hector\nvas a pup. Il was proper that the emf\nnent Wisconsin boxing commission\nshould be present to guide the affair. |\nFather Hedding OF the boxing bill nd\nChairman Leginger occupied ringside /\nseats.\n\n\nThe FIRST bout brought on McCar- !\nnish and Young thinner. the latter /\nwinning Next Buff Zeidel stopped |\nlawson In one round and in |\nthe semi-wind-up Mike O'Dowd and /\nWaffle Monaghan hammered each |\nother around the ring for ten rounds /\nD'Dowd reserved the verdict.\n\n=== 7_1890-04-05_pna_sn84026844_00202193559_1890040501_0334.json\nThe Wheeling daily intelligencer. [volume] (Wheeling, W. Va.) 1865-1903 · 1890-04-05\ncrops: None\nheadline: ABppGEr FROM BELLAIRE.\n\nBrief Mention of Happenings and No,\n\n\nnl,ont pa,nIa\n---\nBrief Mention OF Happenings and Notes\nAbout People.\nThe !Etna foundry aud machine shops have a\nn big lot Of work ON hands.\n\n\nMrs. Thomas Boswell died at her home on\nGayelhiil Thursday night\n\n\nA ladyo the Fifth ward has become deranyed\nover the death OF q daughter.\n\n\nMI. curmn bought the Fink property on\nBelmont street Ut public sale for 5000.\n\n\nJacob Jackley almost severed finger with a\nwindow glass roller while at work this week.\n\n\nMarshal Darby had his clothes badly torn\nwhile afresting susanna Kane Thursday even.\ning. So\n\n\nThe Esna Glass and Manufacturing Company's\nplant is TO be offered at public sale on the 10Th\nOf May.\n\n\n| The new church furniture for the n. E.\nchurch was placed In position yesterday by\nContractor Rice.\n\n\nMr.. Everett Baker and Miss Lena McKcen\nwere quietly married by the Rev, Mr. Lane at\nhis residence, Thursday night.\n\n\nMiss Lizzie Taylor, the training teacher here.\nmas resigned her pace and Will be instructor at\nseveral institutes this summer.\n\n\nMiss villa Wise, one Of the yery efficient teach.\ners here, has resigned her position and accepted\nsimilar one at Washington, Pa.\n\n\nWord was received by Cyrus II. strahe yes.e r.\nday that his son Charles Is on his way hone\niIom the tarwest, and that he Is quite sick.\n\n\nThe dwelling OF Rebecca Blowers, near Barnes.\nVile was burned Thursday night. Lots 81, 600;\nno insurance. 1t was caused by q defective ilue.\n\n\nA sister OF Mrs. ]. Jump. OF the Fourth ward,\ni died NJ her home NJ Burlington yesterday morn\ning. MI. and Mrs. Jump left for there yester.\nday. NEE =.\n\n\nThere has been presented and paid $500 Of the\n| 5000 OF water works bonds called In by the\npre!ent Board OF Water Works Trustees, 10 be\npaid April !.\n\n\nThe meeting Of Council at which final action\nwas to be had In the matter OF forming new\nward, the Sixth, failed TO materialize, as no quo\nrum oil the members put In an appearance.\n\n\nA. new brass band las been organized here\nand the members range In age from 70 years\ndown to IL years. The Oldest man is \"Uncie'' j.\nileathering,on and the youngest member Scott\nHeatheriDgton. Jr.\n\n\n/ Rev. Charles O'Meara Is the name OF the rector\nwho has been chosen for the Episcopal church\nhere and at Martins Ferry: he will divide his\n| time between these two places Mr. O'Meara\nWill arrive here on Monday next.\n\n\nTrain No. 80 on the G. & r. due here at 12:4o p.\nm. ran through landslide at Salt Run yester.\nday. The steps OF passenger car were Knocked\nOF but no other damage was done, although\nseveral trees came down with the slide.\n\n\nThe first melt was run out at the Lantern\nGlobe Company's works yesterday. One pot\nwas broken during the week, but the start, every\nthing considered, was q succeEs. Next week with\nno bad luck. everything will be on In full.\n\n\nThe candidates are husIling. trading on other\ncandidates, and the ticket on Monday will be the\nworst scratched ticket ever voted here. Under\nhand work is claimed 10 have been done, and\nwork at the pools on Monday will be very excit.\nIng.\n\n=== 5_1889-10-11_pna_sn90059522_00175033439_1889101101_0381.json\nSt. Paul daily globe. [volume] (Saint Paul, Minn.) 1884-1896 · 1889-10-11\ncrops: None\nheadline: THE OPERA HOUSE DEAL.\n\nAn Old Ghost of Uncertainty\nWhich Disturbs the Sleep of\nTheateP-GOerS.\n---\nAn Old Ghost of Uncertainty\n\n\nWhich Disturbs the Sleep of\n\n\nTheater-GOers.\n\n\nRumors of \"sure things\" in the way\nof gorgeously beautiful play houses in\nst. Paul have become so frequent of\nlate that any mention of a possibly sue\ncessfuI theater combination is regarded\nby the general public as the yariest\nchestnut. And perhaps the public is\nJustified in its opinion. Certain it is\nthat the city's amusement future is\nabout as uncertain as DR.. Days lights\nin the court house tower. The day that\nhas not developed a new theater-On\npaper-has invariably been cold one.\nYesterday being fair the new\nscheme did not fail to material\nize. The Barnes-Poupeny-Litt COM\nbination were its projectors, and the\nscheme was an entirely new one, hay\ning no connection with the Panorama\nbuilding. or the dozen other schemes\nthat these gentlemen have been charged\nwith. Mr.. Barnes returned from Du-\nluth yesterday morning. ana lie was\nsoon afterwards credited with having\nstated that, in connection with Aaron\nPoupeny and Manager Jacob Lift, he\nhad secured tease OF property OH East\nSeventh street, between Robert and\nJackson streets. Mr.. Barnes departed\nfor Duluth again before sunset, Mr.\nPOupeny is out of the city, and MIR. Lift\nis in Milwaukee, so that the could neither\nbe affirmed or dedied. They may have\nsecured a lease or an option, but it is\ndoubted that it is in the district men-\ntoned. 1t would be impractical to re-\nmodel the Ryan annex. and the rumor\nmust look to the other side of the street\nfor support. There. either corner is\nout of the question, and the only really\navailable property is in the center of\nthe block, owned by William Dawson.\nA part of this was very recently leased\nfor ten years. and the site seems to be\nout of the question. There may, how\never, be more in the story than appears\non its surface, and it is more than pos\n\n=== 61_1898-01-12_pna_sn84026409_0023728804A_1898011201_0043.json\nThe Somerset herald. [volume] (Somerset, Pa.) 1870-1936 · 1898-01-12\ncrops: None\nheadline: HE LIKED TO\n\n\nCHANGE GHOSTS.\nTHIS WAS THE REASON ALFRED\nALLEE KILLED EIGHT OF\n\n\nHIS FELLOWS.\n---\nhis hands thrust deep into his pockets\nand a queer look on his ugly face, he\nmade answer:\n\n\nbecause l like to change ghosts\"\n\n\nHe is a ghost himself now. \\ year\nago, in Laredo, three Mexicans who\nhated him found him in his shirt\nsleeves, weap0nless and intoxicated.\nThey set to work on him with knives.\nHe was incapable Of resistance, and\nsaid nothing more than:\n\n\nDon't do that You don't know\nwhat you are doing. Don't do that.'\nHe had eight wounds on him when\npicked up, and every one Of them went\nto the hollow. He was a thoroughly\nbutchered man. Each of the knife\nstabs represented q life that he had ta-\nken. Alice was an accurate shot, re-\nmarkably swift with his pistol and ore\nof the most coolly desperate men the\nRio Grande frontier has known. The\npassion for slaughter seemed to grow\non him, and in the last decade Of his\nlife he killed four men in ati'rays. For\neach Of these killings he stood trial in\nthe courts and was acquitted, sometimes\non the plea Of self-defense and sOnle-\ntimes for no reason at all that any one\ncould see. Occasionally he was drunk\nwhen making his record, but not al\nways. He was a mercilcss man at all\ntimes, and whisky did not make him\nany mere or any less muTderOus.\n\n\nAlfred Alice was born in Southwest\nern Texas and followed the business Of |\nall natives. That is to say, he was n\nranchman. He had only common\nschool education and early developed\ntraits of wildness. He consorted hab- |\nitualIy with the rustler part Of the pop\nulatiOn and was a frequent figure at /\ndances, horse races and cock fights He /\nwas desperate gambler when once en- /\ngagged, and those who dealt for him\nwere careful to deal square\" More /\nthan once he was accused of standing |\nin with the many gangs of cattle thieves /\nwith which the country was infested, |\nbut this vas never proved. Its certain |\nthat he was a rigidly upright man in\nbusiness and his word was as good as\nhis bond.\n\n\nThe first four murders with which he I\nwas charged were ordinary affairs the |\nranch, the cow camp and the cattle I\ntrail The victims were Mexicans or\ncowboys of no prominence, and they /\nbrought their slayer no great amount Of I\nreputation. He was known as a man /.\nwho would shoot quickly and well, and /\none upon whom it was hard to get the /\ndrop, but he was not classed with the /\ngilt-edged killers, of whom something\nlike a dozen owned the Rio GraHde !\ncountry. Alice WAS not brought into\nimmediate contact with any one Of\nthem, which was perhaps good thing\nf T them, and he went along the even\ntenor Of his way, killing an obscure\nperson now and then, but exciting no\nspecial comment or remark.\n\n\nAbout this time he moved to Pearsall,\nthe county seat of Frio county, and en-\ngagged in shipping cattle. 1t was at the\nheight of the \"nesterp excitement.\nPasture fences were cut nightly, cattle\nwere stolen by the hundred head and\ntSe rough element was in the ascendant.\nThe Rsnehmen Of FriO county formed\nthe Cattltmen's Protective Association\nand made a business Of running down\nthe thieves. Some Of them were tried\nand sent to the peniteniiary, but good\nmany more quietly disappeared. 1t\nwas given out that they had fled the\ncountry, and no one made particular\ninquiry for them. All through the\nmonths Of October and November, 1885,\nhowever, it was noticed that the Mex\nlean vultures were plentiful about the\nmesquite grown pastures Of FTiO county.\nNow and then a corpse with a good\nmany bullet holes in it floated down the\nFriO river. simultaneously the cattle\nbusiness began to pay larger dividends\n\nIi was not known then, but it subse-\nquently developed that Alice was one\nOf the most trusted and effective agents\nof the Cattlemen's Protective AssOcia-\nton. His former intimacy with the\nrustler clement gave him exceptional\nadvantages, and be utilized them. \\\nchoice was offered to him, and he wise\nly decided to range himself on tie de\nOf law and order.\n\n\nRe-established in the good graces Of\nthe wealthy men Of that section and\nwell paid by them, his desire to kill\nsomebody not a rustler got the beltsr\nof him. The affair brought him Into\nState prominence. The victim was\nnamed Rhodes. He was shot on the\npublic square Of Pearsall. He reached\nfor his pistol, but Alice had him cover\ncred before his fingers touched the butt\nand be went dead in his tracks.\nRhodes was well connected in the low\ner counties and his slayer was bitterly\nprosecuted. Self fdefense was the plea\nand justifiable homicide the verdict.\nThis affair cost Alice all the money he\nhad made from the cattlemen and he\nwent to drinking.\n\n\nBoarding an international and Great\nNorthern passenger train at COtulla,\n\n=== 19_1913-02-21_pna_sn83016589_00393340927_1913022101_0070.json\nThe Mankato free press. [volume] (Mankato, Blue Earth County, Minn.) 1879-1924 · 1913-02-21\ncrops: None\nheadline: IS SPIRiT!SM\n\n\nONLY DEM0NISM?\n\nAre Mediums EaCaived as Well\n\n\nas Their Followers\n\nTO THE BIBLE?\n---\ndiums Come Not From Dead HUI\nmans, but From the Fallen Angels,\nSaws Pastor Russel-in the Bible He\nTraces Their Histcry-Satan Him\nself Poses as an Angel of Lisht-The\nBible Is the Torch Of Liberty and In\ntellsence, the Foe to Superstition,\nignorance and Demonism.\n\n=== 5_1910-04-08_p10_sn85066387_00175046586_1910040801_0175.json\nThe San Francisco call. · 1910-04-08\ncrops: None\nheadline: SEALS WIN OUT IN\n\n\n13 INNING GAME\n\nShades of Night Are Falling\nas Oaks Give Up the Ghost\n\n\nof Chance\n---\n1t took the Seals Is long but inter\nestiug innings to defeat the Oaks yes\nterdav afternoon, but Mohler's crew\ncame through with the wallops shortly\nbefore the shades of night fell. 1t was\n10 I when the last Oak faded away\nin the thirteenth.\n\n\nThe oaks drew first blood in the third\nafter Christian had famed. Wares was\nsafe on MundOrffs high heave to first\nand he Immediately purloined the sec\nond station. Swander flew out to Mel\nchior and il was up to Willie Hogan.\nWillie drove one TO Tenant, and when\nTommy fumbled the ball the Oakland\nman was safe and Wares found plenty\nOF time i'm which to make the plate.\nThe Seals tied il up in the seventh.\nwhen NeIchior beat out an infield hit\nand scored on Jimmy McHale's scorch-\ning double TO right.\n\n\nNeIchi0r and Tenant both singled\npast short in the thirteenth and ad-\nvanced on Mundorfr's sacrifice. After\nMcBale had passed away, Williams hit\n10 Wares and the latter threw high\nover 1rSt. allowing Melchior and Ten\nnant to register. Griffin hit to right\nand Williams was thrown out at third.\n\n\nCaptain Alohler directed things from\nthe bench for six innings, when he was\nchased 10 the clubhouse by Umpire Van\n8altren. Witt lat a hated ball and\nMohler interposed some objections to\nthe ruling which Van Haltren did not\nlike Hence the Kids banishment\n\n\nMiller pulled off the one large circus\nstunt of the game in the eleventh in\nming. when be stabbed Hogan's hard\ndrive with his unglOved hand and\ntossed the bail to BOdie in time to\ndouble Wares at second. Here are the\nfigures.\n\n=== 10_1850-05-22_p1_sn84024718_00414183736_1850052201_0083.json\nStaunton spectator. [volume] (Staunton, Va.) 1849-1896 · 1850-05-22\ncrops: None\nheadline: THE COCK LAIN GHOST.\n---\nThe recent 9Rochester knockings\" have recalled\n\"reminiscences Of similar impostures in the history OF\nthe past. Among the most celebrated is the story\nof the \"Cock lane Ghost\" which for time startled\nall London with its antics, and found believers a.\nmong the most learned as we'll as the most ignorant\nof the great metropolis.\n\n\nIn 1852, the Cock-lane ghost made its appearance\nin London. At the house of MIR. Parsons, the pous\nparish clerk of ST. Sepulchers, alarming knocklngs\nand soratchings heard BY hio daughtcI, a\" or'\nless child, only twelve years ofage. On being close\niy questioned, she admitted that she had not only\nheard these noises, but had seen fe apparilion OF\nMrs. Kent, formerly lodger in the house, reported\nto have died OF the small pox after she left H. but be-\nleved to have been murdered. The rumor spread\nlike wiId-fre and as it is always the creduous who\nfirst assembled to witness the supernatural, crowds\nof believers gathered at the Parish Clerks! The\nkuockings aud scratchings became more violent, as\nthe number Of believers increased. clergyman was\nselected among others to sit at the bedside OF Miss\nParsons 10 converse with, or exercise the ghos, as\nthe case might be. He informed the ghost In a loud\nvoice. that its answers, if in the affirmative, should\nhe indicated by one knock and If in the negative, by\ntwo knocks. The arrangement being concluded, Inc\nghost was asked if it the spirit of the murdered\nMary Kent, and it answered with one knock. Ihis\nheightened the public anxiety, and directly she was\nremoved from her home, to several houses IN sue\ncession, and attended night after night by watchers\nand committees embracing all the learned professions.\nand number Of ladies. The knocklngs continued\nno one could account for them, and the faithful ASK\ned\"Iflhere is an mposture, explain how these KNOCK\nings are made?' Even DIR. johnson became cham\npion of the ghost The sequel OF the affair is mus\ndescribed by the historian:\n\n\n\"At length on being pressed TO give some PROOF o.\nn\nits yeracity, the ghost consented 10 attend one o.\nthe gentlemen into the vault where The body was\nburied, and manifest lis presence by knock upon\nthe coffin.\n\n\n\"When the appointed hour arrived, the spirit was\nvery seriously advertised, shat the person to whom\nthe promise was made of striking the coffin was 'ner\nabout to vsitand that the performance OF The promise\n| into the church, and the gentleman to whom thf\npromise was made, went with one more, into the\nvault The spirit was solemniy required to perform\nits promise, but nothing more than silence ensueo\nThe person supposed TO be accused by the ghos'\nthen went down. with several olher but no effect wa\nperceived. Upon their return, they examined tht\nGIRL hnt conld dra confession fIUI her. Detweei\nTwo and three, she desred and was permitted, to g\nhome with her father.\n\n\n@This want of punctually In the ghost gave ia\ntai blow 10 its reputation. Even the most besotlet\nof the believers were staggered by it flimsy at\ntempt was therefore made 10 restore the ghosts credl\nby asserting that The coffin and the corpse had bee\nremoved, which, of course, had prevented the sPlrl\nfrom giving the signal butt on examination, the!\nwere found to be safe in the vault otricter pre-\ncautions were now taken to guard against deception\nbeing practced by the girl her bed was slung Tx\nhammock. in the middle of the room, and she wa\nclosely watched. Driven to her last Shifts she con\ntriyed TO secrete-but not unseen-a bit of board\npreviously 10 her being put to bed and having,\nshe though secured the necessary materials Tor car\nrying on the trick, she ventured to declare that sh\nwould bring the ghost at six in the morning, she &C\ncordngly began 10 make the accustomed SOUNDS\nand, on being asked if she had in the bed any woo\nto strike upon, she positively denied The fact In\nbed clothes were opened, the board was i9une\nand this simple process annihilated the Cock-han\nGhost\"\n","numLines":1721,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1721}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"39c4c8fb-8386-42ea-8c15-4814748fdb2a","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"other"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2c-reader","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
