e\n1326\tcellar. Here was terribly awkward position for\n1327\tfine gentleman to be 10. There was no getting\n1328\tmy leg out of its li,nbo in the dark, and to apply\n1329\t! for help would discover me. Luckily had my\n1330\twalking stick and with the help of that made shift\n1331\tto hobbIe to my lodgings, where arrived undis-\n1332\tcovered, thanks 10 the darkness and latenesss Of\n1333\tthe hour. But the difficulty was not over. | had\n1334\tlost my leg, and no one could make me such anoth.\n1335\terr or, if it could be replaced, the thug could not\n1336\tbe effected without delay ofmany days, and the\n1337\tstory would infaibly get wind. What should\n1338\tdot knew the house into which my stray limb\n1339\thad slept, bu. was not on the right terms with the\n1340\toccupant to trust him with the secret. This was\n1341\tthe most unucky circumstance of a!l-he was\n1342\tJoe Clackaboui, person with whom had quar-\n1343\t! rel, and who was moreover an arrant busybody.\n1344\t\n1345\t\n1346\tIn short. he was no berson to enlrust with the Sc\n1347\tcret of wooden leg\n1348\t\n1349\t\n1350\t| At last thought struck me of method to get nv\n1351\tleg and save nv credit, for saw plainly that my\n1352\tleg must be had Immediately, or else The cat must\n1353\tbe let out of the bag. thought of Beau Babble>\n1354\tton, the foppish fellow mentioned above, as per.\n1355\tsonage on whom [ might tn some conscience.\n1356\tplay the trick of falherIng my lost limb had got\n1357\tmyself into bad odour with his sisters and two or\n1358\tthree score oftheir gosping female acquaintance.\n1359\t| bv meaus Of his foppery and the insensbility OF\n1360\tmy timber toes, and determined now 10 be revcn-\n1361\tged on him by means of the same inlractabe MEM\n1362\tDer. The plan was this--lo send by trusty SER\n1363\twant, note to Mr.. CIackabout, requesting the leg\n1364\tin the name of Beau Babbleton: by which means\n1365\tshould get my limb again without being suspect.\n1366\ted, and Beau might account for The superiority\n1367\t| of shinhnnes in HE own animal acanomy ELI\n1368\tas he was able. ....sa,\n1369\t\n1370\t\n1371\tThe plan succeeded to admiration; and much\n1372\tbetter than looked for. had the luck to see\n1373\tdarky passing under my window next morning,\n1374\tand him sent off with the note: You must i'm\n1375\tagine the surprise of MIR. and Mrs. Clackabout\n1376\tand his two sisters, they sa: at breakfast, when\n1377\tthey received the following.\n1378\t\"Mr. Babbleton's compliments to Mr.. Clacka.\n1379\tbout, and requests that he will have tae goodness\n1380\tto despatch him his leg by the bearer; il will be\n1381\tfound in the cellar. Mr.. B hopes to be excused\n1382\tfor stepping through Mr., C's cellar door last eve\n1383\tming\"\n1384\tNobody knew what to make of this strange\n1385\tepistle at first. One thought it hoax, but on\n1386\tsending into the cellar to examine, the leg was\n1387\tfound sure enough. and the breach in the cellar\n1388\tdoor where the mis-step had been made. And\n1389\tthen what staring and wondering there was a.\n1390\tmong the Ctackabouts at the discovery of Beau\n1391\tBebbleton's artiice. who would have thought\n1392\tlhr-they all exclaimed; \"A tip top dandy\n1393\tbuck of the first water An irresistible creature\n1394\tamong The ladies' and yet doing all this with a\n1395\twooden leg Y OH! Monsirous y!\n1396\t\n1397\t\n1398\tHowever after pretty close scrutiny Of the I'M\n1399\tfortunate limb, it was delivered TO Cufy, who\n1400\tpassed for Beau Babble,ons servant. as no ques-\n1401\ttons were asked, and my stray appendage brought\n1402\tme without any discovery being made. NowI\n1403\twas Richard myself again; 5u; Beau Babbleton\n1404\twas absolutely beside himself Mr.. Clackabout\n1405\tCHANGED to meet him the same forenoon. and wish\n1406\thim Joy on the recovery of his leg. Bean repll-\n1407\ted that his leg had never been i'll. as\n1408\t\n1409\t\n1410\tNot i'll to be sure, but terribly out of Joni 