and it has never left him\n1545\tTer, and never will, although he is\n1546\twing gray with years and Passing\n1547\t\n1548\t\n1549\tyears of passion, and she is a faded,\n1550\tcary woman, out of whose life hope\n1551\tas disappeared, and for whom remains\n1552\tonly the passive obedience to fate.\n1553\tWhen she found herself abandoned she\n1554\tdid not throw herself @7sto the slums.\n1555\tShe did not break under the calamity\n1556\twhich wrecks so many lives. She did\n1557\tr. not hurry herself out of the world.\n1558\tThe disaster revealed her to herself,\n1559\tbe , and all the aimless and restless ele-\n1560\tments of her life crystalized into one\n1561\tn Settled pupose, which, for over twenty\n1562\t. been EARS, has absorbed every other par\n1563\tuis, has Ee, hope, thought and energy of her\n1564\t.eAl-- lv~9lx L.\n1565\t\n1566\t=== 32_1897-10-13_p12_sn85066387_00175037615_1897101301_0203.json\n1567\tThe San Francisco call. · 1897-10-13\n1568\tcrops: None\n1569\theadline: EARLE COT\n1570\t\n1571\t\n1572\tMONEY FOR\n1573\t\n1574\t\n1575\tMESSAGES\n1576\tCoined Money From the\n1577\tOccult Warblipgs From\n1578\t\n1579\t\n1580\tthe Skies.\n1581\tMEDIUMS DEVELOPED TO ORDER.\n1582\tCharles Webber Was Made to\n1583\tBelieve That Spirits Hovered\n1584\t\n1585\t\n1586\tGer Him.\n1587\tSTATES BRING kL0NDIkE PRICES.\n1588\tHow Spurious Medium Cultivated a\n1589\tHarvest Rolf and Departed\n1590\t\n1591\t\n1592\tWith Gold.\n1593\t\n1594\tSTATES BRING kLOIDIKE PRICES\n1595\t\n1596\tHow Spurious Medium Cultivated a\n1597\tHarvest Rolf and Departed\n1598\t\n1599\t\n1600\tWith Gold\n1601\t---\n1602\tIf all reports in spiritualstic circles are\n1603\ttrue Edward K. Earle, independent\n1604\t"}]},"uuid":"d8e06903-dc6e-471c-927e-f863358a669b","timestamp":"2026-10-31T07:44:43.210Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/c1/g05.txt","content":"=== 4_1920-03-01_pna_sn98069867_00415621693_1920030101_0995.json\nThe news scimitar. (Memphis, Tenn.) 1907-1926 · 1920-03-01\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Tue Ministers\nDiscuss Claims\n\n\nOf SpiTitUGLiSnl\n---\nSnirituaIism was the theme of two\n\n\nsermons from Memphis puipits Sunday\nnight In neither one were claims made\n\n\nby advocates Of spiritualsm admitted.\n\n\nThe Christian church has always\n\n\ntaken a stand against spIrituaiism, the\n\n\nBible likewise condemns it, declared\n\n\nthe Rev II. o. Hofstead ID, D., pastor\nOf Court Avenue Presbyterian church,\nin the second sermon OF series on\nthe subject\n\n\n\"Spiritualism has not yet established\nits case and has brought men no GER\ntain voice out Of the unseen depths,\nRev. Robert George, pastor OF the FIRST\nCongregational church told his cungre-\ncation. Or. George is also preaching a\nseries of sermons on the subject.\n''In the New Testament\" said Or.\nHofstead, when Dives requested Abra\nham TO send someone with message\nTO his kinsmen. was told that II could\nnot be done. There was a great gulf\nfixed beyond which they could not so,\nand besides il would do no good,\n\n\nDO we need comfort2 Let me be the\n| last one to withhold that, but you don\nwant it from any source that has no\n| basis in truth. There is comfort Tor\nthose who have lost their loved ones.\n| It's found at the mercy seat. Take\nyour grief to God in prayer, and you\nwill realize the power Of the gospel u\n\n\nheal your broken heart. ,\n\n\nThere are two facts of great i'm\nportance to be seriously considered N\nour study of modern spiritualsm,\" sale\n| DIR. George\n| \"First, that spirit communications car\nonly be obtained through mediums, toI\nmediumship is the cornerstone Of THIS\ndoctrine. 1t seems strange that tbs\nspirits of the great and beloved dead II\nthey can communicate with the living\n\n\nat all why they do not bring their mes\n\n\nsages directly to bereaved hearts.\n\n\n| Second fact that obtains is that tht\n\n\nslender messages gotten through are\nunsatisfactory and unworthy of the de,\n\n\nparted dead that at best one should\n| Judge that they are but the mental re\n| Elections Of earnest yearning inquirers\n\n\n1t would see that there should be min\n\n\nistries more worth while than the dis\n\n\nclosure of lost articles, and table tip\nping and ouija board writing and thu\nusual modes of seance. SO far spirit\nism has brought us no great and mighty\nassurances Of that invisible life on be\nvond, and has provided no additona\n| information nor confronted humanity\n\n\nwith any mighty assurances.\n\n=== 15_1864-07-09_pna_sn83045462_0028065422A_1864070901_0187.json\nEvening star. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1854-1972 · 1864-07-09\ncrops: cached\nheadline: SPIRITUALIsM OUT-DONEI\nAnd the epiritualistic frauds of the\nDAVENPORT BROTHERS\nEclipsed by the young and talented\nST. GEORGE SISTERS,\n---\nTHE WONDER OF MODERN TINES\nfre9S 5sEsf R ksR Rs # the and ss1Ed kHfE\nkSUb k9 D OF P'5\"de rs\naa I! 81\nHusfcAk iNsTkGhENrs SraS Am AND ffHG MYsr1c AP Gs\nT 81 S s1@sd by the\ndone without the aid of the spiRITs. The \"\n\n\nST. GEORGE SISTERS\n\n\nWill do, outdo, and undo the\n\n\n- DAVENPORT BROTHERS.\n\n\n5kf 9h1t siz I'll srFa a ackhR h 4IHE Sh\nit has yer been done before, including the BIND.\ns ING WITH tn, R0PEs R9P by ooramittee,, and IN\nFaoa fHE sFaaH ana\nOk K thsfs is\nffc ss Of ss\nLAT IONS OF THE WIsEsT wherever It GAhoe has BEEN\nintroduced.\nTHE MYSTERIOUS DISPLAYS AND INcoM-\n\n\nPREHENSIBLE DEMONSTRATIONS\n\n\nr.. IN SPIRITUALISMl\n\n\nMade in the En- b Of\ndeIpbia Boston, and other cities where they have\nexhibited,\n\n\nTHE MOST STARTLING PHENOMENON THE\n-. WORLD EVER WITNESSEDI\nThe exhibitions given by these celebrated Sis.\ntiers, are conceded by all to be\n\n\nFAR MORE MYSTERIOUS .'..\nThan those of the renowned\nDAVENPORT BROTHERS,\n\n\nrs their experiments are made entirely\nWITHOUT THF AID OF APPARTUS, AND\n- WITHIN THE sIGHT OF ALL,\nThereby assaring the most skeptical of their fair\nness in every particular. Their wonderful per-\nformanee, however,\n\n\nMUST BE SEEN 70 BE APPRECIATED.\n\n=== 7_1909-08-01_p1_sn83030214_00175039545_1909080101_0057.json\nNew-York tribune. [volume] (New York [N.Y.]) 1866-1924 · 1909-08-01\ncrops: cached\nheadline: AN IMPERIAL sPIRITuALIsT.\n---\nThe Car is by means what can be called\ndeeply read but he takes great delight in Htera-\nture the lighter kind and he is credited with the\nremark: Were / not-wel what ami should he\nthe greatest bookworm I'm the world\" One of his\nmost curious hobbies-and yet perhaps not curious\nwhen considers his extremely temDera-\nment-is the study Of occultism. He loves 10 get\nhold Of spiritual mediums and clairvoyants and he\nis very superstitOus in many ways. Some time\nago certain spiFtualist established at the\ncourt. and II rumoTed that the Gar and this\nman used frequently TO arrange seances together\nweird story used TO be told during the late\nRusso-Japanese the effect that a diver foId\nthe Car that having dved 10 the bottom OF the sea\nhe beheld the Inflated Admiral Makharon standing\non his sunken batteship with q halo round his\nhead. Tell the Car\" said the appariton that\nRussia will soon be- vctorious.\"-'M A. p\"\n\n=== 2_1916-01-08_p1_sn84024055_00280761072_1916010801_0136.json\nThe broad ax. · 1916-01-08\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Conversina With \"Ghosts.\"\n---\nSome scientists of real eminence\nhave accepted the postulate of the in\ndividuality and self consciousness of\nthe SOU after the death of the body\nand have attempted to demonstrate\ntheir belief by asserting communica-\nton with these disembodied spirits\nSir William Crookes, a profound delv-\ner in chemistry pertaining to radio-ac-\nuvity, asserted years ago that he had\ncommunication with souls of dead\nfriends. but for several years he has\nbeen silent anent this matter. Camille\nFlammarion, a rather speculative and\nsensational astronomer, declares that\nhe has seen and conversed with the\nghosts\" of dead friends. Professor\nWilliam James, brother Of Henry\nJames, the novelist, promised before\nhe died that If possible he would COM\nmunicate with his friend Professor\nHyslop, both eminent psychologists,\nbut at last accounts the soul Of Profes-\nSCR James had not spoken.-Exchange\n\n=== 16_1902-02-03_p1_sn84024441_100492325_1902020301_0240.json\nThe evening times. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1895-1902 · 1902-02-03\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Town DEMORALIZBD\n\n\nBY RELIGIOUS FRENZY\n\nLeader Goes Mad and is Sent\n\n\nto Asylum.\n\nBELIEVED HIMSELF HOLY GHOST\n\nWen and Women OF Band Pray Until\nsiOns and Become Unconscious.\n---\nCEDARVILLE. y. J.. Feb. 3.-One man\nin an insane asylum under the delusion\nthat he is the Holy Ghost. others going\nabout declaring they have been anointed\ndivine tealers, fully score mOEentariIy\nawaiting the end Of the world. and an\nentire town demoraIized is the result OF\na wave OF religious frenzy that is sweep\ning over this town.\n\n\nThe county and town authorities have\ndetermined 20 take some action to stop\nIL for fear there VIII be many more IN\nbalanced by the peculiar hystericaI strain.\nbut decisive action only. 10 IS believed\nVIII do this, for the OverWrought partIci-\npants IN the movement viii not listen 10\nreason.\n\n\nComo Without Warming.\n\n\nHardly without warning the movement\nsDrang into being a short time azo at\nunion revival service held by several\nchurches Of the town. AT that time a\ndozen or more men and women who had\nbeen most active in the services arose\nfrom their seats and led by Lewis Noble.\na prosperous young farmer. declared that\nthe ministers who were leading tle se.-\nvices lacked IN holiness and had not me\nspirit Of the Lord\n\n\nAll efforts 10 quiet them for time\nwere unsuccessful as they continued 70\nprotest At last they we're quoted and\nafter the services that night declared\nthey would conduct meetings Of their own\nII is Noble who Is now in the insane\nasylum at Brighton. the county seat. nine\nmiles north OF here but undeterred by\nthis his followers are holding almost\nhourly meetings at their different homes.\n\n\nshouts and CoHyuIsions.\n\n\nThey have no particular programme for\nthe meetings. but so ecstatic do they be-\ncome that their shouts may De heard\nthroughout the ueighb0rhOod while IN\nprayer they fan 20 the floor. their bodies\nrigid and with consciousness almost .zone.\n\n\nThere was only Laughter when the meet\nings first began, but 20 this no attention\nwas paid by the enthusiasts The Lord\nviii direct us\" they said and from night\n70 night they wait for word fFOm Him be-\nfore deciding whose house to visit On\ntheir knees anu with their faces prone\nupon the floor they pray hour after hour.\n\n\nMobiles Hauueination.\n\n\n1t was when Noble determined to pre-\npare the residents OF the village for the\nend Of the worIi that the climax came.\nGoing from house to house. and without\nthe formality OF ringing the do0rbeH. he\ndashed in where families were az diner\nand told them:\n\n\nIl am the Holy Ghost Prepare your\nselves The end is at hand--'\n\n\nWithout waiting FOR reply. fastening 10\nprepare as many as he could according to\nhis delusiOn. he passed on from house 10\nhouse. followed BY a crowd OF hooting and\nieering youngsters\n\n\nBelieving that they were earnestly fol\n\n=== 29_1916-05-14_pna_sn85042344_00200295018_1916051401_1112.json\nTulsa daily world. [volume] (Tulsa, Indian Territory [Okla.]) 1905-1919 · 1916-05-14\ncrops: cached\nheadline: SPIRITUALIST.\n---\nFIRST PROGRESSIVE CHURCH\nwill hold services in X. p. hall at s p.\nm. Mrs Gertrude Hull will lecture and\ndeliver messages. Members and others\ninterested In lyceum work are spe-\ncially invited to meet in H. i. hall ut\n10 a. M, for the purpose Of organ\nizing lyceum. Election OF officers\nand enrolment OF scholars. Bring\nyour children. 'Iusic by Tuttle's or\nchestra.\n\n\nFIRST SPIRITUALIST SOCIETY Of\nTulsa meet Sunday evening at 8\no'clock, second floor Robinson build\ning. Mrs. Roberts, from Blackwell,\nwill conduct the meeting. Everybody\nwelcome.\n\n=== 41_1910-03-26_p1_sn84026820_0027176506A_1910032601_0269.json\nMiddletown transcript. · 1910-03-26\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\n\n\n\nrunning in\" bunch Of eeIs, which,\nfrom the manner in which our informant\nseparated his hands by way Of measure\nment. must have been Rbout five feet\nlong. Skin 'em a. If you loved 'em Chief.\n\n\nThat il costs more for merchant to\nbrush cob webs off his goods than it does\nto advertise.\n\n\nThe Sewing Crcle-S society composed\nOf some two OF three dozen OF our finest\nyoung ladies, and devoted to fun, investi.\ncations, of psychic and occult phenomena\ngenerally-heId very successful seance\non Monday evening Of this week. Their\nOuija board is 3 square shaped little tri.\npod upon which 10 supposed to sit the\ninvisible divining Pythoness \"Ouija\"\nwho, at prompsings from the eager finger\ntips OF the members, responds in some\nsort of q mental telepathic way to their\nqueries. II some of her answers are bit\nsnigmaGical we must remember that her\nGreek prototype rsVing from her bronze\nstool amid the fumes Of Delphi was also\ntrifle ambiguous at times. However, the\nresponses at this inject\" OF the spooks\nand the curious yonnG ladies we're many\nand spicy.\n\n\nThrough our Society reporters success.\nful keyhole interview We are able to give\nq few Of them.\n\n\nTo Miss c.: Yes, he likes blends, but not\nOF the peroxide brand. Have your Too\nbadI Then cut il off at once and wear\nwig till it grows out again. Hope he\nwon't find il out. II he does, get your\ndoctor to swear you we're threatened with\nincipient aIOpeCia,\" and did it too save\nyour lovely locks, ETC.\n\n\nTo Miss A.: No, he is not engaged in\nanything ct present but wearing his little\ncap on his of ear, and saunsering down\ntown with big cherOot in his mouth.\n\n\nTo Miss \": Did his breath smell iunny2\nPerhaps he'd been using oil of cloves for\nthe toOthsche. Don't judge him too\nharshly.\n\n\nTo Miss F.: Did be like she taste Of\nthose ruby Iips7 Well, just try him once\nmore.and see, you sweet doubling little\npusp.\n\n\nTo Miss s.: Yes, be has q bad case OF\ndyspepsia already, and fears masrimon'\niaI cuisine might finish him. \"Ouija\" ad.\nvises you to spend six months or year\nlin cooking school, and then for Wind\n/, up, lay yourself out in Q big dinner, in\nvisiug Pc and ma, aud dear old grandma\n\n\nand the rest-and Of course, Tom. Per.\nhaps this may give him q jolt in your\ndirection.\n\n\nII To Miss g.: No, be does not wear liver\npad and does not snore-at least not\n\n\nenough to disturb bis old grandad, who ia\nO. trifs deaf. But he has ''cold feet\"\nsometimes, though believe, be never\n, gets them in affairs of the heart.\n\n\nFred's got 9 bran new Lille Tube, \"\n! they say. For quite Q spell Fred has\n| been q student of ell sorts Of things cur\nj ious and scientific. Lately, though, be\n{ has been going in for \"Ozone.\" In an\n\n\ninsuspecting moment he read seductive\n\n\n\"Ad.\" that with lurid rhetoric set forth\n\n\nthe countless blessings flowing from the\n\n\nuse of the Wonderful Life Tube\" shat\n!. invigoratss the whole system physically\n3 intellectually, morally, financially, etc\n. He was 90 Tavished ct the prospect thai\n! he ordered one forthwith with foil diEec\n! lions \"How to get strong; double youl\nbrain power; get b foil head OF hair,\nj, etc.\n\n\nd Now, Fred's husky youth with fol\n!, supply Of hair, but, like the rest Of Us, h'\nr thought itwouId be fine thing to double\n2 bis quantum OF gray matter.\nWe never cav the gimcraCk, but 1t hat\nr been described to us as somewhere be\n4 tween four inches and fourteen feet long\nr Directions say ION retiring poke one enc\na of the Life Tube in your mouth and ruE\nd the other out the window and up toward\no the roof where the latest and best thinl\ni. ia Ozone IL on perpetual tap. Fred say\n\n\nIl works fne-likes it better than an\n\n\nliver pad be ever Wore-ean just feel th'\n\n\nOzone trickling down bis throat and run\n\n\nming out ct his toes-he thinks. Has los\n:, that tired feeling that comes over us al\n:\" at this time Of the year. Believes hi\n! corns are better, etc. BEST thing eve\n!. ssruCk-going TO WFite for the agency\nWell, success TO you, Fred.\nn P,S, Since penning the above We re\nD. eret 20 learn that FTed's Lille Tube\na. SomeboW got out Of whack-she ozon\n\n\nrot choksd 10 the Tube or sOmethinG\n= AnyhoW, his tired feelin's are back agai\nle and his cOFns ache same 90 ever. de ha\nliz thrown up che agsncy and sent \"the 81\n% fired thing\"-that's the way he talh\n= about il nov 'back whore e cam\nd, FROM\" WIFE,\n\n=== 4_1898-12-23_pna_sn84022770_00200291141_1898122301_0312.json\nWashington standard. [volume] (Olympia, Wash. Territory) 1860-1921 · 1898-12-23\ncrops: cached\nheadline: A Convincing Argument.\n---\nA correspondent sends us the follow\ning story from Mississippi: Counsel\nfor the defense was addressing a\ncountry J. z. from the \"old school,\"\nSaid he: al realize that | stand in\nthe presence of descendant of the\ngrand old Huguenot family that emil\ngrated from France to escape from re-\nligious intolerance. Many able jur-\nlists have sprung from that family\nand embellished the bench and bar OF\nthe Union. Their watchwords are\nhonor, truth and justice, and their\nnames are spoken in every home. The\nlaw is so plain in this case that he\nwho runs may read Shall | insult\nthe intelligence of this court by re-\niterating a proposition so simple?\nNeed / say more\" No.\" interrupted\nthe Judge, \"'tain't necessary. III\ngive you II judgment\" Counsel sat\ndown while judge, with emphasis,\nknocked the ashes from his cob pipe,\nand counsel for plaintiff began\nMAY it please the court\" Squire,\nwhat are you fxin' to do?\" asked the\nJudge. \"I have the closing argu-\nment,\" was the reply Well, you Jes'\nas well set down. done got my\nmind sot on the other side. Judg-\nment for defendant\"\n\n=== 2_1923-06-04_pna_sn87093407_00340585410_1923060401_0854.json\nThe Seattle star. [volume] (Seattle, Wash.) 1899-1947 · 1923-06-04\ncrops: cached\nheadline: SAYS AMERICA\n\n\nIs UNCRATEFUL\n\nDoyle Declares Spiritualism\n\n\nSaved This Country\n---\nSAN FRANCISCO June g.\n''Spiritualism saved America. yet to-\nday America SCOTS at spirituaiism.\nSir Arthur Conan Doyle told his\naudience at his lecture here last\nnight.\n\n\ns'sFrance worships the memory Of\nJoan OF Arc, the maiden OF Or.\nleans, whose spiritual inspiration\nsaved her country. America dir\ndams the name of Nettin CoIburn.\nthe girl medium. who in the dark\ndays of 1852 conveyed TO President\nAbraham Lincoln strengthening\nwords OF spiritual advice. thus bring\ning about what all historians agree\nWas the turning point Of the civil\nwar struggle\"\n\n\nThe name Of Daniel Webster was\nmentioned rs the guiding influence\nfrom ''the other side for Doyle\nsaid tlst mons other could have\nhad such exquisite expression ass\npoured from the lips OF the medium,\nNettie colburn.\"\n\n\nUnlike France\" Sir Arthur con-\nfinued, America disdains the name\nOf Nettle colburn. whose services\nwere not unlike those o. Joan Of\nArc. n Nettie were in San Fran.\ncisco she would. no dorbt, be in\nthe hands Of the police\"\n\n=== 53_1929-12-17_p1_sn85042243_00199918734_1929121701_0990.json\nThe Bismarck tribune. [volume] (Bismarck, N.D.) 1916-current · 1929-12-17\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Gangster's Haunts\nSecured for Tool\n\n\nIn Pay Of AL Capone\n---\nContinued from page one)\nfirst picked up In Detroit-then to\nchicago and the Capone gang. Since\nthen he Is said to have been the\nleader Of Z bank rohbing gang In\nMichigan. Iowa, Wisconsin and other\nstates.\n\n\nSearch 81. Joseph Home\n\n\n\"A telephone call from st. Joseph\nearly today sent police to the home Of\nFreeman Weise after operators had\nexcitedly reported o cryptic message\npertaining to the Burke case.\n\n\nThe message ran:\n\n\nCant get near the house. Every\nthing = q. 4. They are still taking\nfinger prints. Am stu building on\ngarage. This Is l.: connection with\nthe Burke case\"\n\n\nWeise, to whom the call was made.\nsaid his son. Freeman Jr.. with whom\n10 Lives. had gone TO st. Joseph when\nthey learned Of Burke's expose. Weise\nhad sold Burke the house IN which\nthe ''aSsena\" Of machine gums and\nrepeating rifles was found. and be-\ncame worried because Burke had gtv-\nOn him only $1,000 down On the\n4l4yeoo purchase. he said.\n\n\nBecause Of . letter found In the\npossession Of Burkeis common.law\nwife, Nrs. Daniels. addressed to Mr&.\np. Roeenwall. In haywood. the home\n&c Mn. HooenWall today Was under\nsurveillance. The woman Is thought\n- - Q sister Of Mrs. Daniels and\nn0Ai0a noid II Vna 4 possibility Burke\n\n=== 2_1902-12-23_pna_sn88056158_00415666329_1902122301_0405.json\nThe silver messenger. (Challis, Idaho) 1890-1912 · 1902-12-23\ncrops: cached\nheadline: LIFE's HARVEST.\n---\nThey are moving the meadows. now,\nand the whispering, signing\nSong of the scythe breathes sweet on\nmine idle ear\nSong of old summer dead. and of this\none dying\nRoses on roses fallen. and year on year.\nSoftly as swathes that sink while the\nlong scythe, swinging.\nPasses and pauses and sweeps through\n\n\nthe deep green grass:\nStrange how this song of the scythe sets\n\n\nthe old days singing\nEchoes of seasons gone, and of these\n\n\nthat pass.\nFair ghost of Youth-from your sea-fraga\nrant orchard-closes\nCalled by the voice of the scythe as it\n\n\nsigns and swings\nTell me now as you toss me your phan-\n\n\ntom roses,\n\n\nWhat was the dream you dreamed\n\n\nthrough those vagrant Springs?\nWhat that forgotten air when the heart\n\n\nwent maying\nWhat was the perfume blowing afar.\n\n\nanear?\nYouth Youth Youth\" - the Scythe\n\n\nkeeps signing and saying\n''The rose you saw not-the tune that\n\n\nyou could not hear\"\n-Rosamond Marriott Watson in Har-\npers.\n\n=== 32_1891-03-22_pna_sn83030214_00175038462_1891032201_0299.json\nNew-York tribune. [volume] (New York [N.Y.]) 1866-1924 · 1891-03-22\ncrops: None\nheadline: THE NEW SOCIAL CLUB.\n\nWHAT THE PROJECTORS EXPECT\n\nNOT A RIVAL OF OTHER ORGANIZATIONS, BUT\nsoMETHING TO FILL NEW SPHERE.\n---\ngo have been accepted. As the invitations have\nh.en issued only few days. and additional accep-\nsnoes are constantly received by the General Com\n\n\n,tee there he question that the limit Of mem\nnsb,n Will be reached at the outset The projectors\n- the new Organization are much hattered over the\n\n\nosnects Of the club, indicated by the promptness\nnth which the invitations have been accepted, and\n\n\nh,,e there never any doubt of the Of the\nnterprise has been made douby sure by the\n,ordialty which all those interested have manifested\n\n\nThe projectors, who are among the strongest men in\nbs city, socially and financially, contend that the new\n\n\nnh not intended to be antagOniStic to of the\n.,her organizations. One OF Them yesterday,\n,errinc 10 the apparent understanding among other\nenbs that the organization would gain strength by\n\n\nsakenng them said that there need be no that\nscore as the club would stand in a field peculiarly\nits own\n\n\nsaid he: \"The Knickerbocker Club is the only purely\n\n\nsocial dub in the city which in any cmbodies the\nnrinclIes governing exclusive society, and its mem\n,.xhip is so circumscribed that it embraces only\n\n\n.,t d the representation TO which society is entitled\nn too exclusive to be sufficiently representative of\nsocle.ys component parts. The Union Club, once the\nrrea social arbiter of New York. has become practically\na cnb of Wall Street and fnanciers generally,\nand It's social position while still good one has\nchanged materially. 1t can no longer be said that\nmembership in the Union Club is equivalent to ad-\nmission TO all Of the best houses. Even its strongest\n\n\nand oldest members at least of them, feel that\nn does not meet the social requirements as 1t dld\nformerly.\n\n\nMen who have retired from business seem anxious\nn escape from business cares, at second hand\nand are more eager for social relaxation and diversion\nthan for a mere resort at which to annihilate time\nsuch men are not satisfied with surroundings and as\nsocations which remind them Of their former business\nresponsibilities where the conversations Is all ABOUT\nbusiness affairs and where the diversions are III\nterrupted by the constant absorption OF a large enc\nment in affairs OF the day. They are men VINO have\nearned the right diversions, and their club Is the\nmedium through which they expect to obtain them\nThen there are many Of high social position who\nare still in business bat who wish to take an actNe\nmart in society for the sake of their iamcs OF lor INC\nMeasure they themselves enjoy. such men generally\nlike TO leave business cares behind them when they\nleave their offices for the day. At their club they\nlook for diversion, and it is not pleasant to ma thec\nANYTHING that will remind them OF the Office To say\nu MAINLY there is not much of anything but shop\ntalk at the Union Club any more. II Is purely ana\nsimply Wall street club, and in the ios Oh ii'aioc\nwhich has envelOped it the social functions \" inc OF\nganization have been lost.\n\n\nAlmost identical observations could be made con\ncorning the other large clubs, all of which, no doubt\nhave strong social element distnctively their own\nbut which are sustained chiefly, rather enveloped\nby principles distinct from social considerations Inc\nInon League Club contains many men whose Posl'lohN\nin society are of the best, but politics Is inc rs\nprinciple there Just strongly as business Is III u\"\nUnion Club. Society is composed of men OF all parties\nnotcaly and the social standing OF their laihlh'S\ncannot be gagged by their membership III cruz\nfounded on political principles. Then again. there Is\nanother analogy between the business and the pouca\nclnb which shows them to be equally HhemcIchV \"9\nsocial organizations. The fundamental pFlhclpe III\none is developed it is in the other to the eAcuSoh\nof the first consideration of social clb e. sooicS\n\n\nman in search of purely social diversion does not hc\n\n\nhut\nmore than he likes be cnvcloped III an hoSpeic \"\nbusiness. - t.,\n\n\n.rhe Manhattan Club, like the Union League IS\nsustained by political foundation, ana ike H. 700 'o\nsocial consideration is secondary, II not ost Ss' 20\naltogether There other clubs like these each WIN\nits special function, but in none OF WHICH, eAoeP\"\"e\ntho Knickerbocker is the social feature predominant\nTHE new club is intended TO occupy the vacant Spa\naround which all the other clubs are formed anu ''\nfoundation will be absolutely and entirety soc'ai -\"\nother clubs each may maintain It's dlsthcuNe s'\"\"u\ninc IN, the new one will embrace the social strength\nof all. 49,, E. ,ooor.\n\n\n..when the new club project was started II oeoaln0\nevident that it would draw considerable extent LOT\nits membership upon the Union CLUB, III WHO\" 'ii'\nsoraI element is stronger tnan IN inc o'ioi'' ar\" \"\nwhich for a number OF years theYe Has u0o\" \" ~\nless vigorous strife representing what HIeht oo\nthe p,Iely social and active business ldc\"ohS'\n\n\nhohi,d This strife there is deep social principle lho\nold\n,n, . h,ou,n a. exclusive society. or the 400 III\nhas NAMED exclusive society to be compelled - ic\"\nthat in England and on the Continent IL was i0s\nAs .cornmercialzed aristocracy that Is' Wv 80.\nwhich draws itself apart because OF ule NdsV ~\n-. members have acquired in commerce. Inc 'ofc's\"\ndasignatnn of Us may be true enough DuI soo'e| ~\nsents il. because it intimaies secondary so'i\" PO\n- 49, cou,narcd with the higher society dDrodo\nperb,ns this resentment may be que 10 u'o i'\nthat a large element here regards lsell d.\n20 r