base OF a site and ior lc va t,n\n\n=== 27_1921-12-03_p11_sn83045462_00280657037_1921120301_0559.json\nEvening star. · 1921-12-03\ncrops: None\nheadline: PULPIT AND PEW\n---\nkeeping Fit\" is the subject to be\ndiscussed at the Fifteenth street\nPresbyterian Church tomorrow eveIl-\ning at 6:15 o'clock, under the aus\npices of the Mems Progressive Club.\nAll men are invited to attend.\n\n\nRev. Mrs. G. r. King of Frankford,\nPa. woman evangelist, Is to conduct\na ten-day revival at the Mckinley Me-\nmortal Baptist Church. 4th and L\nstreets northwest, beginning tomor-\nrow night at 8 o'clock. Rev. s. q.\nLamkins is pastor of the church.\n\n\nr y y\n\n\nPaul r. Russell is to teach the Fox\nall class of Foundry NJ. E. Church to-\nmorrow morning at $3.50 o'clock. The\nclass will attend the preaching serv-\nice at IL o'clock, in a body.\nThe quartet of the Western Presby-\nterian Church will give a musical to-\nmorrow evening at o'clock. The\nspecial feature will be the singing of\nold gospel hymns. The pastor, Rev.\nj.. H. Dunham will speak on \"The\nAltar and the Choir\"\n\n\nX * 4\n\n\nAt thank-offering service held in\nthe Luther Place Memorial Church\nlast Sunday evening, pageant rep\nreSenting the Bible story of the Feast\nof First Fruits\" was given by the\nWomen's Home and Foreign Mission\nsioSary Society, under the direction of\nMiss Bess Schreiner. Mrs. Hazen Pot\nter Buehler sang \"A Song of Thanks\ngiving\" by Allitsen.\n\n\ny y\n\n\nDR.. E. S. Shumaker, superintendent\nof the Apti-Saloon League of Indiana.\nwill speak at Hamline NJ. E. Church\ntomorrow at IL o'clock. At the 8\no'clock services Rev. H. p. Fox. the\npastor, will make reply to Thou\nsands Now Living Shall Never Die\n\n\n\\ X\n\n\nNot as the Scribes\" will be the\nsubject of the sermon by Rev. Ulysses\nG. B. Pierce. minister of All Souls\nChurch, tomorrow morning in the\nKnickerbocker Theater. Charles\nTrowbridge Tittmann, bass. will be\nassisted by David Nemser, violin,\nwith Lewis Corning Atwater at the\nOrgan.\n\n\nWilliam c. Lee will address the\nLiberal Religious Union tomorrow\nevening at s o'clock on '\"The New\nThought Movement of Mental Heal\ning\" The meeting will be held in\nUnitarian House. 1889 Columbia road.\nRev. DIR. Randolph will preach to-\nmorrow at Foundry Church at IL\no'clock on '\"The Story of a Haunted\nHouse.\" At s pm. Wayne B. Wheeler\nattorney for the Anti-Saloon League\nof America, will speak on The En-\ning at 7:15 Y. F. Tsao. assistant secre-\ntary general, Chinese delegation. will\nspeak.\n\n\n** * X\n\n\nRev. Dr. Wallace Radcliffe will\npreach tomorrow morning at New\nYork Avenue Presbyterian Church at\nIL o'clock. Col. William MacIntyre\nof Boston. Mass. will speak of the\nSalvation Army at the regular even\ning service at o'clock. He is in\ncharge of the work of the New Eng\nland district and is in attendance\nupon the national field congress now\nIn session in this city.\n\n\nAt the Church of Life and Joy,\n1914 n street northwest. Rev. A. B.\nCampbell-Shields of New York will\ndeliver an address tomorrow at 8\npm. on Freedom Through Sonship.\"\nThe healing service at IL am. will\nbe conducted by Mrs. Josephine Pres\nton. Denver Colo. Soloist for even\nIng, Miss Lenore Gerlach.\n\n\nX y + X\n\n\nVesper services are to be held at\nthe Y. w. c. A. 1888 F street to-\nmorrow afternoon at 4:39 o'clock,\n\n\n\n\n\nFellowship Pageant\"\n\n\nX r X\n\"A Charming Company\" is Rev E.\nHez Swem's subject tomorrow night,\nand \"Man-dust.\" in the morning at\nthe Centennial Baptist Church\n\n\nA party of six singers and mission\nworkers from Philadelphia will be\nat the Central Union Mission tonight\nand tomorrow at and 7:45 pm The\nparty is composed of j. Hatton and\nMiss Anna Hatton. gospel singers\nMrs. Ida Shivers Leech and Jack\nAbnett, hymn writer's and singers:\nMrs. J. A. Gore. a missionary among\ngirls, and John r. Mcintyre. superin-\ntendent of the Whosoever Gospel Mis-\nsion. at Germantown, Pa. These same\nspcafers and singers will take part\nIn the services at the Rhode Island\nAvenue Methodist Protestant Church\ntomorrow morning at IL o'clock.\n\n\nA service and prayer of spiritual\nhealing will be held every Tuesday\nnight at s o'clock at Epiphany\nChurch.\n\n\nIn the remodeling of the building\nIn connection with the erection of\nthe Mckim memorial tower the south\ngallery of Epiphany Church is being\nenlarged to accommodate from sew\nenty-ive to one hundred additional\npeople. 1t is expected 1t will be ready\nfor use within the next two weeks.\n\n\nX\n\n\nRev. James F. Freeman, rector, will\npreach at the IL am. and pm. serv-\nices at Epiphany Church tomorrow\nRev. Robert E. Browning will preach\nat the 4 pm. service.\n\n\nX\n\n\nRev. DR. George A. Miller, pastor\nOf the Ninth StTe~t Christian Church.\nwill Gtve . iectere on THE Funda-\nmental Doctrines of the Christian\nChurch\" befote the class on doc\ntriness At tae School of Religious\nEa1% Of ss NJ schaai Al At oas Gal\nMonday evening This class is open\nto the public without registration.\nAt the Temple Baptist Church to-\nmorrow morning Rev. DIR. Muir will\nhavs ~ his subjec> THE Peak of\nPrayer\" At the evening service Col.\nAlexander Damon OF the Salvation\narmy In war and peace times.\n\n\nAt thu Emmanuel Baptist Church\nRev. Cow G. johnson breaches tomor-\nrow morning aad tho Lords Supper\nwill be observed w1Ih welcome to new\nmembers. At night Csol Margaret BovilI\nOf the Salvas on Army. speaks and\n\n\nsPIRITUALIsT\n\n\nREV. G. IyAL ARTHAN\nciety Hail. 50s st. NV Short iec.ure on\n\"spirituslIsu 40 Il Is.\" fo.lowsd sy\nspirit. spirit m.ss.ss. .s., Tuesday evening :, =\n. IL\n\n=== 12_1884-07-03_pna_sn85033078_00271769477_1884070301_0629.json\nWood County reporter. [volume] (Grand Rapids [i.e. Wisconsin Rapids], Wis.) 1857-1923 · 1884-07-03\ncrops: None\nheadline: a Methodist Ghost Story.\n---\nOne secret Of the power which at\ntends the simple ministrations of the\nMethodist preachers of the primitive\ntype was that spiritual and unseen\nthings were to them what they are tru-\nII, more real than the objects of mere\nsense. The training of some of these\nmen was favorable to this An illus\ntratiOn was once given by an old man\nwith whom l used to chat in the neigh\nbOrhoCd of BOscastIe. One evening,\nas we sat by the lireside, | referred to\nthe old Minister Church not far of, in q\nromantic valley, and spoke of the curl\nGus, CHASTITY legends that foated about\nit. Yes, SIR\" said he, ''queer things\nhave been said, and queer things have\nbeen met with, say what we may.\nThere was one John Warden, sir-yes,\nJohn Warden that afterwards got to be\n4 preacher. John Warden, sir, was q\nfarmers boy-a labOrer, poor fellow.\nThe Lord touched his heart, sir. and\nJohn Warden turned Methodist Well,\nsir, his master and all about him turn\ned upon him, and the poor boy had to\nbear all sorts of persecution. One\nnight, rs they were all sitting around\nthe fire in the kitchen, and many ways\nhad been tried to put poor John out of\ntemper, the master said ut last: 'Relie-\nion, ehn said he. What kind of relio-\nion hast thee got, John, Ell? Why,\nIncest be afraid to 90 down to the\nchurch tonight at - o clock Religion,\neh! This was a sort of challenge,\nsir, to try whether John was good\nenough TO be above fear Poor John,\nsir, in the simplicity of his heart,\nthought the credit Of his religion was\nreally ut stake, and screwing up his\ncourage, he said quietly: No, [ bean't\nafraid to go to the church by night or\nby day WIN thee go tonight, then,\nand let's see what your religion is\nmade OPP Yes, | will They waited\ntill midnight, and John started, sir.\nThe farm was not far up from the val\nley. The question was now they should\nknow he had been TO the church. A\nplan was hit upon. He was to take\nlarge spike nail and u hammer and to\ndrive the nail into the church door.\nJohn went off with his hammer, nail,\nand \\ lantern; and he told me after\nward, years afterward when he was a\npreacher-yes, sir, he never forgot it\n\n\nAS L WCHG gov IL iluOJI1 Tic trees, says\nJohn, and the wind moaned, [ felt\nlittle queer, but [ got to the church all\nright, though just as [ was going into\nthe church the wind came whistling\nround the church, and out went my\nlight. declare l then wished myself\naway from the place. 1t was pitchy\ndark. [ asked God TO help me. and /\nthen felt my way to the door, put my\nnail to it and gave the first blow. [ felt\nq tingling all over me as the echoes\nwent round inside the church. They\nsounded hollow. But l picked up cour-\nage and hammered away until the nail\nwas long way in. Then turned and\nCROPPED my way back to the farm.\nThey laughed at me. and said they\nwould go down in the morning to be\nsure | had been there. They went\ndown early, and there was the nail.\nBut when they opened the door to see\nwhether it had went through, oh, how\nthey stared at one another! There was\nthe nail turned back, turned in and\ncIenched to the door as if the cleverest\ncarpenter in the world had done it. F\"\nSunday Magazine.\n\n=== 5_1845-06-18_p1_sn85026593_00202198065_1845061801_0201.json\nYpsilanti sentinel. [volume] (Ypsilanti, Washtenaw Co., Mich.) 1843-1900 · 1845-06-18\ncrops: None\nheadline: NUMBER 81.\n---\none foot mideg deep IN puddle OF water. Inc\nDoctor was positive that should take cold 10 con-\nsequence Of it. and won wager of him by not\ncoming off hoarse the next morning, 10 his utter\nestablishment.\n\n\nBut this unfeeling limb Of mine, has sometimes\nbrought me into awkward scrapes. never shall\nforget how supremely foolish fell one evening,\nwhen had been ridiculing most numerically =\ncertain foppsh' coEcei,ed fellow about TO ~n in\nthe presence of his two sisters, whom did not\nknnw to be such, and never imagined that Walter,\nnv friend, who sat mex, to me, would let me run\non in such strain without apprising me of the\nblunder was committing. why you incompre-\nstole fellow said he to me as soon we came a.\nway, 'what tine vengeance possessed you to keep\non talking in that style, ,chen 1 aods treading on\nyour toes ercry instant to make you stop\"\nOnce indeed came very near being detected.\nand the artifice by which escaped had the stran.\ngest effect Who would believe that the ghost Of\nwooden leg would break up match? Or that\nby saving my limb, shonId maka Beau Babbleton\nlose his mistress? | will tell the whole story, for\nfor it is seldom wooden leg has kicked up snch\ndust.\n\n\nOne very dark evening was walking home\nward through street where the sidewalk was\nsomewhat narrow, and the cellar entrances projec-\nted quite out to the curbstone. One of these hap\npened to be covered with wooden grating, and in\nwalking over it, trod in the dark upon defec-\ntive part, and my foot broke through. 1t was my\nwooden leg, and in my endeavors 10 extricate it,\ntie unfortunate limb broke loose and fell into the\ncellar. Here was terribly awkward position for\nfine gentleman to be 10. There was no getting\nmy leg out of its li,nbo in the dark, and to apply\n! for help would discover me. Luckily had my\nwalking stick and with the help of that made shift\nto hobbIe to my lodgings, where arrived undis-\ncovered, thanks 10 the darkness and latenesss Of\nthe hour. But the difficulty was not over. | had\nlost my leg, and no one could make me such anoth.\nerr or, if it could be replaced, the thug could not\nbe effected without delay ofmany days, and the\nstory would infaibly get wind. What should\ndot knew the house into which my stray limb\nhad slept, bu. was not on the right terms with the\noccupant to trust him with the secret. This was\nthe most unucky circumstance of a!l-he was\nJoe Clackaboui, person with whom had quar-\n! rel, and who was moreover an arrant busybody.\n\n\nIn short. he was no berson to enlrust with the Sc\ncret of wooden leg\n\n\n| At last thought struck me of method to get nv\nleg and save nv credit, for saw plainly that my\nleg must be had Immediately, or else The cat must\nbe let out of the bag. thought of Beau Babble>\nton, the foppish fellow mentioned above, as per.\nsonage on whom [ might tn some conscience.\nplay the trick of falherIng my lost limb had got\nmyself into bad odour with his sisters and two or\nthree score oftheir gosping female acquaintance.\n| bv meaus Of his foppery and the insensbility OF\nmy timber toes, and determined now 10 be revcn-\nged on him by means of the same inlractabe MEM\nDer. The plan was this--lo send by trusty SER\nwant, note to Mr.. CIackabout, requesting the leg\nin the name of Beau Babbleton: by which means\nshould get my limb again without being suspect.\ned, and Beau might account for The superiority\n| of shinhnnes in HE own animal acanomy ELI\nas he was able. ....sa,\n\n\nThe plan succeeded to admiration; and much\nbetter than looked for. had the luck to see\ndarky passing under my window next morning,\nand him sent off with the note: You must i'm\nagine the surprise of MIR. and Mrs. Clackabout\nand his two sisters, they sa: at breakfast, when\nthey received the following.\n\"Mr. Babbleton's compliments to Mr.. Clacka.\nbout, and requests that he will have tae goodness\nto despatch him his leg by the bearer; il will be\nfound in the cellar. Mr.. B hopes to be excused\nfor stepping through Mr., C's cellar door last eve\nming\"\nNobody knew what to make of this strange\nepistle at first. One thought it hoax, but on\nsending into the cellar to examine, the leg was\nfound sure enough. and the breach in the cellar\ndoor where the mis-step had been made. And\nthen what staring and wondering there was a.\nmong the Ctackabouts at the discovery of Beau\nBebbleton's artiice. who would have thought\nlhr-they all exclaimed; \"A tip top dandy\nbuck of the first water An irresistible creature\namong The ladies' and yet doing all this with a\nwooden leg Y OH! Monsirous y!\n\n\nHowever after pretty close scrutiny Of the I'M\nfortunate limb, it was delivered TO Cufy, who\npassed for Beau Babble,ons servant. as no ques-\ntons were asked, and my stray appendage brought\nme without any discovery being made. NowI\nwas Richard myself again; 5u; Beau Babbleton\nwas absolutely beside himself Mr.. Clackabout\nCHANGED to meet him the same forenoon. and wish\nhim Joy on the recovery of his leg. Bean repll-\ned that his leg had never been i'll. as\n\n\nNot i'll to be sure, but terribly out of Joni said\n&. Out Of joint, out of joint. Sir! What do you\nmean 2 =.\noh, don't mean II was hurt in breaking through\nthe cellar door; indeed believe it was sent back\nin good order: truly you walk very well with il.\nOne would never suspect you as.,\n\n\n'Never suspect me- don't Understand you,\nPray. Sir. what do you suspect me of?\n\n\nof getting the boot on the wrong foot; for you\n\n=== 28_1909-10-09_p1_sn83045462_00280656264_1909100901_0062.json\nEvening star. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1854-1972 · 1909-10-09\ncrops: None\nheadline: Mass Of Holy Ghost.\n---\nRev. Father Eugene del. Mcdonnell.\nS. j.. pastor of st. Aloysius Catholic\nChurch. will be the celebrant of the SOL\nemn votive mass Of the Holy Ghost for\nthe opening OF the schools Of Georgetown\nUniversity. in the Dahlgren Chapel OF\nthe Sacred Heart. Georgetown College\ncampus, tomorrow morning at IL O'Clock.\nFather Donlon. 8. j.. Of Woodstock Col\nlege. Of Maryland. will be the deacon and\nFather McNeai. 8. j.. Of Georgetown Col\nlege, subdeacon. Father Stanton. 8. j..\nof Newtown. Md, Will deliver the sermon\nof the occasion.\n\n=== 15_1922-06-07_p6_sn99021999_00279551254_1922060701_0068.json\nOmaha daily bee. · 1922-06-07\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nThe supreme court declines to bother will\nouija boards thereby getting in line with vas\nnumber of common people.\n\n=== 2_1906-01-03_pna_sn85042303_00237281317_1906010301_0823.json\nThe Daily Ardmoreite. [volume] (Ardmore, Okla.) 1893-current · 1906-01-03\ncrops: None\nheadline: spiritualism.\n---\nDespite the inclemency Of tho\nweather Sunday afternoon, a small\naudience was present at the city hall,\nto hear a lecture by Rev. Alice Ba-\nkor. 81 minister Of Modern Spiritual\nism. The meeting was opened by\nthe reading of short poem Friends\nIn Paradise\"\n\n\nMrs. Baker then delivered a lecture\non \"The Voice of the Soul\"\n\n\nThough it was intended that last\nSunday afternoon would conclude the\nspirtualist services In Ardmore. for\nthe present, Mrs. Baker and her son,\nhave decided to hold another meeting\nnext Sunday afternoon, after which\narrangements will be made for Mrs.\nBaker to return to Ardmore at least,\nonce a month in the interest OF the\nSpiritualist religion.\n\n=== 71_1874-05-09_p2_sn85052141_00202195192_1874050901_0041.json\nThe Hickman courier. · 1874-05-09\ncrops: None\nheadline: lN,S GHOST.\n---\nPuB' { Even with Her\n\n\nyer\n\n\ng Q sEliza Goodwin, who\n\n\niht before the courts\n=. writ de lu1otico\n\n\ndischarged. developed\n\n\nstances which not only\n~, ons of the novelist and\n~ an only find their paral-\n\n\nnv and grim Nemesis of\n80 ology. Miss Eliza God\nser OF respectable fami-\nTT icated and cultivated lady,\n\n\nmere schoolgirl, twenty\n= ago. made the acquaintance\n\n\nIN Throckmorton, an accom-\n4 ad fascinating man of the\nan twenty years her senior.\n\n\nsnce upon her amounted to an\nOn which she could not control.\nq confidIng and inexperienced,\nR st schoolgirls of idealistic ten\n\n\n;, and she very soon engaged her.\n\n\nmarriage to him. When he had\nID this advantage, he ruined and\n\n\nabandoned her, but the shadow of\n\n\niarkness into which she fell envel-\n\n\n| him also, and it has never left him\nTer, and never will, although he is\nwing gray with years and Passing\n\n\nyears of passion, and she is a faded,\ncary woman, out of whose life hope\nas disappeared, and for whom remains\nonly the passive obedience to fate.\nWhen she found herself abandoned she\ndid not throw herself @7sto the slums.\nShe did not break under the calamity\nwhich wrecks so many lives. She did\nr. not hurry herself out of the world.\nThe disaster revealed her to herself,\nbe , and all the aimless and restless ele-\nments of her life crystalized into one\nn Settled pupose, which, for over twenty\n. been EARS, has absorbed every other par\nuis, has Ee, hope, thought and energy of her\n.eAl-- lv~9lx L.\n\n=== 32_1897-10-13_p12_sn85066387_00175037615_1897101301_0203.json\nThe San Francisco call. · 1897-10-13\ncrops: None\nheadline: EARLE COT\n\n\nMONEY FOR\n\n\nMESSAGES\nCoined Money From the\nOccult Warblipgs From\n\n\nthe Skies.\nMEDIUMS DEVELOPED TO ORDER.\nCharles Webber Was Made to\nBelieve That Spirits Hovered\n\n\nGer Him.\nSTATES BRING kL0NDIkE PRICES.\nHow Spurious Medium Cultivated a\nHarvest Rolf and Departed\n\n\nWith Gold.\n\nSTATES BRING kLOIDIKE PRICES\n\nHow Spurious Medium Cultivated a\nHarvest Rolf and Departed\n\n\nWith Gold\n---\nIf all reports in spiritualstic circles are\ntrue Edward K. Earle, independent\n","numLines":1604,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1604}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c0394ae4-85c9-45b7-ab46-49013aa87d70","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"other"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2c-reader","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
