OF\nthe neighbourhood, of unquestioned indifference to\nghosts called in, who, having again opened the\ncofln and ascertained that the corpse was actually\ndead, resolved toexamine the grave, in which was\nfound an intoxicated man who had accidentally found\nbed there. He was discovered to be young man\nwho, crossing the burying yard, had evidently fallen\ninto the grave, from whence his nebratioD had pre-\nvented his ex,ricatng himself, and who had thus\nrun the horrid risk of being buried alive without\nsuspicion OF what had occurred to him.\n\n\nFrench paper.\n\n=== 9_1899-05-31_pna_sn84036012_0021247728A_1899053101_0842.json\nThe Anaconda standard. [volume] (Anaconda, Mont.) 1889-1970 · 1899-05-31\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Ghosts That Ride\n\n\nWith Custer.\n---\nWilliam Stokes, in Army and Navy Jour\nnal.\n/ was with Reno, fightin' hard an pressed\n\n\non ev'ry side,\nTill Terry came, an then we Harmed how\n\n\nClusters men had died;\nAn hastenin' down we saw 'em dead\n\n\nwide eyes an faces gray,\nAn tibet red scene will haunt me, sure,\n\n\nuntil my dyin day.\nBut you ken see no ghosts, not you-you\n\n\nhe'd no comrade thar,\nWhile / he'd Clark o. troop, little Joe,\n\n\nwith sunny hair\nAn girlish face. an eyes an lips aye full\n\n\nO. smiles an thanks.\nAn l found him near by Custer whar he\n\n\ndropped down in the ranks\nThe place is thick with dusky shapes an\n\n\nvisions all around,\nWith gun smoke trailin' up the hills, dark\n\n\nsplashes on the ground,\nAr airy mutterin's in the wind, an voices\n\n\nlike as not\nO. those who ride by Custer's side about\n\n\nthe bloody spot\nAn on they march, each wind-shod troop,\n\n\nthe purple midnight through,\nii ii ~,\n\n\nin review,\nWith sabers drawn an misty banners\n\n\nwavin' over all,\nAn moanin' up'ard to the stars a desolate\n\n\nbugle call.\nThe phantom sounds g battle float along\n\n\nthe peopled air,\nMuffied commands, an shoutin', an des-\n\n\npirate, distant cheer,\nAn shudderin' steeds, an saber CLEANS,\n\n\nan pistol echoes, too,\nAn'-God o mercyi-down'ard rains a\nCHASTITY, crimson dew.\nWhen lightnin spreads O. summer nights,\nits pennons on the breeze,\nWhen winters icy squadrons clatter thru\nthe quakin' trees,\nBut most O. all in June-in June, when the\n| blood-red roses blow,\n| The troopers ride by Clusters side whar\nthe Big Horn waters flow.\n\n=== 10_1920-06-29_p1_sn84027621_00295865647_1920062901_0645.json\nThe Ocala evening star. [volume] (Ocala, Fla.) 1895-1943 · 1920-06-29\ncrops: cached\nheadline: ON THE OUIJA LINE\n---\nBreathlessly the spiritualistically\ninclined lady bent over the ouija\nspelling out the communications from\nher departed spouse.\n\n\nJohn, are you happy there\" she\nasked.\n\n\nEyes, dear\"\n\n\ncare you happier than you were en-\nthe earth ?\"\n\n\neyes, dear\"\n\n\n\"Ah,\" she breathed. \"Heaven must\nbe a wonderful place.\"\n\n\n81 guess so. but Pm not there yet\"\nAmerican Legion Weekly.\n\n=== 18_1907-07-13_pna_sn85042462_00175047463_1907071301_0176.json\nLos Angeles herald. [microfilm reel] (Los Angeles [Calif.]) 1900-1911 · 1907-07-13\ncrops: cached\nheadline: SPIRITUALIST EL0PES WITH\n\n\nM0THER-IN-LAW BECAUSE\n\n\nWIFE DISLIKES SEANCES\n\nBY Associated Press.\n---\nBY Associated Press.\n\n\ni VIENNA, July IA spiritual !\ni list named Banninger, living in =\n!. Budapest, eloped with his mother =\ni in-law, the wife of land owner. =\ni. Banninger was dissatisfied with =\n!. his wife because she was not in =\n!. terested in his seances. He found =\n!. his mother-in-law devoted to spir- i\n!. itualism. They came to Vienna =\ni preparatory to starting for the =\nj. United States. While here they !\n]. were robbed of all their belong !\n!. ings, and they returned to Buda- =\n\\ pest Deutsch and his daughter =\ni are both suing for divorce. =\n4 !. !. i. !. 4 !. !. J. j. !. J. j. ]. i j !. = .\n\n=== 13_1887-12-24_p7_sn83025510_00340585689_1887122401_0341.json\nThe Labor enquirer. · 1887-12-24\ncrops: cached\nheadline: spiritualism at Chamber Of Commerce\n---\nDR.. E. o. Matthews. the cele-\nbrated trance speaker and test\nmedium of Albany, New York,\nwill again hold religious services\nat Chamber of Commerce under\nthe auspices of the progressive\nspiritualists of Denver. on Sunday,\nDecember IS, at2:3o and 7:3o pm.\nAfter each lecture DR.. Matthews\nwill give descriptions of the So\ncalled departed spirit friends that\n\n\nmay be present. A small fee\nwill be charged at the door to pay\nexpenses. All invited. 1t\n\n=== 18_1901-05-06_p1_sn89053684_00414182331_1901050601_0069.json\nThe Savannah morning news. · 1901-05-06\ncrops: cached\nheadline: FISH THAT CLIMB STAIRS.\n\nIn Travelling to summer Haunts\n\n\nThey Display Acrobatic Ability.\n---\nIL Is not an uncommon thing TO see on\npleasant days, when the FISH are running\nat East Taunton, hundreds OF men. wo-\nmen and children clustered around the\nhshway watching the Ash struggling up\nagainst the strong current and trying to\nget into the smoother waters above the\ndam. where they may shoot off al leisure\nto the spawning place In the Nemasket.\n\n\nThe fishway L. 80 constructed that Il a.\nimpossible for the fish to make a clear\nswim from top TO bottom or vice versa\n\n\nThey must work up the river In the\neddies, for the tide Is also very strong\nthere near the dam and until they reach\nthe lower part OF the fshway. Thence\nthey struggle and wriggle into the lower\n\n=== 10_1897-05-28_p1_sn86076999_00271740918_1897052801_0497.json\nMorning appeal. [volume] (Carson City, Nev.) 1877-1906 · 1897-05-28\ncrops: cached\nheadline: a Reno Spook.\n---\nThe Reno Journal is responsible for\nthe following:\n\n\nFor three nights past the officers\nhave been watching a peculiar phe-\nnomenon north of the track and are\nmystified at what has been observed\nOfficers Leeper and Logan ATTEMPTED\nlast evening 70 capture what appeared\nTO them an uncanny shape in human\nform with a peculiar phosphorescent\nlight surrounding it, and with this in\nview took positions some distance\napart and waited. Suddenly the an\npartition, or Whatever it is,\nand 10Th made a rush, but the object\nseemed to tread on air for a\no then vanished, leavingthe officers\ngazing at each other under the LIGHT\nOF their dark lanterns. A second at\ntempt a made with a like result.\n\n\nThe officers are disiUclined to name\nthe locality as they do not desire TO\nbrighten the residents, and for the\nsame reason did not care to fire anv\nshots. But they nevertheless insist\nthat there is something peculiar going\non as,the object has been seen on sew\neral nights between II 80 and - 80 in\nthe same neighborhood. Develop\nments or a sensational character NAV\nbe expected, but whether a genuine\nghost or some practical joker is work\ning the cops\" remains to be seen.\n\n=== 9_1960-01-20_p14_sn83045462_00280608300_1960012001_0348.json\nEvening star. · 1960-01-20\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\n| holdings, blanketed by the lava,\nat So million. He owns 80 acres\nof sugar cane land and the\nWarm Springs resort area, now\nburied by lava.\n\n\nNothing is left in Kapoho but\nempty buildings and material\nthat could not be moved. It's\na ghost town waiting to die.\n\n\nThe villagers were evacuated\ndays ago.\n\n\nSeismologist Jerry P. Eaton\nsaid there is enough lava under\nground to feed the eruption for\ntwo months.\n\n\nA Coast Guard lighthouse at\nCape Kumukahi and a housing\ndevelopment on the cape could\nbe hit, Mr.. Eaton said, if the\nflow continues.\n\n=== 7_1844-09-28_p1_sn82016488_00513688040_1844092801_0313.json\nArkansas intelligencer. · 1844-09-28\ncrops: cached\nheadline: HON. A. II. SEWER.\n---\nNh. EDITOR In my preceding number, | stat\ned the objects. and purposes. ~hcn pro onpted tn.\ninvestigation. into the ,e of tn, bonlS by Sew Ter\nand \" illiamsOn, as well as the nner in which\nthat investigation was conducted bv the Bauk\nCommittee. | now propose to show in this, AMI\nmy succeeding numbers lhat the report, as made.\ncontains garbed statement of facts; that tht. in\nferences and conclu Iions drawn, are gratuiiols:\nand that tho charges made both directly AM midi.\nrectly, are ungenerons. unfounled. an1 unjust.\n\n\nThe rep .rt after stating the sle Of the bonds\nand the conversion of tho proceeds into Southern\nand Western funds. thus proceeds:\n\n\nThe fifth branch of inquiry is, whether saL\ncommissioners had any power or authority invest\ned IN th'm, by tho ngancy. to make such invest\nments! Your comrnlte are of opinion. from !,\ns # te n q IT,\nCentral Board, upon winch the instructions are\npredicaed. that no power Authority, thus to dis\npose Of the Banks capital. could b. derived from\nany Just constructions \"ftIi. powers conterred but\non tho contrary ire necessarily iorrel to the con\ncluson that the exercise Of such power was d.\nrect and palpable violation both of the spirit nnl\nletter Of the powers conferred. by sne -resoulnn\nOf the Central Board, and the subsequent instruc-\ntions Of the committee\"\n\n\nThis paragraph contains serious charge\nagainst the commissioners and upon It several\nothers. in the report are based. Is it true 1t\ncannot he taken for granted but should be suS-\ntained by evidence. Then. where is the profit\nWhere are the instructions. and where the reso-\nution of the Central BnrI! They do net ac-\ncompany the repnrt--thny were not r-portn to\nthe Legislature they are no! prlnte I'm the Jnlrn.\nais; and yet this chary, was sustanc by the House\nOf Representatives, when It was unknown to the\nHouse, what tie Instructions contained, or wna,\npowers were directed to be conferred by the reso-\nluton of.the Central Board. upon which the in\nstructions were predicated. Why did not those\ndocuments accompany the report, that the mem.\nbers of the Legislature, and the people, might en-\nanime for themselves! =\n\n\nAlthough, party canno, be required to prove\nnegative, until prima facie affirmative is made\nout by testimony; and, although in cases like the\npresent, the charges fall to the ground unless PROV\nen-; vet, In this instance, it can be shown clearly\nthat the negative is true !; aud that the comm,S-\nsoners did not violate their Instructions.\n\n\ng. The minority report conlains the fol nwns TX.\ntract from Tie Instruct ioas:-.As hor\"tnGnr'. ynI\nagain left To exercise yJur Orn iisr,pii,n ~\nall things cnnnected with your n'<~' n,l\" rnl>\nis all Of the instruction that cnm bolD'n .D. .,,-\nisature, aud this contor1D, general and uuIun;ind\npower.\n\n\nAgain, neither the Centred tiprr.i. wa - direct.\ned the Instructions TO b\" given. ,ur Mr.. Dawes the\n.\nintmsted that the commissioners had violated\ntheir instruction !; but iuscad of dong so, they\nreceived these Southern aud Western funds from\nthe commissioners, settled and adj jusuted their ac.\ncounts, aud compensated them for their services.\nII was not then thought that they had violated their\ninst ructions.\n\n\nFor the sake of argument, we will suppose that\nthey had no authority for the conversion of tho\nfunds. have heard it asserted. by lawyers, tina\nthe subsequent recognition Of the of the agent,\n! by the principal, although the act was originally\nanthororized, makes ii much the act of the prin.\ncipal, and as obligatory upon him. as il the agen,\nhad express authority the time the act was pe..\nformed. How stands tho present case. when this\nprinciple OF the law is appIr ed to it! The Bank\nEever disav0wed the acts of the commissioners. rs.\nbut received the funds at their hands. settle\ntheir accounts and paid them for their services.\nThe President Of the Bank, in his le,te, In Me.\nRust. which is published with the minority repor,\nsays: \"Ther ,choie coaducf. was no, only sutisfac\ntory o ,hr Central Boara Of tie Bnnk, but hei,\nentire agency in The negotiation and sale of IN,\nbonds disposed Of by them; the DISPOSITION AND AR\n\n\nRANGEMENr of the FUNDS coming inio thei, hand,\nfrom sath syie;iheir long and unvearied oifeni,on\n10 all fhr md'f,s connected ~ifh their mission. III\nprompt asd FAlTHFU. MANNER IN WHICH THEY Dis\nCHANGED EVERY DUTY, comnected With Il, sras sse1\n\n=== 9_1866-03-31_p1_sn84026922_00237288154_1866033101_0364.json\nThe tri-weekly news. [volume] (Winnsboro, S.C.) 1865-1876 · 1866-03-31\ncrops: cached\nheadline: / to 9.70\n\nExtraordinary Double Apparltion Of Liv-\n\n\ning Persons.\n---\nSome two weeks since,a young lady\nof this city, whose father is engaged in\n\n\nmercantile business in this city, awoke\n\n\nfrom a sleep, feeling distressed and\n\n\nalarmed from the effects of an unpleas\n\n\nant dream. The gas.light was burning,\n\n\nbut had been turned down to the closest\n\n\npoint, thus making dim light in the\n\n\nroom and rendering portions of it almost\n\n\ndark. Soon after awaking the young\n\n\nladv's attention was attracted by the\n\n\nwell.defned figure of q lady of her ac-\n\n\nquaiEtance moving from the door, some\n\n\nten feet from the foot OF her bed, toward\nit. Impulsively she called the figure by\n\n\nname, on the instant forgetting the i'm.\n\n\nprobability of the friend being in the\n\n\nhonsc, and the fact that she was not\n\n\nresident of the city but resided in st\n\n\nLouis. Soon, however, all this recurrcd\n\n\nto her and the figure already neared the\n\n\nnow alarmed girl. The form and fea.\n\n\nlures were perfect and distinct, the ex.\n\n\npreston one of cheering greeting, and as\n| it approached closer and closer 10 her\n\n\nside it became DINNER and DINNER, and\n\n\nfinally disappeared entirely, when it had\n\n\nadvanced to about half the length ofthe\n\n\nbed The nervousness induced by the\n\n\nincident, naturally enough induced the\n\n\nyoung lady to arouse the family. who as\n\n\ncribe3 tbs matter &c excited imaginings\n\n\nBut there is singular sequel. She hat\nforebodings despite all that was said te\n, calm them, and the next day wrote hel\nfriend detailing the incident. An an\nswer was promptly received, announcing\nthe good health of the writer, and th'\n, fact that on the same night and at th'\n\n\nsame hour, she had been visited in pre-\n3 cisely the same manner by the sem\nblance of her friend in Albany, and beer\n! Ialarmed thereby, lest it was the foresau\ni ner of evil. The mutual revelation wa\n!. I a relief to both. The circumstance, W\n(; think, has few if any parallels, and ca,\ny partially be ascribed TO the love the Nv\n= girls haze for each other, and Of actiy\nQ nervous tempersments. But as to q\ny entirely satisfactory explanation of i\n's none can be given N\n\n\nA ttis\n\n=== 3_1881-06-04_pna_sn85058130_00206535891_1881060401_0546.json\nThe Salt Lake herald. [volume] (Salt Lake City [Utah]) 1870-1909 · 1881-06-04\ncrops: cached\nheadline: BRIEF TELEeRAms.\n---\nThe Turks are disarming Fort Puntl\n\n\nD:Az is to 20 to France as Mexica\n\n\nminister.\n\n\nRI No one was injured in the riots at Scar\nJeff, Ireland.\nII General Grant il detained at quArAn\n! tine. of New Orleans.\nDsvitt. The jailed Land LeaRusr, is il\n, good health and spirits.\n\n\nThe Montreal sugar market il excileC\n\n\nover an advance in prices.\n\n\nTIE Duke OF GoIfoIk, liberal, is Cd\n\n\nposed to the Parnell movement.\n\n\nAll mountain tribes around Mateur,\n\n\nTunia, have submitted 10 the French.\n\n\nA jockey club has been organized in\nthe City OF Mexico with 800, 900 capital.\n\n\nWilliam Malone. jr., son Of the soma\ntor, married Mary A. Tanner on Thur\nday. L\n\n\nThe British government has paid the\nsl5' 600 fishery award due the United\nStates. .-.-.- -.-- a.\ntbnkling left Albany for New York\non Thursday. thus dsmOrafiEirg his fol-\nII = - -\nStar route cAses will. not beready for\nthe present term OF the Washington\nest. Jalien's\" hurt Is in no degree\nserious. He will b. speeded at Saginaw\nnext week. - ~ y \"a -\nEnglish Catholic peers will bring\nArch bishop Croke's uttsrsnces before\nthe Pope. FF\nMrs. Senator Malone is going to Eu-\nrope to remain several years TO educate\nher daughter.\n\n\nSeveral of the horses in attendance at\nthe Detroit meeting are somewhat Sun\nder the weather\"\n\n\nFrench ministers in the Senate will\ntake no part in the discussion Of the\nScT1tts'l de Liste bill. .r\n\n\nAttorney Generst MeVeash thinks\nthe Star route fraGds will nns be tried\nuntil next September. ----.-.--\n\n\nThe Palmer-Sullivsn Company has\npurchased the Mexico, TOholuea sud\nCuanlillam Rlilroad. f@.tFHH Up\nA delegation OF West VirRinians has\nasked Garfield to appoint A. w. Camp\nbell to foreign mission. ..dtla-.\n\n\nMail contractors are puzzled 70 kpow\nwhether to employ counsel before they\nare attacked through the courts. eE1\n\n\nPresident Grevy says he has author\nlied no one to express An opinion in his\nnsme on the Sertltin dc Lists bill.\n\n\nIn any question affecting landed inter-\neats in England the Catholic nobility\ncasts its influence on the tory side.\n\n\nIrish magistrates have been cautioned\nagainst reading the riot act except on the\ngravest occasions. E\n\n\nGudgell & Simpson, of Pleasant Hill,\nMo-, have selected from the best herds in\nRnRland and Scotland ninsty-nine Here\nford cattle.\n\n\nJohn Griscom, the Chicago faster, has\nlost sixteen pounds in five and q half\ndays. He is active, vigorous, healthy\nand not nervous.\n\n\nPatent Commissioners Marble is 10 be\nmade commissioner Of the land office,\nand Martial Dudley, Of Indiana, is to\ntake the patent Office.\n\n\nCardinal Manning did not deny the\nuse Of Catholic hsliE TO Land Leaugers,\nbut counselea Catholic societies to Keep\nclear Of the Land League.\n\n\nIowa ereenbaekers dominated full\nstate ticket, headed by D. II. Clark for\ngovernor, and ending With Mrs. Mary Z.\nNash for superintendent of public in\nstruction.\n\n\nA monument erected by the Ladies'\nMonumental Aasoeiatinn AZ Nj Ofiya\nCenetery, Frederick County, Nd. TO\nmark the graves Of southern soldiers, was\non Thursday.\n\n=== 5_1903-03-28_pna_sn88064111_00280761679_1903032801_0915.json\nThe Lafayette gazette. [volume] (Lafayette, La.) 1893-1921 · 1903-03-28\ncrops: cached\nheadline: z h@ a v@t@r-\n\n\nRHENA BOUDREAUX, Editor\n---\nLast Friday night the Avata1\n\n\nmeeting was not so well attended\nas it would have been had the\nweather been more favorable;\nthe program however was very\ninstructive and interesting, be-\ning a Louisiana night. Mr..\nAlleman's talk on Louisiana was\nvery much enjoyed by those\npresent.\n\n\nThose of the Avatar who at\ntended the Attakapas meeting\nSaturday night were delighted\nwith the comic program so well\nrendered by the members of\nthat society.\n\n\nIs not there some way of get\nting rid of the advertising man,\nwho will deliberately stalk into\nand litter them with paper, such\nas free drinks to coca-cOla?\n\n\nThis week seems to have been\nan unlucky one as far as acct\ndents are concerned. Monday\nMr.. Gilbert Kahn, while using\nthe saw in the work shop had\nthe misfortune to cut the end Of\nhis forefinger off. On the same\nday Mr.. DeBlanc, picking up an\niron bar at the forge works, was\nvery seriously burned; the bar\nhad been heated at the wrong\nend by some careless student,\nand the young gentleman being\nignorant of the fact suf-\nfered a painful accident.\n\n\nAfter being visited by a spell\nIF the measels, Mr.. Mumps now\nboldly knocks at the dormitory\nloor; poor Miss Guidry is his\nirst victim; we trust that she |\nwill soon recover and that the |\nwill soon be free from\n\n=== 3_1857-01-16_pna_sn86092535_00202190595_1857011601_0057.json\nWheeling daily intelligencer. [volume] (Wheeling, Va. [W. Va.]) 1852-1859 · 1857-01-16\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nTHEsE STAYING TmEs.-Yes, slay is the word.\nFor instance, a team started up street yesterday\nlike mad cap, and in its course came in collision\nwith dray. The dray was so standing that a\nvery slight jar to the tail of it, would give the\nStats very forcible side motion And so it\nhappened, and down came the horse almost\nbrvathlcss. This is but one of the Staying ac-\neidents we see every day. Yesterday evening\nan elderly gentleman had his pedestals suddenly\nKnocked from under him by the sled of reck-\nless urchin, who came tearing down the hill on\nUnion street, at a:4O rate. Last, but very far\nfrom least, this department had the wind raised\naround its head by huge snow ball, which one\nyoung scavcnger hurled at another in prOmis-\ncuous crowd. This all comes under the head of\n''Gying; and so might we say of the slips and\nfalls which we witness at every corner. We\ntee every time VIC go out as if it was the swim\nter of our discomfort\" and our nerves, what\nform our own mishaps and sympathy with 9th\nerS, will be in utter arrangement by the first of\nApril\n\n=== 21_1912-08-01_p1_sn88076432_00271740785_1912080101_0260.json\nCourier Democrat. (Langdon, N.D.) 1891-1920 · 1912-08-01\ncrops: None\nheadline: ASailor Yam\n---\nWell, mebby dey is. but ghost ships\namt\"\n\n\nThey were at the Sailors Snug Har-\nDor, sitting on benches in the sun.\nSome of the younger ~ones-young fell\nlows of s~eventy-had imbibed modern\nnotions concerning apparitions. The\nman who demurred, Bob Barclay, an\nold tub some eighty-six years old,\nwas not minded to have the traditions\nOf a lifetime thus demolished.\n\n\nwhat de y know about ghost\n| ships?' asked one Of the sunshine\nbathers.\n\n\nWhat do 1 know about ghost ships?\n| Well, hist. mates. and I'll spin y a yarn\nabout one. and when l get through /\ndon't want none O y to say 'twan't no\nSIGH thing. I'm goin' to tell y what l\nseen with my own two eyes,\"\n\n\nThe old fellow was shrunken like a\nbladder with the wind gone out of it,\n| and his voice was husky as a cracked\nbell. But he believed the yarn he told,\nas any one could see by the earnestness\nwith which he told it.\n\n\nat was this way, mates: I'd left the\nsea and had gone to live with my\n| darter in a seaport town on the south\nAtlantic coast. The apple O. my eye\nwas my grandfather, Molly Sewell,\nas pretty a little craft as ye eve see.\n| Most young uns hain't got no use for\n| old buffers, but Molly had a lot O. use\n| for me, and there was nothin' she\n| wouldn't do for me. The only thing l\n| could do for her was to make fancy\nbaskets for her, as I'd learned to do\naboard ship, and spin yarns for her\n| about the sea. She'd inherited a love\n| for a sailors life from me and a lot\nO. other granddads before me-we was\nall sailors and she couldn't get\nenough yarns about sea life out O. me\nto satisfy her.\n\n\nzone day I see her walkin' with a\nyoung sailor, Jack Barston, OF the\nbeach, and when she come in I said,\n| Molly. air We goin' to sail in that\nship?'\n\n\n\"'Nhat ship, grandpa she asked,\n| colorin'. She knew well enough what\nI meant.\n\n\n\"'The ship 1 saw skuddin' before the\nwind on the beach this afternoon\n\n\n\"She jist dropped her hand down on\nmy shoulder. put her arms around me\nand didn't say nothin'.\n\n\n\"It didn't make no difference to me\nher havin' a sweetheart. She was jist\nas lovin' to me as before. Barston\nvas as fine a young mar as ever pull\ned a rope, and none Of us had any OBJ\nlection to him. But he had to save\nsome money to put a gal he'd marry\ninto a cot. and that would take him\nsome time. He made several voyages\nafter he got engaged to Molly, and\nevery time he come back he'd doubled\nhis pile. At last he said he'd make\none more voyage and when he come\nback from that he'd get married and\nstay ashore. He'd have enough to\nbuild his cot and buy him a good boat\nfor odd jobs by which he could make\na Twin.\n\n\ncarter he'd sailed away on this last\ncruise on the President Adams Molly\ncame to me for comfort. She allus\nrelied on me for that when her boy\nwent away, because I'd let her talk\nabout him and nothin' else. And she\nsays to 'grandp',' says she. Somehow\nl got an idee that Jack amt never\ncomm back from this voyage I jist\nTUX her in my arms and tried to smoth-\ner it out of her. but I couldn't. But,\ngrandp',' she went on. he promised\nme that if he lost his life by bein'\nwrecked or dyin' of fever or anything\nlike that he'd send me some token to\nlet me know that he died thinking of\nme-.\n\n\nII disremember what year it was\nthat Jack was due. but it was in the\nspring. Molly got a letter from him\nfrom Singapore figurin' out the date of\nthe ships reachin' the home port with.\nin a week. At the beginnin' O. that\nweek Molly was very narvous. She\nnever acted before about Jacks com.\nin home as she did this time. She KEEP\nsayin': 'Grandp'. he won't come. l know\nhe won't come The first day Of the\nweek Jack allowed the ship would\nlcdSu lOIl pdSSeu. anu lllC SGCUllu dllU\nso on to the sixth. and no ship Adams\n\n\nION the morning of the seventh and\nlast day a thick fog was rollin' il\nfrom the ocean. Molly and l sat quiet\nly on a dune. l holdin' her hand te\nkeep up her courage. waitin' for the\nfog to lift and hopin' the Adams would\nbe in the ofing. AFTER awhile there\nwas a break in the mist, and then\nwith all her sails filled, came a shiI\nI knew to onct was the Adams. Blu\na chill run owe me. for I knowed there\nwasn't any wind.\n\n\nMOLLY looked and clutched her hos\nON with her hands.\n\n\n'\"Then the fog slowly closed in OF\nthe ship, and we couldn't see no NOR\nOf her. We waited. and l knowed i\nshe was a real ship is soon as the TO:\ncleared away we'd see her ridin' a\nanchor in the harbor. But when i\ncleared up she wasn't there.\n\n\n\"Molly and l went home. both know\nin she hadn't been there. Jack Bars\nton had sent his ghost to tell Moll,\n4laA H. Aal -a..l Ill ii Z\n\n=== 19_1893-02-03_pna_sn84026918_00295862099_1893020302_0658.json\nThe weekly Union times. [volume] (Union C.H., South Carolina) 1871-1894 · 1893-02-03\ncrops: None\nheadline: in THE HEART OF BRAZIL.\n\nDESCRIPTION OF A TYPICAL IN\nOla RUBBER CAMP.\n\na Source of Profit to Many, But tho\n:, Poor Indian Gets Practically\n\n\nNothing for His Perllous Labor.\n---\nthese Amazonian towns, where every\nthing reeks of india rubber--wbere it is\ntho one enSrossing industry Of the\npeople, the one topic Of conversation,\nthe one thing constantly seen, smellcd\nand tastcd-you are sure to be seized by\nthe spirit of discovery, sooner or later,\nand burning desire to turn hunter\nyourself and become a habitante, il for\never so brief a period, of n genuine\nrubber camp in the wilderness. And\nnothing Is easier than to crafty that\nlaudahlc ambition, especially if you are\n\"takcn\" with it at Manaos-a thousand\nmiles up the mighty river, iu the heart\nof the greatest rubber producing section\nCt the globe.\n\n\nIn our case it was particularly easy,\nfor our host is a rubber merchant (as are\nmost Of the substantial citiz,as OF the\nplace., who keeps from fifty to one\nhundred Indians constantly cmp'oyel as\ncollectors, under tho leadership of a\n\"captaiu,\" or head collector, of their\nown choosing.\n\n\nThe following facts. stated briefly a,\npossible, arc summary Of the informt-\nton VIC have picked up in Brazil, from\ndivers sources, relative to tho rubber\ntrade. Like other industries in various\nParts of the world, it appears 10 be con.\ntrol!el hy capital on the Scriptural plan\noi \"to him that hath shall he givcu,\"\nWhile the actual toilcrs are very poorly\npaid for their labor. Hcreabouts it it\ncustomary for wealthy man to obtain {\ngrant OF land of greater or less extent,\naccording to his political influence aud\nfinancial powers Of persuasion, with the\nexclusive righJ of gathering rubber\nthereon for Ii stated number OF years.\nThe entire river trade Of the Amazon is\nrun Oh the credit system. The rubber\nhunters, who are mostly Indiups, arr\nfitted out by their employers muci as\nAmerican miners used to be supplied\nwith Hgrub stake\" in their search for\nprecious metals. Each hunter, before\nhe sets forth on a fresh journey, is pro-\nvided with gun, u quantity of ammu-\nmilton, blanket, hammock, and enough\nprovisions to sustain him from two to\nsix months; all Of which is charged to\nhis account at the highest market price,\nan,i i'll return he stipulates to sell to this\nsame accommodating dealer, at some\nfixed sum per pound, all the rubber he\nmay collect during his trip, after paying\nwhat lie owes for the outfit.\n\n\nBut he seldom gets the debt paid up,\nand, according to the laws OF Brazil, as\nlong as he owes penny the man to\nwhom he is indebted eau claim him for\nwork, holidays aud all, III bondage\nakin to slavery. They are engaged in\nthe first place for a term of years, aud\nthe majority of hunters, in consequence\nOf their debts and propensity to drink\nup all available cash in the form Of\nchicha, are practically never released\nfrom tho contract.\n\n\nThe employers, in their turn, arc\nbound to be in debt to the small traders\nin the river towns, to whom they sell the\nrubber. They pay absurily high prices\nfor inferior goods and get little for the\nproduct Of the enterprise as compared to\nthe price of rubber when it gets out Of\nthe clutches of the \"middle-men, whilo\nthose who do all the actual work aud\nendure the risks aud hardships get next\nto nothing. The small trader, likewise,\nis in debt to the wholesale dealer in\nPara; and the wholesaler is even more\ndeeply indebted to the New York, Balti-\nmore or London firm which furnishes\nhim with supplies aud finally secures the\nrubber. Some body makes heavy profits\nout Of every transaction, but it is not\ntho poor Indian, upon whose courage\nard powers Of endurance all depends, nor\nyet the smaller traders.\n\n\nThe annual expectation Of India rub\nber from Para is said to be upward OF\ngo, 600, 900 pounds, worth from So, O00,-\ncoo to $4,000, 900. Tnc rubber tree of\nBrazil (siphonia elastica, near relative\nof the iicus elastlca of the East Indies\nand the urccola elastica Of Asia) is\nreally giant species Of milk weed. 1t be-\ngins TO yield when about fifteen years\nold, and the Government has repeatedly\nsuggested plans for cultivating it by\nplanting large areas with trees and con-\nQuoting the business like that of coffee\nand sugar plantations. But Brazilians\nseem to be peculiarly devoid of the\npower to take ga long look ahead\" aud\nSO far nobody has been found willing to\nwait fifteen years for the first returns on\nan investment.\n\n\nHcreabouts the rubber hunters are\ncalled Siringuerios, as in Central Amer\nlea they are known as Yularoes. They\nusually go out in small parties, having\nchosen one Of their own number as \"Cap-\ntain\" and as soon as tile rubber swamps\nare reached they select a suitable spot\n= Tor INC pasc or opcravrons aud proceed TO\nconstruct rude camp, which serves as\nthe general rendezvous. They then wan.\nder of, slngly and in couples, searching\nall day for new trees and returning to\nheadquarters at nlghtfall. The scamp\"\nconsists of a central hut, built on stilt-\nlike supports to elevate it above the ma\nlaria haunted norass in which rubber\ntrees most abound, with palm matched\nroof, projecting all around like that Of a\nSwiss chalet.\n\n\nThere arc neither windows NJ: door\nto this rude home, nor any interior\nfurnishings except hooks upol which\nhammocks may be suspended wlen rains\ndrive their owners inside. Ordinarily\n'al outdoors serves we'll for kitchen,\nand the men prefer to suspend their\nsleeping paraphernalia from tho trees\naround the central fire, built for the\ntriple purpose of frightening away\nDrOwlinO bpasts counteractin o. the effects\n\n=== 1_1920-10-07_pna_sn85040437_00414215397_1920100701_0117.json\nThe Washburn times. [volume] (Washburn, Wis.) 1896-1976 · 1920-10-07\ncrops: None\nheadline: THE HAUNTED POOL.\n---\nSY0pSis. - DIR. John Nicholson,\nJust beginning his career, becomes\nresident physician and companion\nOF Homer Sidney at Hartley house.\nMr.. Sidney is an American, semi\ninvalid, old and rich and very de-\nsIrous to live. Mrs. Sidney is\nSpanish woman, dignified and reti-\ncent. Jed, the butler, acts like\nprivileged member OF the family.\nHartley house is a line old isolated\ncountry place, with a murder story,\nq haunted pSol.\" and many watch\ndogs. and an atmosphere OF mys-\nterry. The haunted pool is where\nRichard Dobson, son of former\nowner of Hartley house, had killed\nhis brother, Arthur Dobson. JED\nbegins operations by locking the\ndoctor i'm his room the very first\nnight. Doctor John fixes his door\nso he can't be locked in. He meets\nIaobel. daughter of the house, anc\nfalls I'm love at first sight. In the\nnight he finds the butler drunk and\nholding Mrs. Sidney by the wrist.\nHe interferes. Mrs. Sidney makes\nlight Of it. John buys a revolver.\nJohn overhears Jed telling Mrs.\nSidney he will have his way. In\nreply she says she will not hesitate\nto kill him. Mrs. Sidney asks John\nto consent to the announcement of\nhis engagement to Isobel. The\nyoung people consent to the make\nbelieve engagement. Later they\nfind it is to head off Jed. who\nwould marry Isobel. Jed tries to\nkill John, but the matter is\nsmoothed over. John, though \"en-\ngagged to Isobel, conceals his love.\nMr.. Sidney visits a nearby prison\nand has Dobson, the murderer,\npointed out Queer stories of the\n''haunted pool are told.\n\n=== 13_1874-06-12_p1_sn85034076_00200292364_1874061201_0609.json\nThe Andrew County Republican. [volume] (Savannah, Mo.) 1871-1876 · 1874-06-12\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\n1ouT ulSuuesS, 1Aao 40 iiV'iC AAioUy,\nshe loves the ~failies I have seen her\nlooking for us many time\"\n\n\n\"She is welcome\" said the princess.\n\"Form a ring, my trends let us\ndance\"\n\n\nIn a moment they had formed a circle,\nand danced round and round the tree till\nKitty was almost dizzy watching them.\nThen they stopped and broke into little\ngroups and chatted to each other.\n\n\nChimes are changed\" said one fairy.\nOnce the children often came to\ndances and were glad to seek us out, but\nnow they care nothing about u.s.\"\n\n\n\"Not quite so bad as that\" said the\ntall fairy who had recognized Kitty.\n81 see more mortals than almostany n\nof us, unless it may be Ivy here, and I\ncan tell you there are some children,\nyes, and grown people, too, who care\nfor u.s.\"\n\n\n\"Ah, yes\" said graceful Fuchsia,\n\"I know how they look for us and\nwhisper their wishes to u.s.\"\n\n\nEYES,\" said Ivy, for all old Scarlet\nhas been among mortals so long, I go to\na great many of their haunts that he\ndoes not, and I will tell you how it is.\nChildren love fairies as they love oil\nbright and pretty things, but a great\nmany children have q sort Of fairyland\nof their own /; not like ours, you know,\nfor their beautiful dresses and toys tear\nand soil and break, while ours are al\nways the same ; but when theirs are\nare fresh they arc very lovely, and they\nare SO taken up with them that they\nhaven't time to care for us. Then they\nhave so many parties of their W that\nthey don't care for our dances, and\nsometimes their mothers give so much\nattention to all these fine things that\nthey never think to tell them of the\nfairies, and so they. even hear of\nthem. Scarlet's friend, little Kitty,\ndoesn't think so much of fine things\nbut that she can love the flowers and\nfairies\"\n\n\nZone thing more\" said a tall, beau\ntiful fairy, the loveliest OF them all,\nwith pure white robes and pale golden\nhair-she looked like the beautiful\nangel in the church window, Kitty\nthought. cit is only good and gentle\nlittle children who can see MS; children\nwho love each other and are good to\ntheir mammas. We couldn't have i'll\ntempered, greedy or rude children find\nus-we should vanish away instantly\"\n\n\n\"Halloo - cried Joe, flinging open\nthe door with a bang. What are You\ndoing there. you little stupid?\"\n\n\n\"Hush !\" said Kitty. \"Just see the\nfairies!\"\n\n\n\"Fairies, indeed, Iguess so; where 2\n\n\nKitty pointed to the jar, but the\nfairies had vanished.\n\n\n\"There is nothing there, you\nlittle goosel You've been dreaming.\nThere don't cry, I don't mean tc\nplague you,\" for the tears started tc\nKitty's eyes, and Joe wasa k~ind-hearted\nboy, in spite of his roughness. The lit\ntie old man Just peeped ont from the\nmaple boughs, and nodded as Joe put\nhis arm around the little girl, in a shy,\nshame-faced way.\n\n\nKitty pointed eagerly. \"See, Joe !\n\n\nels it a butterfly ?\" said Joe. \"II\ncatch it for you\"\n\n\nHe hastily stretched out his hand\nbut the fairy vanished.\n\n\n\"It was a fairy, Joe did you reall.\nsee it :\"\n\n\nJoe looked puzzled.\n\n\n81 saw some wings futtering, Tut\ncan't find anything. Are there reall..\nfairies after all ?\"-ILedrH and Home\n\n=== 4_1941-07-27_p1_sn88063294_00414187511_1941072701_0147.json\nDetroit evening times. (Detroit, Mich) 1921-1958 · 1941-07-27\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\n'Ccet uuc Don toirc utcrcrs1ute\" ne said\n-But / stI can't piece 30 together The one\nwhite man on the Islands rs Encst Biddell, the\nHudsons Bay Company representative there\nII wag he who sent out the story by messenger\nand dog team one hundred miles to the Great\nWhale River post on The mainland, where they\nhave tranamitter. l just had the post on\nthe air\n\n\nBut AN / could make out was a hodge\npodgo about the Twenty fourth chapter of st.\nNattlD The World Wars Ghost ship\nan amazing display Of Northern lights. men,\nwomen and children going out naked TO \"A hill\ntop In twenty degrees below zero. \"A falling\nmeteor the second coming of Christ wo-\nman clubbing a child TO death two men\nshooting others from behind Imagine that\nOf Eskimosl Well The Mountics will have\nsome job with this\"\n\n\n\\Y/IiEE radio word Of the affair reached\nW headquarters of the Royal Canadian\nMounted Police at ottawa. they tired to get in\n\n=== 18_1903-11-13_p9_sn93053726_00271763517_1903111301_0392.json\nWaterbury evening Democrat. · 1903-11-13\ncrops: None\nheadline: Ghosts.\n---\nII WMVBENs According to the FngIiSh thensophist9 c. W. Leadbeateh ghosts are classifedl in seven types. These are: Thoughtl forms, elves, churchyard ghosts, astraH\n\n impressions, double of living man, ani-, nal apparitions and the genuine ghostl\n","numLines":1443,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1443}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1874c642-c2ec-431d-a899-d8b7ba6ec392","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"other"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2c-reader","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
