75\t\n176\t\n177\tTIONALLY CLEVER\n178\t---\n179\tSIuuenCS Caught lIle ehdCI SplIlI VI\n180\tGeorge Cohan's melodramatic farce,\n181\t\"The Seven Keys to Baldpate,\" in a\n182\tperformance given at the High School\n183\tAuditorium last Friday evening, by\n184\tmembers of the Senior Class. One of\n185\tthe most difficult of modern plays for\n186\tamateur presentation, it possessed\n187\tmuch of the charm the author intend\n188\ted it should. The stage was cleverly\n189\tarranged and the lighting effects were\n190\tgood, quite out of the ordinary, and\n191\ta direct compliment to the director.\n192\t\n193\t\n194\tJohn Costello carried the weight of\n195\tthe performance and he made noble\n196\tcharacter. He had power, dignity,\n197\tand humor. The ease with which he\n198\tcarried the \"lead\" was remarkable\n199\tfor high school student. At all\n200\ttimes he was the prominent and sue\n201\tcessful character in every action.\n202\tcivilian Magee,\" novelist, was a\n203\ttrue type and John lived the part.\n204\tHis voice was remarkable as was his\n205\tInterpretation of the many embaras-\n206\tsing situations that confronted him.\n207\tCohan reveals all of his characters in\n208\ta mysterious way, usually in the twi-\n209\t\n210\t\n211\t\n212\t\n213\t\n214\taudience but it pleases and interests\n215\tall. The methods used were slightly\n216\thigher than usual, and each single in\n217\tdepreciation added to the mystery of\n218\tthe farce, and before we knew it, a\n219\ttheft, murder, a pack of lies, were\n220\tall committed-Oh yes, and few\n221\thearts broken. Peter, the hermit\n222\t(Sam Bjone) was as pathetic figure\n223\tas he was an actor. Sam added much\n224\tto the SHADY\" dealing that took\n225\tplace, by his uncanny chuclde in the\n226\tghost scene, and his 'fhermit-like\" re-\n227\tmarks.\n228\t\n229\t\n230\tMisfortunes befell Magee (Cos-\n231\ttello) as soon as he reached the Inn\n232\tto write his story. His innocent pre-\n233\tparatories to always elk things\" for\n234\this lady fair, Mary Norton( Virginia\n235\tKells,) was the only real source of\n236\tany laughter in the production. Vir-\n237\tginia was a charming newspaper re-\n238\tporter, and proved to be \"promis-\n239\ting\" help to Magee. She supported\n240\ther part with enthusiasm, skill, and\n241\tgrace. Harold Claseman offered an\n242\tearnest, subdued and understanding\n243\tpicture of Quinby, the caretaker. Ly-\n244\tla Ellison, played as Mrs. Quinby, and\n245\tshe gave old \"Lij\" plenty to think\n246\tabout. \"Jim Corgan,\" (Herman He\n247\tDIM thought he comprehended life,\n248\tbut found out the reward most crook\n249\ted politicians eventually come to, is\n250\tthe road Of disappointment and re-\n251\tmorse. Herman acted his part with\n252\ta firm and thorough comprehension of\n253\tit's value. Vione Steege, as Myra\n254\tThornhill, gave a touch of real COM\n255\tedy in her \"wise remarks\" to Hay\n256\tdin (Jerome Johnson,) the railroad\n257\tmagnate. She made a good real\n258\tolive\" ghost; especially good was her\n259\t\"death\" scene, since the gun Max\n260\tJohn McDermond) used would not\n261\texplode. Max might have made it a\n262\tlittle more melodramatic if he had\n263\tused the pistol as club instead of\n264\ta fire arm. Max and Haydn worked\n265\twell together, and were ably assist\n266\ted by their righthand man, Bland,\n267\tplayed by Philip Steiner. It's hard\n268\tto imagine Philip thrashing anyone\n269\tbut Cohan had US believing Bland in\n270\ttended to. There were the usual\n271\tnumber of fights and arguments, and\n272\teach one was characterized by shoot\n273\ting and the shrill screams of the\n274\twomen, especially Mrs. Rhodes,\n275\tGamma Robischon). Anna was sim\n276\tcere, pleasant widow, and we were all\n277\tvery happy to learn that she didn't\n278\teventually marry the crooked mayor.\n279\t\n280\t\n281\tCarl Fischer added the police\n282\tchiefs part, and he was decided sue\n283\tcess. Ably assisted by patrolmen,\n284\tII\n285\t/. the chief rounded up the entire \"ring\n286\tof frauders,\" and was just about\n287\tready to make a fine get away, when\n288\tthe owner of the resort Edwin Gray,\n289\tmade his debut, and the audience\n290\twas handed real surprise. The play\n291\twas SO finely propelled that one didn't\n292\trealize the utter impossibilities of\n293\tmost of the situations, and the \"fixed\"\n294\tnature of most of the action. To\n295\tfind the whole affair was a put up\n296\tJob merely to teach the novelist how\n297\tilliterary. and trashy were his works,\n298\tone felt very much fooled because of\n299\tthe cleverly hidden motive behind the\n300\twhole production.\n301\t\n302\t\n303\tThe entire play was a clever piece\n304\tof work. Miss Knickerbocker, the\n305\tcoach, through intensive training and\n306\thard work, produced one OF the best\n307\tproductions given here. She deserves\n308\tmany compliments on her excellent\n309\taccomplishment and her skill in pro-\n310\tducing plays is recognized fact. This\n311\tlast presentation was one as human\n312\tas was the problem it presented. It\n313\tdidn't require any excessive mental\n314\tagility nor especial receptiveness.\n315\tArgument was offered in dramatic\n316\tform by means of continually excit-\n317\ting story, well acted. No one failed\n318\tto feel the mental stimulus Of \"Seven\n319\t\n320\t=== 16_1911-01-05_pna_sn88077573_00237283727_1911010501_0423.json\n321\tThe Marion daily mirror. (Marion, Ohio) 1892-1912 · 1911-01-05\n322\tcrops: cached\n323\theadline: spooks Give WRONG TIP.\n324\t---\n325\tColumbus, o., Jan. 5.-Contrary to\n326\ta message from the spirit world\n327\twhich Anna e. Baison, Dayton Spir.\n328\titualistic medium, is reported to have\n329\treceived the night previous to the\n330\tdecision, the medium has lost her\n331\tcase in the supreme court and the\n332\ttitle to a $4,000 Dayton house and lot\n333\twas given to DR.. Charles A. Pauley OF\n334\tCincinanti, Notwithstanding Mrs. Bal\n335\tson a