who does\n1527\tnot get his commissions at both ends. =\n1528\tSrrihner\n1529\t\n1530\t=== 3_1883-11-08_p1_sn85025620_00212479330_1883110801_0175.json\n1531\tThe Worthington advance. [volume] (Worthington, Minn.) 1874-1908 · 1883-11-08\n1532\tcrops: None\n1533\theadline: HER HUSBAND THINKS SHE rs\n1534\t\n1535\t\n1536\tIYTY'r1\n1537\t---\n1538\ta Georgia lady recently became de.\n1539\tranged While attending Il revival meet\n1540\ting in Worth county. She went ravi,lR\n1541\tabout the house and yard with Il bucket\n1542\tOf water. baptizing everything in her\n1543\tpathway. She baptized her husband\n1544\tand children, ami while doing it sang\n1545\tsongs she had heard but once or twice.\n1546\tAlthough All unlettered woman she\n1547\treads any chapter in the, Bible, pro-\n1548\tnonncing correctly and distinctly, pays\n1549\ting attention to punctuation points,\n1550\tetc. She preachesnearly all the while\n1551\tuses the choicest words, and displays\n1552\tgreat knowledge in the handling Of\n1553\tdifferent subjects. Although not d\n1554\tMason, she Knows all the mysteries Of\n1555\tems OF Mnsons have gone to see her and\n1556\tthey have All gone away dumbfounded.\n1557\tShe neither eats nor sleeps. Her bus;\n1558\tband has come to the conclusion sha\n1559\tis Il witci.-North Georgia Gazette. i\n1560\t\n1561\t\n1562\tHer husband is an ignorant priest\n1563\tled man. All that this woman does\n1564\tand much more is done every day by\n1565\tmediums, and the facts are perfectlE,\n1566\tfamiliar to thousands uf people who\n1567\tknow --t!t;st the persons are neither\n1568\twitches nor insane. It is Il simple,\n1569\tlnatterof spirit control and exactly\n1570\tlike 1.-THE gift of tongues\" Of ''prsph-\n1571\tecy\" ETC., mentioned as occurring in\n1572\t\n1573\t\n1574\tBible times. Yet in the full light of\n1575\tthe 18th century people who are sus-\n1576\tceptlble to this spirit control are treat\n1577\ted 10y ignorant doctors. preachers, and\n1578\tbig@tS as insane or as fraunds. A gen\n1579\ttleman III Ohio some time since had\n1580\this wife seat to the insane ASYLUM. at\n1581\t\n1582\t\n1583\tNewburg. heca!Se she w:sy subject to\n1584\tspirit control at times. There the poor\n1585\twoman must stay in the hands Of iynO-\n1586\trant physicians, who will not study the\n1587\tlaws of psychology and Of spirit con.\n1588\ttroll, because a materialistic church on\n1589\tthe one hand ana an egotlstical science\n1590\ton the other. condemn such invpstiga-\n1591\ttions as ''ot the devil'Or us unworthy\n1592\tthe lord'y pretensions OF se-called\n1593\tscientists ts.-Worthington ADVANCE.\n1594\t\n1595\t\n1596\t1--WE are glad to recognize in A. p.\n1597\tMiller, editor Of the ADVANCE. Il\n1598\tbrother indeed who is not afraid to\n1599\tspeak out the plain. simple truth which\n1600\tso readily takes all tie mystery out of\n1601\tmany Of these otherwise wonderful\n1602\toccurrences. Just use Il little common\n1603\tsense in the light Of known and well\n1604\tfacts, and the case Of the\n1605\tGeorgia lady is simple and not very\n1606\tstrange. When will our physicians\n1607\tlearn wisdom and use good common\n1608\tsense in the treatment of so-callea in.\n1609\tsane ?-1ndependent Age.\n1610\t"}]},"uuid":"625dba56-be0a-4621-8656-945a71e54eed","timestamp":"2026-10-31T07:50:05.500Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/c1/g07.txt","content":"=== 5_1906-06-15_p1_sn83045239_00295876797_1906061501_0098.json\nEcho de l&#39;ouest. [volume] (Minneapolis, Minn.) 1883-1929 · 1906-06-15\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nun quart d'heure.\n\n\nOn instant apres, il penetrate\ndams la piece design6e Oh il SO trou-\nvait en- presence dun Arabe de\nhaute stature.\n\n\nii Sc tenait debout, latitude\nhautajne, malgr6 les menottes aui\ntenaient SEES poignets lies.\n\n\nQue me veux-tu? demanda du-\nrement Destrem, en- lexaminant\ndes pieds a la tote\n\n\n- Te r6v6ler un secret, Sidi-\n| Kh6bir, en- echange dune grace.\n\n\n-Laquelle2\n\n\nLa vie sauve.\n\n\n-Je v%errai; quel est ton secret\n\n\nWest au sujet du meurtre du ca.\npitaine de Bussiares que j'ai vou!n\nto parler.\n\n\n81 tn me promets la vie sauve,\nlie puts te r6veler imm6diatement\nle nom de ~l'assass sin do ton ami.\n\n\n-Alors, parle; je t'6viterai la\nmort.\n\n\n-O'est bien; je to remercie,\nKh6bir.\n\n\nSache done que l'assassin de ton\nfrAre d'armes 6tait IN roumi, un\nFrancais.\n\n\nI'm Francais? s'exclama Des-\ntrem au comble de la stup6facti IN.\nEt tn sails son nom?\n\n\n-Oui; il SO nommait Charly.\n\n\n-Charly, lui?....\n\n\nMails, qui done await enlev6 le\nle bless6?\n\n\nMoi, fit nettement PARABLE.\nsans abaisser son regard p6n6-\ntrant.\n\n\nJ'agiseais par ordre de mon pc\nre, lie v6n6r6 marabout q qui j'ai\nsucc6i6 depuis.\n\n\n-Ainsi CO matjn Oh je ia rr6tai\ndams le desert, avsc ia caravane, tn\nemportals le capitalne de Bussia-\nres?\n\n\n-Oui, Kh6bir; ]e le conduisais\nvivant encore q Sidi- Okla\n\n\n-Od il mourut, sans doute?\n\n\nLIE lendemain meme de son ar\nriv6e, dams la maison du mara\nbout.\n\n\nQue dewitt son corps?\n\n\nIL fut d6pos6 dams un silo, loin\nde l%'oasis, atm que les chacals Ell\nlea hy6nes pussent SO repa1tre de\nsa d6pouille.\n\n\n-Alnsi, tn affrmes que Charly\nfut son assassin?\n\n\n-Je lie jure par le saint Prophe'\nte!....\n\n\n-Sais-tu dams quel but il com.\nNIT CO crime?\n\n\nPar vengeances,l je crois. u\nhaissa1t mortellement son ancien\ncapitaine qui l'avais Iaiss6 condam-\nner jadis aux travaux publics.\n\n\noh! le miserable! s'6cria Des-\ntrem, jele chat1erai; q mon tour je\nvengerai Bussiares!\n\n\nPuts sc ressaisissant dun effort,\nil conclut\n\n\n-C'est blen, je vais rentrer au\nconseil, ct puisque Je tai promis\nla vie sauye, tn l'auras, compile\nsur moi.\n\n\nUne demi-heure plus tard, la\ndernlere seance du conseil de guer-\nprenait fin.\n\n\nDAs ga sortie du conseil, le con-\nsell le colonel Destrem SO dirlgea\nvers SO demeure, Oh il allait retrou.\nyer Paule de Bussiares, le baron\nDutertre ct Mile de Mirecourt.\n\n=== 25_1926-05-13_p1_sn89064489_00393340022_1926051301_0192.json\nThe Sauk Centre herald. (Sauk Centre, Stearns County, Minn.) 1886-current · 1926-05-13\ncrops: cached\nheadline: SENIOR CLASS PLAY\n\n\nASPLENDID EFFORT\n\n\"SEVEN KEYS TO BALDPATE'\nWAS AN UNUSUAL STUDENT\n\n\nOFFERING\n\nJOHN COSTELLO STARS\n\nYOUNG MAN CARRIES SHOW\nHIS SUPPORT WAS EXCEP-\n\n\nTIONALLY CLEVER\n---\nSIuuenCS Caught lIle ehdCI SplIlI VI\nGeorge Cohan's melodramatic farce,\n\"The Seven Keys to Baldpate,\" in a\nperformance given at the High School\nAuditorium last Friday evening, by\nmembers of the Senior Class. One of\nthe most difficult of modern plays for\namateur presentation, it possessed\nmuch of the charm the author intend\ned it should. The stage was cleverly\narranged and the lighting effects were\ngood, quite out of the ordinary, and\na direct compliment to the director.\n\n\nJohn Costello carried the weight of\nthe performance and he made noble\ncharacter. He had power, dignity,\nand humor. The ease with which he\ncarried the \"lead\" was remarkable\nfor high school student. At all\ntimes he was the prominent and sue\ncessful character in every action.\ncivilian Magee,\" novelist, was a\ntrue type and John lived the part.\nHis voice was remarkable as was his\nInterpretation of the many embaras-\nsing situations that confronted him.\nCohan reveals all of his characters in\na mysterious way, usually in the twi-\n\n\n\n\n\naudience but it pleases and interests\nall. The methods used were slightly\nhigher than usual, and each single in\ndepreciation added to the mystery of\nthe farce, and before we knew it, a\ntheft, murder, a pack of lies, were\nall committed-Oh yes, and few\nhearts broken. Peter, the hermit\n(Sam Bjone) was as pathetic figure\nas he was an actor. Sam added much\nto the SHADY\" dealing that took\nplace, by his uncanny chuclde in the\nghost scene, and his 'fhermit-like\" re-\nmarks.\n\n\nMisfortunes befell Magee (Cos-\ntello) as soon as he reached the Inn\nto write his story. His innocent pre-\nparatories to always elk things\" for\nhis lady fair, Mary Norton( Virginia\nKells,) was the only real source of\nany laughter in the production. Vir-\nginia was a charming newspaper re-\nporter, and proved to be \"promis-\ning\" help to Magee. She supported\nher part with enthusiasm, skill, and\ngrace. Harold Claseman offered an\nearnest, subdued and understanding\npicture of Quinby, the caretaker. Ly-\nla Ellison, played as Mrs. Quinby, and\nshe gave old \"Lij\" plenty to think\nabout. \"Jim Corgan,\" (Herman He\nDIM thought he comprehended life,\nbut found out the reward most crook\ned politicians eventually come to, is\nthe road Of disappointment and re-\nmorse. Herman acted his part with\na firm and thorough comprehension of\nit's value. Vione Steege, as Myra\nThornhill, gave a touch of real COM\nedy in her \"wise remarks\" to Hay\ndin (Jerome Johnson,) the railroad\nmagnate. She made a good real\nolive\" ghost; especially good was her\n\"death\" scene, since the gun Max\nJohn McDermond) used would not\nexplode. Max might have made it a\nlittle more melodramatic if he had\nused the pistol as club instead of\na fire arm. Max and Haydn worked\nwell together, and were ably assist\ned by their righthand man, Bland,\nplayed by Philip Steiner. It's hard\nto imagine Philip thrashing anyone\nbut Cohan had US believing Bland in\ntended to. There were the usual\nnumber of fights and arguments, and\neach one was characterized by shoot\ning and the shrill screams of the\nwomen, especially Mrs. Rhodes,\nGamma Robischon). Anna was sim\ncere, pleasant widow, and we were all\nvery happy to learn that she didn't\neventually marry the crooked mayor.\n\n\nCarl Fischer added the police\nchiefs part, and he was decided sue\ncess. Ably assisted by patrolmen,\nII\n/. the chief rounded up the entire \"ring\nof frauders,\" and was just about\nready to make a fine get away, when\nthe owner of the resort Edwin Gray,\nmade his debut, and the audience\nwas handed real surprise. The play\nwas SO finely propelled that one didn't\nrealize the utter impossibilities of\nmost of the situations, and the \"fixed\"\nnature of most of the action. To\nfind the whole affair was a put up\nJob merely to teach the novelist how\nilliterary. and trashy were his works,\none felt very much fooled because of\nthe cleverly hidden motive behind the\nwhole production.\n\n\nThe entire play was a clever piece\nof work. Miss Knickerbocker, the\ncoach, through intensive training and\nhard work, produced one OF the best\nproductions given here. She deserves\nmany compliments on her excellent\naccomplishment and her skill in pro-\nducing plays is recognized fact. This\nlast presentation was one as human\nas was the problem it presented. It\ndidn't require any excessive mental\nagility nor especial receptiveness.\nArgument was offered in dramatic\nform by means of continually excit-\ning story, well acted. No one failed\nto feel the mental stimulus Of \"Seven\n\n=== 16_1911-01-05_pna_sn88077573_00237283727_1911010501_0423.json\nThe Marion daily mirror. (Marion, Ohio) 1892-1912 · 1911-01-05\ncrops: cached\nheadline: spooks Give WRONG TIP.\n---\nColumbus, o., Jan. 5.-Contrary to\na message from the spirit world\nwhich Anna e. Baison, Dayton Spir.\nitualistic medium, is reported to have\nreceived the night previous to the\ndecision, the medium has lost her\ncase in the supreme court and the\ntitle to a $4,000 Dayton house and lot\nwas given to DR.. Charles A. Pauley OF\nCincinanti, Notwithstanding Mrs. Bal\nson asserted the property had been\ndecided to her by DR.. Pauley's father.\nthe late Milton r. Pauley, prior to\nhis death.\n\n=== 1_1887-01-13_p1_sn86092392_00414187304_1887011301_0266.json\nCeredo advance. [volume] (Ceredo, W. Va.) 1885-1939 · 1887-01-13\ncrops: cached\nheadline: HEADLESS HORSEMAN.\n\nBullots and Such Havo no Eject\n\n\nUpon Him.\n\nEighty workm.o Driven Out by y Knight\n\n\n~ Apparition in tho Mountains.\n---\nWHEELING, w. Va, Jan. For some\ntime stories have been in circulation con-\ntanning u supposed ghost which haunts n\nlumber camp ON Cheat river, In Tucker\nCounty, this State, but like all ghost\nstories they caused9nly smiles. Recently,\nhowever, the entire force OF choppers and\nlaborers at the camp left In body and\ndrow their pay, refusing 90 return 90\nwork. There were over eighty employees,\nand colorcd cook was the rst 20\nleave. He sworo that ono morning be-\nfore daylight, when he was cooking break\nfast, hcedless horsemhn, with gigantic\naxe ON his shoulder. fed through tho\nwoods making no noise. Tho men ridi.\nculed him. In fow days u party OF\nchoppers reported seeing tho same phe-\nnomenon, and later Il appeared repeatedly,\nboth at night and In the day. The men\nwere employed by Henry a. Davis a Bro,\nOF keyscr. Men sent out by the firm say\nthey can not account for the thing. Shots\nfired at IL have no effect Tho supcrsti-\nuous fright OF the men has spread far and\nwide, aud this weeks Pioneer OF 1t\nGeorge whose editor visited the camp.\ngives the story In detail, aud says any Wis.\nitor can satisfy himself by sight and hear\ning, as the apparition makes peculiar\nwhining noise as IL glidcs through tho for\nest. Men have tried In vain TO employ now\nworkmcn TO take tho places OF those who\nfed.\n\n=== 23_1942-12-10_pna_sn88085187_00200291785_1942121001_0706.json\nThe Tacoma times. [volume] (Tacoma, Wash.) 1903-1949 · 1942-12-10\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nwere caught with ships and\nplanes huddled in the open like\nsitting ducks. inviting slaughter.\n\n\nAnd despite that horrible ex-\nsmple's ample warning. at Manila\nour planes were caught the same\nway. spelling a second disaster.\nPhilippine defense was left mostly\n70 native levies so scantily trained\nthat often they broke under fire.\nEquipment was always scanty,\nfrequently outmoded. For which\neomplacent and captious generals\nand admirals and Manila politicos\nwere to blame.\n\n\nPEOPLE AT FAULT\n\n\nWashington politicos were i'm\nmediately to blame for the stove\npipe cannon I saw at the Army\nmaneuvers then just ending, but\nprimarily to blame were the COM\nplacent American people. That\nsudden discovery made then fight\ning mad, and aroused trainees\nsnarled as they drove bayonet\ninto dummy. Pearl Harbor has\nresurrected the American fighting\nspirit to inspire the best trained\narmy we ever sent into war\nand the best led.\n\n\nAs never before in peace. we\nhave trained generals. In those\npost-Pearl Harbor maneuvers\nEisenhower and Clark proved\nthemselves as chiefs of staff,\nFrendenhall as corps COM\nmander, Patton as tank COM\nmander. They learned to handle\nmen in the field and the men\nlearned to handle themselves in\nmountain, jungle, desert, shore\nlandings - as Africa shows.\n\n\nThey learned new tactics COM\nbining individual initiative with\nteamwork. They mastered new\nequipment now streaming from\nproduction lines that a year ago\nseemed clogged with inertia and\nred tape.\n\n\nToday Africa and the Solomons\nshow that we have the best wea-\npons in the world In the new\nCarand rifle, carbine, los nm.\nhowitzer, medium tank and newer\ndevices for be-blitzing the blitz-\nkrieg.\n\n\nAS for personal equipment, an\n\n=== 16_1871-09-01_pna_sn85033019_00414215531_1871090101_0138.json\nDodgeville chronicle. [volume] (Dodgeville, Wis.) 1862-current · 1871-09-01\ncrops: cached\nheadline: SandalphOn.\n\nBY HENRY W. LoNGPELLow.\n---\nBY HENRY W. LONGPELLow.\nHave you read in the Talmud of old\nIn the legends the Rabbins have told\nOf the limitless realms of the air:\nHave you read 1.-THE marvellous story\nOf Sani alphon, the Angel of Glory.\nSandalphon, the Angel OF Prayer?\nHow, erect, at the outermost gates\nOf the City Celestial he waits,\n\n\nWith his feet on the ladder to light.\nThat, crowded with angels unnumbered,\nBy Jacob, was seen. as he slumbered\nAlone in the desert at night?\nThe Angels of Wind and of Fire.\nChant only one hymn, and expire\n\n\nWith the songs irresistible Stress\nExpire in their rapture and wonder,\nAs harp strings are broken asunder\n\n\nBy the music they throb tO,eXpress.\nBut serene in the rapturous throng,\nUnmOved by the rush of the song,\n\n\nWith eyes unimpasslOned and slow.\nAmong the dead angels, the deatblesS\nSandalphon stands listening breathless,\nTo sounds that ascend from below\nFrom the spirits on earth that adore.\nFrom the souls that entreat and implore\nIn the frenzy and passion OF prayer\nFrom the hearts that are broken with losses\nAnd weary with dragging the crosses\nToo heavy for mOrtals to bear.\nAnd he gathers the prayers as he stands.\nAnd they plunge into. flowers In his hands,\nInto garlands ofpurple and red;\nAnd beneath the great arch of the portal,\nThrough the streets of the City Immortal,\nIs waited the fragrance they shed.\nIt is but legend I know\nA fable, phantom, a show\n\n\nOft the ancient Rabbinical lore;\nYet the old medieval tradition,\nThe beautiful, strange superstition,\n\n\nBut haunts me and holds me the more.\n: When look from my window at night.\nAnd the welkin above is all white,\n\n\nAll throbbing and painting with stars\nAmong them majestic is standing,\nSandalphon. the angel, expanding\n\n\nHis pinions in nebulous bars.\nAnd the legend I feel is part\nfthe hunger and thirst of the heart,\nThe frenzy and fire of the brain,\nThat grasps at the fruitage forbidden,\nThe golden pomgranateS of Eden,\n\n\nTo quietits fever and pain.\n\n=== 13_1922-11-05_p1_sn83045217_00295860832_1922110501_0208.json\nGreat Falls tribune. [volume] (Great Falls, Mont.) 1921-current · 1922-11-05\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \nNEW RECORDS\n\nBRUNSWICK\n---\nTwo master works for piano SOLO\nwhich arc about as wildly contrasted\n\n\nas any two numbers could be are On\n\n\nWings of Song and '\"Tarantella Ven\nezia j. Napoli,\" played by Leopold COD\n| owsky for Brunswicks November re\nleases. The soaring melody OF the\npolished. gentle Felix Mendeissohn Is\nthe opposite pole TO List S dazziihs\nTarantella dance of Venice and Naples.\nPLAYED the world over. music lovers\n\n\nand students will eagerly welcome God\nowsky's interpretations. Never has he\n\n\nmore convincingly demonstrated his SUI\nIpreme command of tone and technlc, Tor\nin one he is the poet of the piano, whlie\n\n\nin the wild tarantella he is q relncar~\n\n\nnation of Lszt himself. :,\n\n\nFriedrich Fradkin's specialized work\n\n\nin the artistic presentation, through\n\n\nthe medium of his expressive violin.\nof popular lyrics has found instant n\nwide public favor. To hear Fradkln\nsing upon his violin Lovely Iucerne\",\nand Three O'clock in the Morning\nwhich he played for the Brunswick No\nvember releases, is to get a new ana\nheightened conception of the beauties IN\npopular songs. This record is not to\nbe surpassed for universal entertain\nment-it will give loads of JOY and hap\nIillitHS NIISItSC1 IL 6otNs\n\n\nThee Karle, tenor, knows the art of\nrecord making. and the happy wedlock\nof two lyrics for Brunswick's Novem-\nDer releases, is the evidence thereof.\nRobinson's sons \"Snowy-Breasted\nPearl offers this popular artist ex-'\npressional opportunities in which he\nand Tom Moore's beloved words,\nsoft in the Silly Night are charged\nwith new beauty, not only through Ste\nvenson's melodic setting. but, as well.\nby virtue of Karle's lovely voice, and\nhis feeling for text and music.\n\n\nThese danccs-\"Tricks.\" by the gift\ned Confrey, and Dancing Fool\" by\nTed Snyder. are already known to a\nBrunswick audience. They have\nunusual merit tanally and rhythmically,\none as a fox trot, the other as a SHIN\none-step. The Isham Jones' orches-\nira make them concert numbers for the\nNovember releases. at the\nsame time they heighten their dance\nmesmerism.\n\n\nBlue\" and the Haunting Blues\"\nwith all the weepy walls and minor\nare fox trots that you must\nnot miss. Bennie Kruger and his or\nGuido themselves in these\ndances. November releases, and even\nthen do not run out of blueing. For\nthe real thing in the modern manner,\nfor volume, color and brillianey these\ndances are lit\" and their success is\nassured since Bonnie Krueger's\norchestra records them.\n\n=== 13_1935-12-30_p8_sn83045462_00280602632_1935123001_0660.json\nEvening star. · 1935-12-30\ncrops: cached\nheadline: The Ghostly Door Knocker.\n---\n\"What are you going to say in your\nnext speech?\"\n\n\nI'll refer to Dickens\" said Senator\nSorghum.\n\n\nChristmas Carol?\"\n\n\nPerhaps; although I'm not sure\nwhether I'm due to figure as scrooge\nioyously indoors or as Marley fastened\noutside as the knocker.\"\n\n=== 10_1913-12-27_pna_sn96076817_00415669513_1913122701_0143.json\nThe diamond drill. (Crystal Falls, Iron County, Mich.) 1887-1996 · 1913-12-27\ncrops: cached\nheadline: SYNOPSIS.\n---\n\n\n\nheard quarreling with Aanska. During\nns excitement strange woman who\nElves her name a. Rosalie re Grange. APP\nNe street all OF Mrs. Moore's boarders.\nIhclndinE Miss Estrilla. an invalid. who\nwas confined 10 the room she occupied\nana whose brother was fsvorits among\nths other boarderH\n\n\nCHAPTER II-Wade IL arrested as he\n= about 10 leave the country. Mrs. lie\nGrange. who. while plying her trade as\ntrance medium. had aided Police In\nspsetor Martin MCGEE several times. calls\n~ bis ONCE 70 tell what she knows OF\nhnnska. widow OF the murdered man,\nwhose existence had been unknown. APP\npears\n\n\nCHAPTER Hints Hanska says she\nhad lett her husband and discloses the\nfact that Wade represented her and Wis\nIN an EFFORT to settle their afairs. She\nadmits Wade was ia love with her Mrs.\n\n\ngrange. sympathizing with Mrs.\nHAnska, takes the latter and her COM\nsss. '0\"' D'''y D'ro'r\" Lane nome win\nCHAPTER INVADE IL held by the\neoroner's jury for the death OF Hanska.\nCHAPTER v-Tommy North, who had\nbeen held by the police, Is released and\nbecomes infatuated at once with Betsy\nBarbara.\n\n\nCHAPTER vI-DrIyen by the belief\nthat Betsy Barbara loves Estrilla Tommy\nNorth gets drunk and Ia discovered by\nBetsy Barbara\n\n\nCHAPTER With. next morning\nTommy apologies 10 Betsy Barbara and\nThomas w. North Advertising Agency.\nCHAPTER yIII-Mrs. La Grange. with\nInspector MCGEE. examines the house\nwAsrs Hanska was killed and nnds on\nthe IRE eecrAe outside Hanska's window\nred shoe button. which she conceals.\nCHAPTER IX-afrs. lie Grange secretly\nsr~rsnes The shoes OF her boarders In\nsearch OF one the red button will nt She\npretends 10 go into a trance IN Miss Es\nirilla's room snd communes with spirits\nCHAPTER X.-Rosalie secures from In\nIpector MCGEE the services OF an Italian\ndetective, 10 work under her direction\nCHAPTER XII Resale finds evidence\n20 show that the Estrillas' real name IL\nPereE and that they formerly lived In\nPort OF Spain.\n\n\nCHAPTER XII-Rosalie goes into an\nother trance In Miss EstrillR's room and\ngains the young womsn's confidence.\n\n\nCHAPTER XIII-Rosalie holds further\nseances and strengthens Miss Estrlllas\nbelief that she is talking through Resale\nwith the spirits OF her departed relatives.\nCHAPTER XVIII succeeding seances\nResale leads Miss Estrilla 90 believe she\nis talking with the spirit OF John Hanska\nand gets information that leads her 10\nprepare for a supreme test.\n\n\nI'm here, said hosare lie Grange.\nTO do what can for you an your sic.\nter both. Now come. I tell you--or will\nYOU keep on bein' a fool?\" At this\ndash OF mental cold water, he rose.\nResale walked close behind him,\nready 70 support him should he stag.\nger. Outside. park footpoliceman\nwalked slowly down the path. Estril.\nIa saw him, started. hesitated.\n\n\nNot unless you make scene!\"\ncried Rosalie. anticipating his thought\nI'm not arresting you--can't you un.\nderstAnd thatg\" She hurried him TO a\nlonely park bench, half hidden In the\nshrubbery. When she turned to look\nhim full I'm the face again. his color\nwas normal; he had regained his grip.\nAnd he spoke with a touch OF his old\nboyish insouciance.\n\n\nThis IL a little melodrama YOU are\nstaging, Mrs. lie Grange7 Am the\nhero or the viliain1\"\n\n\nIl expected you 10 be suspicious an\ntry TO blum this through\" said Rosslle\nin her most matter-offact tone, that\nwhy | stole this note an brought II\nhere\" She had been keeping her\nhands In her nun She drew them out,\nnow. and handed him the vital paper\n\n\n81 am telling TO the police all I know\nof my part and my brothers part In\nthe death OF Capt. John H. Hanska |\nhave confessed that we followed him\nto America 10 get my jewels. and that\nIl was my brother Juan who appeared\n20 have stabbed him.\n\n\n\"MARGARITA PEREZ\"\n\n\nHe read t. Az he looked up he was\nstill master OF himself but Resale\ncould perceive behind his mask a kind\nOF vibration, an inner agitation or all\nhis nerves But his will still mas\ntered his voice.\n\n\n\"Margarita Perez-who is she?\"\n\n\nshe is your sister. You are Juan\nPerez-not Estrilla. You are from\nfoTt OF spain. You came here 10 iol-\nlow Captain Hanska--\"\n\n\nWhere did you hear this?\" inquired\nEstrilla. with a pitiful attempt to put\nsarcasm into his voice.\n\n\nII have listened to her confession\"\nreplied Resale calmly. she told the\npolice--after she sigued that paper\nhow you went into Captain Hanskas\nroom at night to get your family jew\nels, how that trick alarm on his strong\nbon woke him up. an how you killed\nhim\n\n\nBut Juan Estrilla had leaped UP now\nas though his nerves would be denied\nno longer.\n\n\nYou are here 10 betray mel know\n4 nowi\" be said.\n\n\nr suspected this trouble Was com\n\n=== 6_1826-11-04_pna_sn83021391_00340588010_1826110401_0124.json\nLiterary cadet, and Saturday evening bulletin. [volume] (Providence, R.I.) 1826-1827 · 1826-11-04\ncrops: cached\nheadline: 40! 'tis the curse ofloye, and still approved,\nThat women cannot love, where they are loved.\n\n\nts /.\n---\nThat women cannot love, where they loved\"\n\n\nWINTERS TALE.\nLast Thursday morning, as we were entering our\nOffice, we found young man standing at the door,\nwho, after asking for the Office Of the Cadet, and\nreceiving sr eply, handed US the following poem.\nwhich he requested us to publish, as he had made\nup his mind TO quit country, where resided one,\ncruel and unkind as his faithless Sally. He was\nclad in pair ofleather insrpressibs, the appear\nance of which indicated great antiquity, and Of ha-\nwing been worn by his ancestors, from the days Of\nJack the Giant Killer, down to the present genera\nton. The other part OF his attire was in strict con.\nformity with his inexpressibles-and taking him all\nand in all, he was, certainly, an odd kind Of fish,\nWe promised TO comply with his wishes, and ifthe\nlines should ever reach his unknd Sally. we hope\nshe will again take him into favor. But 10 the\npoem, and here il is and a more love-lorn ditty\nnever emanated from the most melancholy IA\naUEs:\n\n\nWith thee III hoped to waste the pleasing days\nTill in thy arms aye of joy was pass'd,\nThen old with love, nsensibly decay,\n\n\nAnd on thy bosom gently breathe my last.\nI'd scorn the Lydian Rivers golden wave\n\n\nAnd all the vulgar charms Of human life\nonly wished TO live Paulina's slave,\n\n\nAud when long had servd her, call her-acie.\n\n\n\nIII seek some lonely church or dreary hall,\n\n\nWhere fancy paints the lingering taper blue,\nWhere damps hang mould'rng on the vy'd wall,\nAnd sheeted ghosts drink up the midnight dew.\nThere leagued with hopeless anguish and despair,\nA while in silence o er my fate repine,\nThen with a long farewell TO love and care\n\n\nTo kindred dust my wearis3 limbs recline.\nWilt thou, Paulina, shed a gracious tear,\n\n\nDer the cold grave, where all my SORROWS rest,\nSrcw kindred flowers, applaud my love sincere,\n\n\nAnd bid the turf lie easy on my breast.\n\n\nIcHABOD HoCKELTV.\nThus have we complied with the request of Mr..\nICHABoD HoCKELTY, and assure him that we much\nadmire his verses, although they were written some\nseventy years ago, by one TOBIAS SMoLLETT, who\nprobably once wore the leather inexpressbes, now\nin possession of the Hockehy family. As poor\nlCHA BOD is melancholy swain we shall give him\na word of advice, and in doing so, we shall quote\nthe pathetic language Of POLLY SMALL Fay to\nGREGORY GRUNT, as thus set forth, in English\nprint by the aforesaid GREooRY: -\" Well, | was\non the eve Of my departure for Botany Bay; but\nbefore left SALLY, would needs have a parting\nkiss. found her at the stalls at Bilngsgate, and\nblubbered, so6bed, and whined when SALLY,\nthe dear creature, as she reclined her head up\non her pretty little hand, and her elbow keg Of\npicked oysters, gave me one of her killing gances\nand exclaimed-O twas enough TO break my heart\n:\" Gregory, Gregory now don't be after making\nadd fool Of yourself\n\n=== 8_1874-06-23_p1_sn84026788_00202192348_1874062301_0104.json\nSpirit of Jefferson. [volume] (Charles Town, Va. [W. Va.]) 1844-1948 · 1874-06-23\ncrops: cached\nheadline: FINANGIAL.\n\nGold, nsed as Money, Is Tie aCo,ae-Sonea\nOf Monarchy aud Despotisn; and tie\nCause at ignorance. Crime, and Rzite\nS1asesy Throughout The Earhl\n---\nOF Monarchy AID Despotism; and tie\n\n\nCause at ignorance. Crime, and Rzite\n\n\nSlavery Throughout the EarA1\n\n\nEditor Of tke Spirit of Jeferson.-As q.\ns. Grant, President of the United States, in\naddition 70 his veto of the currency BILL has\nmore recently-undef the thin disguise of an\nexpression of his views and opinions of a\nproper and suitable medium of exchange\nissued IN fact his stdecree\"-as his prototype\nNebuchaduezzar did long before him-that-\nland without, too, '.the sound of the cernet,'\nflute, sackbut, p%saltery, and duleimer, and all\nkinds of. music\") ..every nan shall fall down\nandNWorship the Golden Image\" It is high\ntime that the people Of the United States\nshouldtake 1t under considerations, and decide,\nas a brave and unterrifed people, what they\nwill do, even in the face of the other part of\nthe .decree\" the \"burning fiery furnacem\n\n\nIn order, then, that the people may have,\nbefore them. the true facts of the case, and\nthe cause of their disastrous condition, and\nthe proper remedies, in the first instance, to\nrelieve them, l send you the following letter\nof DR.. P. P. Field which, hope you will lay\nbefore then5 as it touches all these points\nbriefly aud clearly; and may, indeed, be used\nas a declaration Of our grievances, aud of our\n\n\nA10SpSaNS1SS1 WV ID SoihS w NhO\nmoneyed Oligarchy, and their bribed and be-\ngifted tools whether in Congress, ON the\nBench, in the press, ia the palpit, or else\nwhere.\n\n\nThe letter is headed t'labor and capital\"\nand the dullest of mortals can readily see that,\nwhen in the exchanges and transactions be-\ntween labor aud the productions of labor and\nmoney, there is a leverage of sstwo hundred\nper CEN\" in favor of s'money' and against\nlabor and its productions, that this ..is a suf>\nfcient difference to carry the entire surplus\nproduction of the nation from the hands of\nthose who produce them, into the hands of\nthe money lenders\"\n\n\nWe must have u money that will yraduaHy\n\"redistribute the property which is now SO\nunequally divided\" and. also, \"preyent a re-\ncurrence of similar conditions in the future\"\nThe peoples money, based equally on all prop\nerty, and labor, and the peoples credit, will\neffect these just and desirable results. They\ncan never be effected as long as Gold is King,\nand his subjects uphold the mons,rocity that\nthe \"King's chat is better than poor folks\ncorn\" Now, Mr.. Phelps, of New Jersey,\nasserts this, when he says Gold is the only\nbasis of paper money, aud when no we'll\nknows that the Convertible chat\" issued by\nhis King has always proved to be mere \"chaf'\nwhen he has been called upon to redeem it.\nSuspension\" was the cry of his kingship;\nand ever will be-\n\n\nWe want, and must have, a just and fair\nmoney. We cannot have it, as long as we\nprefer one mans production and labor over\nanother's, even if that production be gold\nTo do SO, gives this difference of two hundred\nper cent\" in favor of gold, and would do so\nin favor of any other specially favored 'com-\nmodify\" No commodity is fit for money\nIn the language of one of the ablest Gnanciers\nof this or any other country, Wallace P.\nGroom. ''The use of Gold, or other merchan-\ndisc, as money, is a barbarism, unworthy of\nthe age\"\n\n\nThe use of Gold, as money, has beggared\nthe world and reduced it to despotiem. If\ncontinued here, it will beggar, and reduce us\nto the same condition. Away with it, say I,\nand up with the peoples money away, too,\nwith all the tyrants, at home and abroad, who\nwe'll know that gold-with that difference of\ntwo hundred per cent\" against labor-has,\nalways, been the Cornerstone\" of monarchy\nand despotism, and will be here, too, the\ncornerstone\" of monarchy and despotism !;\nunless We '~reject\" it, and substitute for it the\nCornerstone\" of Labor; and that is the\n'~'people's money of solid values, instead of\nthe \"King's chaff.\"\n\n\nJNo. A. THOMSON.\n\n\nCrr,r;k Do;rk w VI. t.. OIL 1917\n\n=== 12_1904-05-19_pna_sn89064489_00393340253_1904051901_0167.json\nThe Sauk Centre herald. (Sauk Centre, Stearns County, Minn.) 1886-current · 1904-05-19\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nhanging about the room that would\ncast jus. such shadow. | discovered\nnothing that was even remotely salts\nfactory, and convinced that the arm\nwas supernatural, L keyed myself up\nand took a lamp close to the chimney.\nNo marks of any kind were to be seen.\nWith the activity of search my full\nself-control returned, and also, instead\nof a lessening, as would be natural,\nthere was deepening of the conviction\nthat there was some important infor-\nnation for me in the movements OF\nthe arm.\n\n\n81 took out my knife, and carefully\npricked all over the place where the\nhand entered the wall. On one end OF\na brick found circular hole, as if it\nhad been ground out, but now filled.\nwith dust. In this ring was sunk,\nand when I got my finger through it\nyou can imagine my feelings, for it\nhad certainly been put there to open an\naperture. If I pulled, what wotild be\nthe. result? Would door swing open\nand disclose skeleton? 1t took some\nnerve to pull, but Twas never. given to\n'hesitancy, and. SO gave a jerk.\n\n\nNothing stirred. i tried again, with\nstrong, steady movement. At this\ntwo bricks came away in my hand, up\nheld by the ring: They had been fas-\ntened together on the inside, and the\nring was bolted through. I tried, tc\nget the. light to shine in the opening,\nbut could not Then / ventured with.\nmy hand and arm, as had seen the\nghostly arm do, but l could not reach\nanything.\n\n\nIl was much excited, AMI determined\nto tear. the wall away. That hole was\nmade for some purpose, and 1 was go\ning to learn what l brought some\ntools, and went to work. l supposed\nthat l should have to go to the level\nof the floor, but when l had taken out\nabout two feet two bricks wide l came\nto a shelf, upon which rested a small\nwooden box Eagerly l drew this out,\nexpecting to find money. for it came to\nme as obvious that the thinking sound\nmade by the ghostly arm was designed\nto tell that there was money within\nfor me. But as l took up the box my\nspirits fell. 1t could not have weighed\nmore than half pound.\n\n\n= \"The reaction from nay hope was so\ngreat that l went to the table slowly,\nand opened the boz with feeling\nsomething like disgust. A note was\nlying beside a small bottle. This\nunfolded, and read with slight curl\nosity, which became evaporation by\nthe time l was through. 1t merely said\nthat the bottle in the box contained an\nimprisoned genie in liquid form, whose\npurpose was to benefit the finder by\nturning all iron upon which it was\npoured into gold. .. =\n\n\n\"A hoax! 1 exclaimed, in anger from\nmy overstrained nerves. Then\nstopped short How had found that\nbottle? BY means more occult than\ncould explain. And there. was no de-\nnying anything, for box, bottle and\nnote lay before me. If the ghostly arm\ncould do so much, what might not the\nliquid do? l sobered down. The whole\nthing was plainly of a piece, and hav-\ning gone so far, it might be we'll to go\nthe entire length.\n\n\nMY ardor revived. l took the bot\ntie up gingerly, and held it to the light.\n1t was filled with blush liquor which\nseemed to emit little sparks when agi-\ntated. Could it turn iron to gold? My\nhead was fairly dizzy with the thought\nl must try. $685. ,-.. .\n\n\nTaking it over to the brick hearth.\nl opened it, and poured few drops on\nan ak which I had brought into use in\ndemolishing the chimney. Instantly d\nsteaming vapor arose, sending off a\ntremendous: heat. The helVe was\nburned up in the twinkling of an eye.\nand l jumped back in amazement. By\nthe time looked again, an ak of gold\nlay in place of the iron, and the brick\nwas powdered half an inch deep.\n\n\n\"As soon as the AK could be examined\nand satisfied myself that it really had\nbeen changed. went wild. Every\nthing in the house that was of iron\nspeedily brought and made over into\ngold. So eager was that I did nor\nnotice the liquid until realized that it\nwas all expended. What a fool had\nbeen not to husband it! Yet not Alto\ngether, for scattered about was a for\ntune in gold, which gazed upon while\nresting for few minutes.\n\n\nNow, there isn't any use in drawing\nthis story out, as you have the main\nfact. concealed the gold-my gift\nfrom the ghostly armand afterward\nturned it into securities through the\nproper channels.\n\n\nThe finish is given in a word. I was\na rich man, Mr.. Atherton was a willing\nfather-in-law, and I have the loveliest\nwife in the world. You are both in\nvited to dine with me tomorrow even\ning, and enjoy her welcome to my\nfriends ,>r. 49,.\n\n\nIA short pause followed the conclu.\nsion of Middleton's story. Then Jarvis\nsaid, That's very interesting way to\ntell us how you won your wife by max\ning money, but, er-\" -'.\n\n\n\"Wen?\" ........\n\n\nThat's just it,\" broke in Masters.\nwhen we remember how you used to\nspin yarns, you know\"\n\n\nGentlemen\" interrupted Middleton.\n\n\n\n\n=== 1_1868-12-03_pna_sn84027398_00279529418_1868120301_0611.json\nThe Tipton advertiser. [volume] (Tipton, Cedar Co., Iowa) 1856-1962 · 1868-12-03\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nPlate; and more than Atty Wood-cuts Of\nFahsions, Embroideries and other Pat\nterms. The stories are Of the highest\nclass. \"Perfidious Planchette,\" Is the\nwe'll timed title Of one. Mrs. Ann S.\nStephens new novel, opens in the\nmost mastery manner. The Author of\nmargaret Howth,' has also a powerful\ntale. We do not see how any lady can\ndo without Peterson\" Nop is the\ntime to subscribe for 1869! Terms 500 a\nyear. To clubs, four copies for $500,\nwith premium engraving, \"The Star\nOf Bethlehem\" to the person getting up\nthe club, or eight copies for $1,000, with\nboth the engraving and an extra\ncopy for premiums. Specimens sent\ngiraffe to persons getting up clubs. Ad-\ndress CHAs. J. PETERSON, 3O6 Chestnut\nStreet Philadelphia, Pa. -\n\n=== 7_1891-05-02_pna_sn82014635_00237286716_1891050201_0472.json\nThe Wichita daily eagle. [volume] (Wichita, Kan.) 1890-1906 · 1891-05-02\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nhe had received letters which required\nhis immediate departure, but that he\nwould not deprive Miss Cameron of her\nseance, and would meet me at her moth.\ner's house in the evening at the hour\nwhich had been named.\n\n\nAt that time and place found him,\napparently making himself quite at\nhome; and presently opening a small\nbox which he had brought with him he\ndrew from it the same crystal with\nwhich he had operated upon me.\n\n\nSeating himself in front of her as she\nreclined easily in her crm chair he COLA\nmenced the mysterious passes with his\ncrystal. watched her closely, and as\nhe moved it slowly in front of her\ncould perceive that she gradually grew\npale; then her eyelids dropped, and she\nwas apparently in a sound sleep. Her\nmother called to her, touched her, and\neven used some gentle violence to awak-\nen- her. but without the slightest appar-\nent effect.\n\n\nPointing the crystal at her Stolberg\ndrew silently backward toward the door,\nwhen, to our establishment, the sleeping\nfigure rose, or rather glided after him,\nout of the door, into the hall, down the\nstairs. and as he opened the front door\nStolberg called to Mrs. Cameron, who\nstood with me at the head of the stairs\nwatching the results of this wonderful\ninfluence:\n\n\n\"You see, madame, she would follow\nme anywhere\" and, as though to prove\nit. she passed out of the door, which\nStolberg shut, and. TO my horror, heard\nhim lock it after him.\n\n\nfew down the two flights of stairs\ninto the basement, my brain turning\nmad, it seemed TO me, and reached the\nsidewalk by the lower door just in time\nTO see carriage turning at full speed\nthe next corner.\n\n\nReturning to Mrs. Cameron | found\nher in a swoon, cut of which, as she\nawakened to sensibility, she passed into\ncommissions and at midnight was corpse.\nMeanwhile the police had been Informed,\nmessages sent in all directions, but OF\nStolberg or his unhappy victim / have\nnever heard since.-Bufalo News.\n\n=== 7_1866-06-26_pna_sn85025007_00415663596_1866062601_0202.json\nAlexandria gazette. [volume] (Alexandria, D.C.) 1834-1974 · 1866-06-26\ncrops: cached\nheadline: \n---\nGORGE E. WHITE,\n\" Liberty Hall,\n\n\nSO and 81, Cameron street,\nInvites attention to his very extensive and\nchoice stock of\n\n\nGROCER les,\nembracing Teas, Cotfees, Sugars, Spices, Hams\nBacon. Flour Candles Soap, Molasses, Butter,\nCheese, Canned Meats and Fruit