her brow\n1141\t\n1142\t\n1143\tPinch her pale check\n1144\t\n1145\t\n1146\t'a'e ana much pusalon,-\n1147\t\n1148\t\n1149\tarray her lated form\n1150\t\n1151\t\n1152\tIa roarseiy patched am.\n1153\t- l,e' 'Hefe be nolaIng bur hls Ioy.\n1154\to'e' her forth aud deck her nm,\n1155\t'''1 you Sia'l pat 1t 10 The proof indeed\n1156\t% change his station IN he yo,1\n1157\t\n1158\t\n1159\tThai he shall see\n1160\t\n1161\t\n1162\t>\"o'e young ihlrgs who climb oh.,,, -,\n1163\tu t'\"' records Of hs wealth and nan us Knee\n1164\t'''.'o \"'e'Ilets with blm 70, L,s IL,\n1165\t\n1166\t\n1167\t.\"',P P\"aelef' On his .canlv ms sI'} dread.\n1168\t~ ~ many ualts 10 DIVIDE \"'1'\n1169\tr'e'efy sun ofcopC,,\n1170\tanu iHihet u reduce II. .u,Ii\n1171\t\n1172\t=== 28_1909-02-08_p9_sn85042462_00175035758_1909020801_0159.json\n1173\tLos Angeles herald. · 1909-02-08\n1174\tcrops: cached\n1175\theadline: DR.. WORCESTER\n1176\tTELLS OF WORK\n1177\tFOUNDER OF THE EMMANUEL\n1178\tMOVEMENT breaches\n1179\tCRUSADE HAS EXTENDED OVER\n1180\t\n1181\t\n1182\tENTIRE WORLD\n1183\tBegan with Fight in Boston Against\n1184\ttuberculosis and Was Confined\n1185\t\n1186\t\n1187\tto Pastors Special\n1188\t\n1189\t\n1190\tParish\n1191\t---\n1192\tSdllOu THAT IctASQ LIlS SSolllIl5 ScpdSleS y\n1193\tto the utmost.\n1194\t\n1195\t\n1196\tLong before the hour of service peo-\n1197\tpie began to gather. The parishioners\n1198\tcame in through the choir entrance\n1199\tbut the general public was not ad-\n1200\tnitted until ten minutes before the\n1201\topening Of the service. Many stood\n1202\tduring the entire service.\n1203\t\n1204\t\n1205\tDR.. Worcester, who is medium\n1206\tsized man with a face full of good will\n1207\tspoke in an easy, convincing, CONVEX\n1208\tsational tone. with no attempt what\n1209\tever at oratorical effects and with few\n1210\tif any, gestures He described how\n1211\the had started and carried on his work\n1212\tgiving a personal touch to the whole\n1213\taddress.\n1214\t\n1215\t\n1216\t\"I am much cheered at ethe interest\n1217\tmanifested in our work\" said DR.\n1218\tWorcester. 'It has been matter of\n1219\tsurprise that in this distant state\n1220\tthere has been as much interest taken\n1221\tas in any of the other states.\n1222\t\n1223\t\n1224\tThere is today a very interesting\n1225\treligious movement. The interest in\n1226\tour work is simply astonishing. This\n1227\twork has been carried on in the east\n1228\tfor about two years. When we began\n1229\twe had no idea of anything more than\n1230\tanother form of church work in our\n1231\town parish We had been interested\n1232\tin the work of fighting tuberculosis\n1233\tfor two years and we thought it pos\n1234\tsib1e to cooperate. but had no idea of\n1235\ttreating others than our own parish\n1236\ttoner's.\n1237\t\n1238\t\n1239\t1.-The idea was to treat our parish\n1240\ttoner's for nervous disorders. We have\n1241\tmade no effort to extend our work\n1242\talong this line.\n1243\t\n1244\t\n1245\tWork Is Worldwide\n1246\t\n1247\t\n1248\t''All over this country and in Eng\n1249\tland, Ireland, the Philippines. Hawaii,\n1250\tCentral Africa and South Africa are\n1251\tgroups of people engaged in the same\n1252\tform of service. Not long ago re-\n1253\tceived a letter from Japanese pro-\n1254\tfe@sor in Toxic, saying he had a class\n1255\tof 800 Japanese.\n1256\t\n1257\t\n1258\tct is a sign of the times. 1t would\n1259\thave been impossible to gain this\n1260\twidespread interest by advertising.\n1261\tWhen the time is ready for the idea\n1262\tit is only necessary to enunciate it\n1263\tand it will spread by its own vitality.\n1264\t\n1265\t\n1266\t\"This is one of the most interesting\n1267\treligious phenomena that has ever\n1268\tpassed over the country, and has been\n1269\tsaid to be compared only with the\n1270\t. first years of Christianity.\n1271\t\n1272\t\n1273\t''It seems to be a movement of the\n1274\tspirit of God and is spreading largely\n1275\toutside of the churches. Wherever we\n1276\tgo we find people who are dissatisfied\n1277\tand are trying to carry out the Chris\n1278\ttian religion.\n1279\t\n1280\t\n1281\t'It is our attempt to carry out the\n1282\tprinciples of the gospel of Christ. We\n1283\tbelieve that is all necessary for men.\n1284\tWe do not want to form a new sect\n1285\tor a new cult. but to go back to the\n1286\told religion. This religion of ours, we\n1287\tdo not get much good from it because\n1288\twe do not apply it to our lives. This\n1289\tsounds strange to say, but it is true.\n1290\t\n1291\t\n1292\t\"In religions behind which there is\n1293\tonly one personality, as in our own\n1294\treligion and Buddhism, there comes a\n1295\ttime when the founder is forgotten.\n1296\tThen it is necessary to go back to the\n1297\tfounder and study his life and princi-\n1298\tpies. Something of that kind came at\n1299\tthe time of the Protestant reformation,\n1300\tand, unless all signs fail, this is an\n1301\t'other reformation in the hearts of the\n1302\tpeople. The hearts of the people are\n1303\tstirred, and there is a vital movement\n1304\tat present.\n1305\t\n1306\t\n1307\tScientific Movement\n1308\t\n1309\t\n1310\talong with this there is an interest\n1311\ting scientific and psychological move\n1312\tment. We have learned through psy-\n1313\tchology many things. We have learned\n1314\tto draw on a source of power and to\n1315\tgain great physical endurance. Today\n1316\tit is possible and easy for me to do\n1317\ttwice the work that I did ten years\n1318\tago.\n1319\t\n1320\t\n1321\tThere is an ability in a man to\n1322\tdraw on a power iii excess of ordinary\n1323\twants. What the nature of that power\n1324\tis I cannot say. 1t is spiritual and\n1325\tis found within US. I believe the soul\n1326\tof man and the spirit of God inSepara-\n1327\tBILE. We are parts of God in his spir-\n1328\titual nature. In saying the kingdom\n1329\tof God is within u.s., Jesus meant the\n1330\tspirit Of God is within you.\n1331\t\n1332\t\n1333\tLet US consider how to tap this\n1334\treservoir. Take the terrible event OF\n1335\tthe San Francisco earthquake. That\n1336\tshows it in two ways. in the individual\n1337\tand in the community. | was per\n1338\tfectly amazed at the recuperation OF\n1339\tthat city. So much of the city has\n1340\tbeen. rebuilt, but if they had simply\n1341\tl~-nented that city would be a waste\n1342\tplace today. I was talking with a\n1343\tdoctor who came east soon after the\n1344\tdisaster. He had lost all, home, H.\n1345\tDrary, instruments and his patients\n1346\tHe simply said it would be his joy tC\n1347\tbegin again. Fate has no power over\n1348\tsuch people.\n1349\t\n1350\t\n1351\tIl have talked with many people\n1352\twho were in that disaster and have\n1353\tbeen told how they were on their feet\n1354\tal. day and did not feel fatigued. What\n1355\twas the cause? 1t was an unusual\n1356\tcircumstance that enabled the people\n1357\tto draw on a power which they are\n1358\tordinarily not able to reach.\n1359\t\n1360\t\n1361\tIn many events~of life, in the un-\n1362\tusual, we are able to draw on some\n1363\tsource of power and do what otherwise\n1364\twould be impossible. If we could only\n1365\tlearn how to make use of this at al\n1366\t4 times, how different it would be! Il\n1367\twe could make our life in Gods life\n1368\twhat blessing it would be to US!\n1369\t\n1370\t\n1371\tRare Moments in Life\n1372\t\n1373\t\n1374\t1--WE owe what moral and spiritua\n1375\tstrength we have through life to those\n1376\trare moments when God spoke to us\n1377\tand our hearts spoke to God. Wher\n1378\twe turn to Jesus Christ we see the\n1379\tdifference. Christ's life in God was\n1380\tnot an intermittent thing. In the Ser\n1381\tmon OH the Mount, we see how Jesus\n1382\tsought to find God in every event OF\n1383\tlife. The only stand that he was will\n1384\ting to propose to his disciples was\n1385\tBE ye therefore perfect, even as your\n1386\tFather in heaven is perfect\n1387\t\n1388\t\n1389\tLet me show what the realization OF\n1390\tGod in our lives would mean, anC\n1391\twhat it would do for US. First, it would\n1392\t\n1393\t=== 13_1836-05-06_pna_sn84024735_00414184091_1836050601_0452.json\n1394\tRichmond enquirer. [volume] (Richmond, Va.) 1815-1867 · 1836-05-06\n1395\tcrops: cached\n1396\theadline: \n1397\t---\n1398\tand that the movement intended is advisable.\n1399\t\n1400\t\n1401\t(SgnedD f. THORN,\n1402\t\n1403\t\n1404\tri. RAGGED,\n1405\t\n1406\t\n1407\tj. ROBERTS,\n1408\t\n1409\t\n1410\tc. \". SIMMS,\n1411\t\n1412\t\n1413\tCounter signcd) A. HOTC1IKiSS.\n1414\tNew OatEASs, April Give have not heard official\n1415\ty of any steps having yet bccu taken BY our Governor\n1416\tin regard TO ;he requiston of General Gaines\n1417\t\n1418\t\n1419\tIL does appear us if frontiers are really in dan.\n1420\tger ro,o Indian Disturbances aud the of\n1421\tGen Gaines warrants such belief, then humanity aud\n1422\tevery consideration OF policy, require that speedy action\n1423\tshould be made in complying with the request of the\n1424\tProclamation should at least be made and perbaps\n1425\tthe chvary of Louisiana would find ways and means 10\n1426\toeviate an empty treasury aud the dispersion Of Lee\n1427\tgislatvc body. We wait with anxiety some movement\n1428\tupon the Notwithstanding one connected with inte-\n1429\trests of highly important character\n1430\t\n1431\t\n1432\tIII we understand Alright the treaty between CO.\n1433\tverninent and Mexico any interference the part of\n1434\tthe Indian bcs residing within the Mexican territory\n1435\teither in iavonr of the Me or our own subjects is\n1436\tviolation of the treaty Ii the Cainanchcs have then\n1437\tjoined with Santa Anna, it will be- as we deem the\n1438\tbounden duty Of Gen. Gaines to pass the Sabine and\n1439\tenter the Mexican country Unnecessary, put down The\n1440\tSayuge-and this his Of duty, independent\n1441\this patriotism, will prompt him to do.--iaiictin.\n1442\t\n1443\t\n1