ICATIONS hsve at\n/ length been opened between tie French aud Italian\ngovernments with view 10 rid Paris of The pest OF\nthe little Italian uinersnt musicisns who, as u\n| known. are exploited by . set OF Idle vagabonds\nwho beat them severely II they DO not bring home\ncertain amount Of money. Arrangements have beeu\nmade TO send the children back their own country,\nand the Journal believes that some nave already been\n| returned 10 their homes.\n\n=== 4_1905-10-18_p1_sn84026853_00237287204_1905101801_0068.json\nThe Abbeville press and banner. [volume] (Abbeville, S.C.) 1869-1924 · 1905-10-18\ncrops: cached\nheadline: PASSING OF THE GYPSY.\n\nRomany Ryes Gradually Disappearin\n\n\nFrom tho Haunts Of Mon.\n---\nRomaay Ryes Gradually disappearing\n\n\nFrom tho Haunts Of Mon.\n\n\nThe gradual disappearance and extinc.\nton Of that picturesque vagabond, the\ngenuine. simon pure gypsy, has caused\nmuch comment, but the reason for the dy-\ning out Of the old race ID somewhat difh-\ncult to find. There are probably more\nrowing, wandering ~vagabonds In the world\ntoday than ever before, but a real Romany\nIL seldom found even in England, where\nhe formerly fourished. In this country\nreal gypsies were never numerous n are\ntoday less 30 than ever before. They are\noccasionally met with, but the bands are\neither very small, or are composed OF horse\ntrading scamps and fortune telling ia\nkirs,\" who have not a drop Of the Romany\nMood in their veins.\n\n\nIn the old days the gypsy impressed him.\nself and his personality on the literature\nand the art of the countries In which he\nroamsd. It was q poor novel that did not\nintroduce a gypsy fortune teller, and q\nDoor play that did not have at least one\nchild stolen by gypsies, while the artists\nfairly reyeled In studies and sketches OF\no maidens. But the gypsies OF today\ndo not indulge in any child stealing pro-\nclivitiss, although they occasionally make\no \"bluf'' at telling fortunes. As for the\nchildren, they usually have enough of their\nown not to bother their heads and risk\ntheir liberty by stealing those OF other\npeople.\n\n\nA band of gypsies which appeared In the\nprovince Of Quebec the other day actually\ntried to sell their own children, arguing\nthat times were so poor that they were no\nlonger able to provide for the little ones\nSo It can readily be seen that tho day OF\nthe gypsy 10 past. II he over had any use\nfulness be has long ago outliyed it.\nTroy Times.\n\n=== 26_1854-09-01_pna_sn85026050_00280775940_1854090101_0347.json\nFremont journal. [volume] (Fremont, Sandusky County [Ohio]) 1853-1866 · 1854-09-01\ncrops: cached\nheadline: postrp.\n\nA Lyric for tIlc Times.\n---\n\"Past two O'Clock aud cloudy sky2\"\nYet the host sat wakeful still, and shook\nHis head, aud winked with a knowing look\n\"Ho! ho\" said he, W ith chucbling tone,\n\"l know the way th thing is done\nTwice five are ten, a nd another V,\nTwo ones, two twos, and ragged three,\nMake twenty-four for my well-flled fob\nIle! its rather good nights job!\nThe fools have uzzled my brandy unG wine,\nMuch good may it do them-Mc cash Is mine!'\nAnd he winked again with knowing look,\nAud from his cigar tn ashes shook\n\"He he the yonk ers are in my net\n| have them safe, aud III 1eeGe them yet.\nThere's Brown-what jolly dog is he!\nAnd he swells the way that | like TO see:\nLet him dash for while at this reckass rate,\nAnd his farm is mine, as sure as fate.\nI've mortgage now on Tompkns' lot\nW hat fool he was TO become a sot!\nBut it's luck TO nfc--in month or so\nshall iorec!ose, and the scamp must go.\nZounds. won't his wife have a taking on\nWhen che learns that his house and lot are gone d?\nHow she will blubber, aud sob, and sigh\nBut business is business, an d what care 10\nAnd Gibson has murdered his child, they say;\nlie as drunk as a 70 OF here, yesterday:\nAnd gave him hint as J wentt TO fill\nHis jug-but the brute ro uh have his will\nAnd the folks blame Re-why, bless their gz.\n\n\nzirdsi\n\n\nIL didn't sell, he w ould go TO lizards!\nl've right to engag in Lawful trade,\nAnd take my chance where there'saeash to be\n\n\nmade.\n\n\nIf men get drunk, and 90 home 10 turn\nTheir wives out door, lis the ir own concern\nBut hate TO have women come TO Inc\nw it'll their tweedle- dun and their tweedle- de\nWith their swollen eyes and haggard looks,\nAnd their speeches harmed from the temper\n\n\nance books;\n\n\nWith their pale, lean children-thc whimper-\n\n\ning Goos\n\n\nWhy ant they get TO the public schoolsl\nLet the huzzies mind their own afftirs,\nFor never ha We interered with theils.-\n| will turn no customer away\nWho is willing 20 buy, and able 10 pay;\nFor business is busin ess-\"he! he! he\"\nAnd he rubbed his han DIS in his chuckling glee--\nMANY lark have caught in my net\nhave them sare--l will fleece them yet!''\n\"lle he; he!' Twas an echoed sound\nAmaz ID the grog seller looked around;\nThis side and th at through the smoke pcered he,\nBut nought but the chairs could the grog-\n\n\nTer see.\n\n\n\"llo! ho! he! he!\" with gutteral note!\nII seemed TO come from an iron throat rise,\nENHu HlS huttS lHtN huooN, anu ms Hui B\"h w\nAnd he opened his mouth, and strained his eyes.\nAnd 10 in corner, dark and dim.\nStood AN unconth form, with an aspect grim\nFrom his grisly head, through his snaliy hair,\nSprouted of hard rough horns, puIr--\nAnd redily his shaggy brow below,\nLike sulphurons flames did his small eyes glow,\nAnd his lips were curled with sinister smile,\nAud the smoke belched forth from his lips the\n\n\nwhile.\n\n\nFolded and buttoned around his breast,\nWas qurint aud silvery cleaning vest,\nAsbes tos il seemed-but we only guess\nUhy he should fancy cold dress\nBreeches he wore OF an amber hue,\nFrom the rear OF which, IA tail peeped through\nHis feet were shaped like bulock's hoof,\nAnd the boots he wore were calorc proof.\nIn his hand he bore-if hand il was,\nWhose fingers were shaped like u vutures s\n\n\nclaws\n\n\nA three-ined fork, and its prongs SO dull\nThrough the ockets were thrust of grinning\n\n\nskull\n\n\nLike a sceptre he waved it to and fro.\nAs he softly chuckled 'ha! ha! no! ho!\"\nAnd all the while were his eyes. that burned\nLike sulph urous flames, OH the grog seller\n\n\nturned!\n\n\nAud how did he feel be meath That look7\nWhy, his jaw fell down, and he shivered aud\n\n\nshook,\n\n\nAnd quvered and quaked in every limb,\nAs if an ague-fil had hold OF him!\nAnd his eyes to the monster grim were glued,\nAud his tongue was as stiff as billet of wood,\nBut the iiend laughed on-\"ho. no! he! he!\"\nAnd switched his tail in his quiet gee.\n\"Why, what do YOU fear my friend7\" he said\nAnd nodded the horns of his grizzly he ad--\nYOU'RE an ally Of mine, and love you welli\nIn a very warm county that men call He'll,\n| hold my courl-aud I'm proud to say,\n| have not faithfuler fend in pay\nThan you, dear sir for work of evil\nMayhap you don't know me,-l'm called the\n\n\nDevil\"\n\n\nLike a galvanized corpse, so pale and wan,\nUpstarted, instanter, that horror-struck man\nAnd he turned up the white of his google eyes,\nWith look half terror and half surprise,\nAnd his tongue W as loosed, but his words were\n\n\nfew\n\n\n\"The devil!-you don't\" eyes, faith! do\"\nInterrupted Old Nick -\"and here's the proofs,\nJust twig my tail, and my horns and hoofs!\nHaving come from warmer climes below,\nTo chat wi tn friend an hour or so,\nAnd the ght being somewhat chill, think\nYou might ask an old fellow to take drink.\nNow let it be strong-the clear, pure stuff\nSweetened with brimstone-a quart is enough,\nStir up the mass in an iron cup,\nAnd heat by fire tIll II bubbles up!'\nAS the devil bade so the grog.seIer did,\nFilled the hagon with gin to the lid\nnd when il boiled and bubbled Ger,\nThe fiery draught TO his gucst he bore\nNick in jifiy the liquor did quaff\nAnd thanked his host with a gutteral laugh\nBlu faint and few were the smiles. ween,\nThat on the grog'seller's face were seen.\nFor mortal fear was on him then,\nAnd he deemed that the ways OF living men\nHe would tread no more-that his hour had\n\n\ncome,\n\n\nAnd his Master, too, TO call him home!\nThought went back TO the darkened past,\nAnd shrieks were heard on the wintery blast,\nAnd gliding before him, pale aud dim,\nWere gibbering fends and spectres grim\nclio! ho!\" said Nick, ''tls a welcome cold\nYou give to a friend so true and old,\nWho has been for years, in your own employ,\nRunning about like an errand boy,\nBut we'll not fall out for clearly see\nYou're rather afraid ctis strangely ofme\nDo you think I've come for youl--never fear\nYou can't be spared for a long while here!\n\"There are hearts 10 break, there are souls 10\n\n\nwin\n\n\nFrom the ways OF peace, TO the paths Of sin\nThere are homes TO be rendered desolaie;\nThere is trusting love 10 be changed 10 hate;\n\n=== 79_1898-12-16_p8_sn84024441_211103176_1898121601_0029.json\nThe evening times. · 1898-12-16\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Dr.. Ledthermdn,\n\nNINE. FLORENCE JUDSOA,\n\nM ME-. THEO,\n---\nM t.. E.THEO,\n\n\nMedium and Card Reader Washington's most\nfamous Clairvoyant and Palmist. Consult her on\nbusiness love, and family affairs; reunites the\nseparated; removes spells; causes speedy mar\nriages, and gives good luck. Open daily. German\nspoken; 25c and Soc 929 ii st. NV. del4-7t\nSPIRITUALISM-MR E. Noble, Medium, 1909\n\n\nfth st. NV; public seance Friday night;\nMRS. COLLINS, DIR. ANDREWS, and other me-\ndums will assist. del5-3t\nWANTED-Lady acquaintance; must be of neat\n\n\nappearance and not too timid. Address\nSTR ANGER this Office. de15-3t =\nDIVORCES AND ALIAfONY obtained no charge\n\n\nto ladies. Address LAWYER H., this Of\nfee. de14-7t\n\n=== 13_1916-06-02_p1_sn86002159_00513680429_1916060201_0415.json\nThe Dolores star. · 1916-06-02\ncrops: cached\nheadline: HOLLAND FULL OF\n\n\nFOREIGN SPIES\n\nInternational Secret Agents Fol-\nlow in Footsteps Of All\n\n\nTravellers.\n\nHAUNT HOTELS AND CAFES\n\nAttempt to Place Spy In American Min\nlisters House Fooled - Marked\nFondness for Things Amert\n\n\ncan Among the People.\n---\nThe Hague.-Holland is full Of spies\nOf all nations. but an overwhelming\nmajority OF them are in the service OF\nthe central empires.\n\n\nIn Rotterdam the German spy Is\neverywhere-in the hotels. concert\nhalls. theaters and cafes. He in turn.\nis spied upon by the paid agents OF the\nopposing powers. and all OF these spies\nunite in looking into the affairs and.\nwhen circumstances permit. the pri-\nvate papers and correspondence Of\nnew arrivals. Not even the American\nminister at The Hague is immune. as\nonly a few weeks ago he foiled a\nscheme which had for its object the\nplacing OF spy OF one of the bellignr-\nent powers on his pay roll as domes-\ntic servant\n\n\nWhen reached Rotterdam went to\nthe ever crowded cafe attached to the\nGrand Hotel Couymans. and while sat\nthere. bewildered by the sound of\ntongues that outbabeled Babel, a dap\nper. clean-shaven man. who might have\npassed for twenty-five. but really was\nabout forty years OF age. took a chair\nnext to mine and opened the ball by\nasking. Are you an Americana\"\n\n\nAlso German Agent.\n\n\nDuring the next ten minutes he men,\ntoned the names of a score Of New\nYork business men who were-I let\nhim tell it-intimate friends. and then\nhe sought to discover why l was in\nHolland. AS reward for frankness\nwhich rivaled his own. and therefore\nrevealed nothing. he warned me to\nguard my papers carefully and never\nto leave them in my hotel, as there\nwere hundreds OF German spies about\nwho would not stop at anything to\ngain information for the fatherland\nAZ he left the cafe London acquaint-\nance dropped into his vacant chair and\nwhispered. DO you know the man\nwith whom you were talking?\"\n\n\nNO.\" replied. but like his brand\nOf cigar\"\n\n\nWELL\" said my Englishman. be\nware OF him; he is a German secret\nagent who poses as an American\" ~\n\n\nl thanked him for his warming ac-\ncepted . cigar from him. too. and was\ntold next morning that he also Is\nGerman agent.\n\n\nThis is no place for another limo\ncent Abroad\" was my thought, and\nquickly left for The Hague. only to\nfind that had stepped from the fry\ning pan into the fire.\n\n\nHere have obtained firstAand\nknowledge of the wonderful German\nintelligence service which has succeed\ned after nearly two years persistent\nunremitting labor. in converting a sec\nton of the population-whose pockets\nhave been hit by the blockade-to\nantf-Brftish. though not pro-German\nviews.\n\n\n\"Gott strafe England\"\n\n\n1t must be said. however, that as\ngeneral rule these propagandists are\nshrewder than the agents who were d.\nrected by Doctor Dernburg. in the\nUnited States. and they have concen-\nrated their energies in the promotion\nOf Colt strafe England\" sentiment\namong the Dutch. II they have not\nsucceeded to the extent hoped for by\nBerlin. 1t is due. not to lack of zeal or\neffort. but to the common sense of the\nDutch government and people, who,\nwhen the German agents charge that\nthe English blockade is forcing the\nworkers Of the country into idleness\nand compelling the inhabitants to eat\nwar bread\" reply that. while England\nis undoubtedly partly to blame for the\ndeplorable state Of affairs. Germany.\nwhich has torpedoed and mined many\nDutch vessels. is from this same Dutch\npoint Of view quite as culpable.\n\n\nThere is only one attractive thing\nabout these spies in Holland-their\nmoney. which they spend freely.\n\n=== 30_1876-12-14_pna_sn84026844_00202193419_1876121401_1197.json\nThe Wheeling daily intelligencer. [volume] (Wheeling, W. Va.) 1865-1903 · 1876-12-14\ncrops: cached\nheadline: Galeqnteliqe11re1.\n---\nhfLy wears oil ago. muscular Anu \"SSH\n~ .S.,ionsJ, oa entering the pulpit, he\nio\"t o6F his COS. aud hung 1t on . nail\nSsbioJ him, then opened his collar and\nwr;,Ibands, and wiped the perspiration\ni,oa, bis face, neck and hand